{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All inputs are in place. Note: the worklist itself contains the model family name \"Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8\" in OVERSEER-LAUNCH.md — this is fine, it's a handoff document, and the forbidden-name rule applies only to spec/worklist byte sequences. My scripts should use the artifact name flashnext-fp8 and the served name flashnext, and must never use the family name. Also, test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene only scans the worklist byte sequences.\n\nWait — an important detail: `test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene` scans the raw worklist byte sequences for banned tokens. My files are not the worklist. But note: does the worklist goal byte sequence contain \"qwen\"? The worklist says \"full naming lives in README.md and the evidence\" — no problem.\n\nNow, about the repo-invariants test `test_scripts_are_syntax_clean` — this runs bash -n against all of host/**/*.sh, including host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh (existing). My new files must pass bash -n.\n\nAlso note: run-smoke.sh's model registry check references \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" — hmm, that's in scripts/run-smoke.sh, which is not a worklist. Fine.\n\nNow let me plan the deliverables:\n\n1. **host/fn-env.sh** — the canonical env file, sourced from the ray head, ray worker, and vllm serve. What's needed:\n   - `export PYTHONHASHSEED=0`\n   - `export PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85`\n   - `export HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1`\n   - Inductor + triton cache dirs under the state directory, never under tmpfs. Something like FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"; TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor; TRITON_CACHE_DIR=$FN_STATE_DIR/triton. mkdir -p.\n   - NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME computed from `ip -br addr`: only thunderbolt rails with a routable /30 IP (neither link-local 169.254.x nor absent). Rail 0 thunderbolt0 10.99.0.x is guaranteed; thunderbolt1 must not be listed unless it has a peer IP. At write time compute and log which rails were chosen.\n   \n   \"At write time compute\" — hmm, fn-env.sh is sourced at bringup. The phrasing \"the env file computes this from `ip -br addr` at write time and logs which rails it chose rather than hardcoding both\" — I'll interpret it this way: at env load time (bringup, i.e. the moment just before the file's content is written to RCCL — at ray start / serve start), the file computes the rail list. \"Write time\" = the moment the env var is written/exported (at source time). Grepping for 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' and 'thunderbolt0' in fn-env.sh must succeed — thunderbolt0 must appear in the file (as the guaranteed rail / candidate). So fn-env.sh has a function that walks candidate rails (thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1), checks that `ip -br addr show dev $rail` has a global IPv4 address that is not 169.254, and joins them with comma. Log chosen rails to stderr/journal. If zero rails chosen → hard fail (loud refusal). thunderbolt0 is guaranteed — but still computed; can be asserted as a fallback.\n\n   - `export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` unconditional, with a loud comment referring to host/rdma/ab-protocol.md.\n   - `export VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_`\n   - `export VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n   - Loud comment forbidding exports of engine defaults that are read via the is-set probe (F.9): VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER*.\n   - FN_-prefixed variables for ray propagation. But note: VLLM_* variables also need to propagate to the ray worker... Actually, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_ means ray will propagate FN_* variables to workers. But vLLM's own ray env copy list includes by default a set of prefixes (VLLM, RAY, etc.). Does the ds4 donor use DS4_ prefix because vLLM doesn't propagate custom prefixes? Actually, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY is precisely the mechanism to add extra prefixes. vLLM's defaults already include \"VLLM\", \"RAY\", \"CUDA\", \"ROCM\", \"NCCL\", \"PYTORCH\", etc. So the VLLM_* and NCCL_* exports propagate by default, and FN_* needs the extra prefix. Good — so the FN_ prefix propagation is a correctness requirement.\n   \n   Also from ds4 donor: worker python pool cap: RAY_NUM_CPUS → but this is passed at ray start (--num-cpus). The ds4 env exports `RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}`. Let's do the same: export RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} with comment, used by fn-cluster-up.\n   \n   Maybe also TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC etc. The brief doesn't require them; but \"compose the discipline\" — a modest NCCL socket timeout block is reasonable: TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS? Keep focused; add a few with justification (2400 sec heartbeat for cold kernel cache). Hmm, caution: don't over-engineer. What the brief enumerates is required; additions must be within the write boundary and must not violate F.9. Since model loading takes tens of minutes on cold kernel cache, I'll include a small NCCL timeout block from the donor (TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2400, TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=0, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=2400000).\n\n   Also RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1? On Strix Halo with 1 GPU per node, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0. ray status needs to report 2.0 GPU — 1 GPU per node. Should I export HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0? Donor had it. Hmm, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 with a single device is harmless. But might interfere with KFD device detection... The donor pinned it for multi-GPU selection. On this fleet each node has exactly one GPU (Strix Halo desktop). I'll skip HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES and instead keep the RAY block minimal: RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1 (donor doctrine for ray + rocm visible devices interaction), RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=0 (donor: avoids ray OOM killer killing the worker; on a 128GB UMA box vLLM legitimately uses most of memory). These are documented in the donor manifest and safe.\n\n   FN_-prefixed variables: the FN_ prefix is used in VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_ — so configuration variables should be FN_-prefixed so they propagate. E.g. FN_MODEL_DIR, FN_SERVED_NAME, FN_PORT, FN_MAX_CTX. Define FN_ configuration defaults in fn-env.sh.\n\n2. **host/fn-cluster-up.sh** — full bringup:\n   - Load fn-env.sh.\n   - Worker over ssh on the wire: WORKER_SSH=\"worker\" or fleet identity 10.99.9.2? Steering says worker-side actions use the ethernet wire (10.99.9.2), never ride the 10.99.0.x rails. The dotfiles SSH nickname exists; per §2.1 the fleet IP is 10.99.9.x on lo — stable identity. So FN_WORKER=${FN_WORKER:-10.99.9.2}.\n   - Reap first, gate later: reap stale serve processes on both nodes (bracket grep trick), SIGTERM→SIGKILL, hard-gate on zero residue.\n   - podman on both nodes: start container (name fn-pair? image flashnext:dev) — \"podman on both nodes (worker over ssh on the wire), distributed head on coordinator, worker join\". So: on both nodes `podman run` a long-lived container with /dev/kfd /dev/dri, --ipc=host, network=host, model directory mount, results mount, cache mounts. Name: flashnext-pair (or follow donor: same name on both boxes, e.g. \"flashnext\").\n   - ray start --head on coordinator inside container; ray start --address=... on worker inside container over ssh. Note: ray head address must be reachable from worker over... Ray control traffic rides on what? NCCL sockets ride on rails; ray GCS connects over... The distributed head listens on node IP. Since the container uses network=host, ray head binds to coordinator IP. Worker should join using coordinator's address — over which interface? The tensor rails carry tensors only; wire carries control (steering 5). So ray address should be the coordinator's wire address 10.99.9.1? Or 10.99.1.1 (eth-fleet /30)? Fleet IPs 10.99.9.x are on lo and are the stable identity. Hmm: \"worker-side actions use the ethernet wire (10.99.9.2)\" — so ssh to 10.99.9.2. Ray join: worker must dial coordinator. Use fleet IP: FN_HEAD_IP default 10.99.9.1, worker dials $FN_HEAD_IP:6379. That keeps control on wire/lo identity. NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME on the rails handles the tensor plane. Good.\n   - Worker python pool cap small: ray start --num-cpus=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} on both (head + worker) with --include-dashboard=false on head only (donor: ray panics if passed to worker).\n   - Hard 2-GPU gate before serve: poll `ray status` for \"GPU: 2.0\" (or parse resources), with timeout, abort loudly.\n   - Then serve process: tensor-parallel 2, --enforce-eager, model /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, served name flashnext, --distributed-executor-backend ray. P10 ruling: \"first light runs in that sanctioned mode\" — piecewise capture with mmap op as a split boundary — but the task brief says \"serve process at tensor-parallel size 2 with eager execution\". The claim 4.2 says \"first light eager on both rails\". Serve in eager (--enforce-eager) as the task says; P10's piecewise mode is for the table path's guard... Actually the task goal explicitly says \"eager execution\" and acceptance only checks the tooling. Going with --enforce-eager.\n   - Serve launched under systemd-run --user transient unit? Donor used systemd-run --user --unit. For flashnext-pair.service (oneshot, RemainAfterExit, ExecStart=fn-cluster-up.sh), if cluster-up execs the serve process directly, then the oneshot unit stays \"activating\" until serve returns... Donor pattern: systemd-run --user transient unit supervises podman exec wrapper, main unit returns success. But RemainAfterExit=yes + Type=oneshot means ExecStart must return. Model load takes tens of minutes — TimeoutStartSec=45min like donor, and serve runs in background via systemd-run transient unit (unit name flashnext-serve-manual), then cluster-up polls API readiness (donor: 105×20s ≈ 35 min) and verifies. This matches \"failure cannot leave ranks stranded\" (ExecStopPost teardown) and the donor's structure.\n   - Also gate on API health before declaring success (wait-for-reality pattern §5.1 library-reachable: poll /v1/models or /health via curl on localhost port).\n   - FN_PORT default 8427? Donor used 1234. Pick FN_API_PORT=${FN_API_PORT:-8000}? vllm default is 8000. Let's go with FN_PORT default 8000. Hmm, llama-swap might occupy something. Use 8000, overridable.\n\n3. **host/fn-cluster-down.sh** — idempotent teardown on both nodes: tolerate broken config, reap serve (SIGTERM→SIGKILL), ray stop --force local + ssh, stop/remove podman containers both nodes, exit 0 unconditionally.\n\n4. **host/fn-preflight.sh** —\n   - byte diff of FN_ env across both ranks: compute env on both nodes (source fn-env.sh inside container? or on host?) and diff sorted `env | grep '^FN_'`... but fn-env.sh only exports vars when sourced. Byte-diff the FN_ env across both ranks — means compare env that each rank sees. Simplest robust approach: on each node run a bash that sources fn-env.sh and prints `env | grep '^FN_' | sort`, then diff. But env contains computed values like NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME — those aren't FN_ prefixed. The spec says \"byte diff of FN_ env across both ranks\" — i.e. the FN_ variables. But the critical divergence risk is the full doctrine env (NCCL etc.). The ds4 donor's concern: \"without it, the two TP ranks diverge\". Let's byte-diff the full canonical env block: source fn-env.sh with a clean baseline and dump all exported vars (`bash -c 'set -a; source fn-env.sh; env' | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|VLLM_|RAY_|TORCH|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_)' | sort`), compare. That's closer to intent (rank env agreement). Write both dumps to temp files, diff, record in receipt data. Hmm, preflight writes its own receipt? The brief lists receipts tp2/residency/fidelity/context for run-tp2. Preflight result can be part of the tp2 receipt data (rails_chosen, preflight pass). Have fn-preflight.sh exit non-zero on mismatch, print a summary, and optionally write results/receipts/preflight.json? receipts-verify checks every *.json under results/receipts — the \"preflight\" step has no bounds → any well-formed receipt is fine. Write preflight.json with status pass/fail; on failure run-tp2 stops. Actually if preflight fails, don't write a fail receipt (which would fail receipts-verify later)? The ladder wants failures to be visible... receipts-verify counts status:fail as a violation. For an overnight runner, a failed step writing a fail receipt is the \"typed blocker\" story, but it makes receipts-verify red. Better: on failure preflight exits non-zero without writing a receipt; run-tp2.sh reports and stops. Hmm, but F.12: \"never record an overnight step skipped without a typed blocker\" — that's about recording a step skipped. Simplest honest behavior: preflight always writes a receipt (pass or fail), and exits non-zero on failure. If it fails, the night produces a failing receipt = graded failure, typed by the data. But then receipts-verify exits 2 even if everything else is fixed... Acceptance criteria only require syntax + doctrine + tests pass, and run-tp2 is not executed at gate time. Writing a fail receipt on actual failure is honest and the ladder grades it — consistent with \"receipts are graded, not told\". Going with that.\n   - Latency-hold device reads at both ends: on both nodes read `od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` (needs root; try sudo -n) — expected value 0 means held. Also `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster`. Record both; if not held (value != 0) warn/fail. U.2 says latency hold must be armed; the tp2 receipt records held round-trips within budget at both ends. Preflight records device reads, round-trip probe measures RTT vs 200µs budget.\n   - Per-rail link speed record: read /sys/class/net/thunderboltN/speed (Mb/s) on both nodes.\n   - Round-trip probe on both rails: ping peer over each rail that has IP. Rail 0 peer is 10.99.0.2/10.99.0.1; rail 1 thunderbolt1 has no IP today, so probe is \"skipped — no peer IP\" (recorded). Round-trip via wire too? Task says \"round-trip probe on both rails\" — rails only. Rail with routable IP: ping -c N -W ... parse avg RTT µs; assert within budget 200µs? U.2 says tp2 receipt records held round-trips within budget at both ends — \"both ends\" means both nodes' latency holds, read at both ends. RTT budget: latencyBudgetMicros=200. Record rtt_us and budget; only fail on rail 0's RTT if above budget? Measured band is 96–112 µs, flagged as not blocking. Set threshold to 200 µs per lowlat doctrine. Rail 1 without peer IP: recorded as unprobed with reason.\n\n5. **host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service** — oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, ExecStart=%h/.../host/fn-cluster-up.sh? The unit is a repo artifact; per donor the installed path is %h/ds4-cluster-restart.sh. For flashnext: ExecStart=%h/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh? Donor assumed the scripts lived at $HOME. Our repo is at /home/tom/mecattaf/flashnext. Use a repo-root-relative %h path? systemd specifiers: %h = user home. The donor unit used %h/ds4-cluster-restart.sh (scripts at home). On flashnext the repo is at ~/mecattaf/flashnext. Should I write ExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh? Hmm, that hardcodes the checkout location. Alternative: document that the unit is linked/copied with adjustment. The test asserts only ExecStopPost and no WantedBy. Acceptance greps 'ExecStopPost' and absence of 'WantedBy'. Follow donor: ExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh, ExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh, ExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh, with a comment explaining why StopPost (ExecStart failures don't run ExecStop). No [Install] section, with comment. Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, TimeoutStartSec=45min, TimeoutStopSec=5min. Wait — user unit or system unit? Donor's was user? `systemd.services.ds4-vllm.service` and %h... %h works in user units (user home). Actually in system units %h is /root. Donor's ds4-vllm.service was presumably a system unit running as the user via User=? The manifest doesn't say; ssh/podman as user tom suggests user-level. I'll write it to work with `systemctl --user link`, and document that. Use %h specifier.\n\n6. **scripts/run-tp2.sh** — first-light runner:\n   - preflight (host/fn-preflight.sh) → abort on failure.\n   - up (host/fn-cluster-up.sh) → wait for serve ready.\n   - Two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared: fixed prompt, temperature 0 (greedy), max_tokens=300, run twice against /v1/completions with served model name flashnext; byte-compare the completion text; record in tp2.json with byte_identical_repeat=true (bound in receipts-verify _tp2!), plus per-rail link speeds (from preflight), rails chosen, latency held at both ends (U.2), and preflight env diff pass.\n   - Residency after 50 warmed decode tokens: issue a request that generates ≥50 tokens (after a warmup request), then read per-rank GTT from sysfs + process RSS + table page-cache residency. GTT from sysfs: amdgpu GTT usage... sysfs per rank: each rank is a process on each node. GTT from sysfs — hmm, per-node /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used? Strix Halo is an APU; patches mention VRAM reporting / unified memory. \"Per-rank GTT from sysfs\" — probably read /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used? The claim says GTT. amdgpu sysfs has mem_info_gtt_used? Actually amdgpu doesn't expose GTT directly... It does: `mem_info_gtt_total`/`mem_info_gtt_used` exists on newer kernels? There are mem_info_vram_used/total, mem_info_vis_vram, mem_info_gtt_total/used (added around 6.x?). On Strix Halo with amdgpu... fork patches 0004/0011 mention VRAM reporting on unified memory. Implement: for each node, read /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used if present, else compute from kfd? Keep it generic: try mem_info_gtt_used; record raw number; convert to GiB. Process RSS: sum RSS of vllm serve + ray workers? Per-rank = per-node serve process tree. Walk /proc via pgrep or ps for RSS total. Table page-cache residency: fincore of model files? Table = engram table files in the model dir (which file? ple/engram table...). Without knowing exact file name... The checkpoint directory /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8 contains the table as some file. Page-cache residency = count cached pages of the table file(s) × page size. Use `mincore` via python (fn can run python3 on host, or inside container). Since this is a scripted runner to be executed overnight with real hardware, I need to be practical: implement best-effort measurement:\n     - GTT per rank from sysfs (mem_info_gtt_used, fallback 0 with flag)\n     - Per-rank RSS (ps)\n     - Table page-cache residency: linux `fincore` util (from util-linux) against files matching the table pattern (e.g. *ple* / *engram* / *.table*?), or via cgroup? Simplest: fincore every file in model dir and sum pages of files that look like the table? But which file is the table? The ds4 story: 51.2B-param hash-lookup embedding... In ds4-vllm it was a big mmap file. Table files — in Qwen3.8-Flash-Next... no name available. The container rootfs has fn_offload_batch.py etc. Hmm. For the mmap path: \"table bytes GPU-resident is zero\" is the key bound: data.table_gpu_resident_bytes == 0. How to measure? If the mmap path is engaged, no table bytes GPU-resident — assert from absence of table allocation on GPU: read GTT/VRAM usage and compare to expected? Practical approach: compute table_gpu_resident_bytes as 0 if the mmap guard is engaged — but that's telling, not measuring.\n     \n     Let me think about what's measurable: the engine's mmap path page-cache-faults the table from disk. GPU residency of table bytes would show up in... no direct probe. Honest proxy measurement: page-cache residency of table file (pages cached × 4096) shows table is served from page cache; GPU-resident table bytes: assert 0 by fincore + accounting of weights loaded to GPU: total model bytes minus table bytes ≈ expected GPU allocation. Too speculative.\n     \n     Practical script design: python helper embedded in run-tp2.sh measures per node:\n       - gtt_bytes: /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used (or gttd), fallback None → record null and don't fail on GTT bound? receipts-verify bound: gtt_gib_per_rank per rank > 80 fails; if key missing, loop over {} — passes. But honesty: record what we can.\n       - rss: sum RSS of serve process tree.\n       - table page-cache residency: fincore against files matching the table glob (FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*.ple *.engram *table*\"?) — hmm. Actually let me reconsider: fork patches: 0009 \"Model-Relocate-ple_emmap_to_the_platform-neutral-comm...\" and 0010 \"ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t...\". PLE = the table. Let me look at those patches to find table file naming and how residency can be probed. Also fn_offload_batch.py / fn_expert_union.py / fn_synctrace.py — the instruments — might reveal hooks.\n\n   Let me read the patches and instruments before writing residency measurement.\n\n   - Fidelity capture: fixed-prompt loss and frontier logits under results/ — need engine support for loss/logprobs: /v1/completions with logprobs, and \"loss\" requires a scoring endpoint (vllm's score?). Frontier logits = logits of final token? Capture: for a fixed prompt set, request completion with logprobs=5, save JSON to results/fidelity-*.json, record sha256 fingerprint. Fidelity receipt data: file list + hashes.\n   - 262144-context probe: build a long prompt (~262144 tokens?) — that's enormous; a probe that sends a prompt and requires --max-model-len >= 262144. Serve must set --max-model-len 262144 (or bigger). Donor used max ctx 524288. FN_MAX_CTX default 262144? The context receipt records decode within 10% of short-context figure: measure decode tok/s on short request and long-context request, ratio ≥ 0.9. Probe: fill prompt to ~262144 tokens via repeated fixed text, then decode a small number of tokens, measure decode rate; compare with short-context decode rate.\n   - Receipts: tp2.json, residency.json, fidelity.json, context.json with schema step/status/ts/data.\n\n7. **tests/test_host_tooling.py** — assert that fn-env.sh contains doctrine lines (PYTHONHASHSEED=0, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF line, HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1, NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME computed not hardcoded — assert absence of `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1` hardcode? Task says \"assert that env file carries doctrine lines and unit files carry ExecStopPost and no WantedBy install\"). Additional: bash -n against every host/*.sh and scripts/run-tp2.sh (duplication with repo-invariants, fine); assert fn-env.sh forbids probe-default exports (no VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM export, etc.); assert cluster-up contains reap-before-gate ordering? Keep to spec: doctrine lines in env + ExecStopPost present + no WantedBy + no [Install].\n\nNow, about the computed NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME: fn-env.sh computes at source time. But note: it should be computed per node — each node sources fn-env.sh locally on that node (ray worker sources on worker). Both nodes currently have only thunderbolt0 with IP, so both compute thunderbolt0. Log the choice: echo to stderr.\n\nAlso, fn-env.sh must be idempotent and overridable via FN_ variables (donor uses ${DS4_X:-default} pattern).\n\nLet me now check patches 0009/0010 and instruments for table naming and residency probes.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me examine the PLE/mmap patches and the instruments to understand table naming and residency probes:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dc9a87745359490f8bcff141","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && head -80 patches/0009-Model-Relocate-ple_emmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch 2>/dev/null || ls patches/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_39d074d483544e60b013a530","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && grep -n \"ple\\|PLE\\|engram\\|table\" patches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3743,"output":6412,"cacheRead":38016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6183,"totalTokens":48171,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988735818,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a3910c46-49e2-4073-a568-c23974e4d993","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
