omp unconfined, ipc host, network host, --pull=never? Use defaults.\n\nEnv injection into container: pass required vars via -e, or via `--env-file` generated from fn-env.sh's exports. Simplest: at container startup source fn-env.sh and `podman run --env-file <(env | grep ...)` — but the ray worker reads env at ray start (propagated from head via VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY + default prefixes). NCCL/VLLM/PYTORCH vars must exist in the serve process's environment. Also start ray inside the container with fn-env.sh sourced: `podman exec $C bash -lc 'source /host/host/fn-env.sh && ray start ...'`. Mount the repo's host/ directory? Mount the whole repo read-only into the container at /opt/flashnext? That couples the container to the host checkout. But worker also needs scripts — worker's repo copy? Donor ran worker via ssh podman exec without a repo, using only env vars passed at ray start.\n\nA cleaner donor-derived approach: pass env explicitly at ray start and serve launch (not relying on files inside the container):\n- Generate env-file on coordinator: bash -c 'source host/fn-env.sh; env' | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|VLLM_|TORCH|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_|TRITON_)' > /tmp/fn-env.list (tmpfs is fine for transport. Cache directory must not live on tmpfs—the env-file transport is not a cache).\n- podman run --env-file that-file -d ...\n- podman exec inside: env is already in the container's base env. ray start reads it. Serve picks it up. This is simple and correct: doctrine env baked into container env at `run`. But NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is computed on coordinator at file generation time. Worker needs its own computed value... the rail is symmetric on both nodes (rail 0 has IP on both). Still, safer to generate an env file on the worker node (ssh worker 'bash -lc \"...compute...\"' > list) and run the container with --env-file there. fn-env.sh is needed on the worker—via ssh stdin: `ssh worker 'cat > /tmp/fn-env.sh' < host/fn-env.sh` and then ssh worker bash /tmp/fn-env.sh ... env. Good: the script ships env to the wire. That satisfies \"env doctrine computed per node\".\n\nRay head: podman exec -d coordinator-container ray start --head --port 6379 --num-cpus=$RAY_NUM_CPUS --include-dashboard=false. Worker: podman exec worker-container ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:6379 --num-cpus=$RAY_NUM_CPUS. Wait—ray's address: worker connects to coordinator's ray GCS. Which IP does ray head advertise/bind? `ray start --head` binds to the node's IP (auto-detected, probably the default route—the wire). Worker dials $FN_HEAD_IP (fleet lo 10.99.9.1). Route worker → 10.99.9.1 goes via the wire (eth), because the fleet IP is reachable via the ethernet /30 route? §2.1: 10.99.9.x/32 on lo is a stable identity. 10.99.1.0/30 eth-fleet is always-on. Routing between fleet IPs presumably goes via eth-fleet (#240 made eth the control plane). Good—ray control goes on the wire, and the rail stays tensor-only via NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME pin.\n\n2-GPU gate: ray status on the head container: poll `podman exec $C ray status` for \"GPU\" total 2.0, timeout ~120s, otherwise abort loudly (dump ray status).\n\nServe: on coordinator, transient via systemd-run? Simpler and more robust: `podman exec -d` to run vllm serve, writing the log to a mounted state dir. But then the unit's teardown relies on fn-cluster-down's reap. Donor used a systemd-run user unit to monitor the podman exec wrapper, making it easy to stop + track via journal. For our service unit: flashnext-pair.service ExecStart=fn-cluster-up.sh returns after serve becomes ready. Serve itself runs detached (podman exec -d). RemainAfterExit keeps the unit active. ExecStop/StopPost = fn-cluster-down.sh reaps everything. Good—\"failure cannot strand ranks\": if cluster-up fails midway, ExecStopPost runs cluster-down.\n\nserve flags: vllm serve /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8 --served-model-name flashnext --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --enforce-eager --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX --max-num-batched-tokens 512? Donor pinned kv-cache bytes. For flashnext, keep minimal-but-doctrinal: --tensor-parallel-size 2, --distributed-executor-backend ray, --enforce-eager, --max-model-len ${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}. Donor warns \"no comments inside line-continuation commands\"—carry that warning over. Add --gpu-memory-utilization? Donor said to pin kv bytes instead. On unified-memory Strix Halo, default gpu-mem-util 0.9 may grab too much. Donor used 0.83. Add --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}. No kv pin (no validated number on this workload), with a comment that the pin overrides util once validated.\n\nAPI readiness poll: curl /v1/models until flashnext appears, up to FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT (default 2700s = 45min), 20s interval.\n\nThen run-tp2.sh continues: completion.\n\nrun-tp2.sh flow:\n1. bash host/fn-preflight.sh (writes results/receipts/preflight.json. Aborts if non-zero).\n2. bash host/fn-cluster-up.sh (abort on failure).\n3. Two greedy completions: POST /v1/completions {\"model\":\"flashnext\",\"prompt\":FIXED,\"max_tokens\":300,\"temperature\":0}. Compare .choices[0].text bytes. byte_identical_repeat.\n4. Write tp2.json: data {byte_identical_repeat, rails: preflight's rail speed, rails_chosen, rtt_us per rail, latency_hold: {coordinator:0, worker:0}, budget_us:200}.\n5. Warmed decode: warmup request (12 tokens), then a request with ≥50 decode tokens (max_tokens=64). Then residency snapshot: python3 helper does locally + ssh-worker: GTT from sysfs, process RSS (pgrep -f \"vllm serve\" → ps o rss), table page-cache residency via fincore over glob-matched files, ple_mmap engagement from serve log grep → table_gpu_resident_bytes. Write residency.json {gtt_gib_per_rank:{coordinator:x,worker:y}, rss_gib_per_rank, table_page_cache_gib, table_gpu_resident_bytes:0, read_after_warmed_decode:true}.\n6. Fidelity: a few fixed prompts. Capture completions with logprobs (frontier logits = logprobs of final generated token), plus loss via the score endpoint? Does vLLM's OpenAI server have /v1/score? For loss: use completions with prompt and echo + logprobs=1 and compute NLL from returned logprobs—feasible: request with max_tokens=1, logprobs=5, echo=true? Simpler: capture token logprobs on a fixed continuation and compute average NLL = loss. Save JSON files results/fidelity/prompt-N.json, hash them, and receipt fidelity.json {files:[...], hashes, prompt count}.\n7. Context probe: build a long prompt of ~262144 tokens (repeat a fixed sentence. Estimate token count ≈ chars/4. Target 262144*~4 chars ≈ 1MB of text—fine). Measure short-context decode speed and long-context decode speed. ratio = long_decode_tps / short_decode_tps. Write context.json {target_context:262144, short_decode_tps, long_decode_tps, decode_ratio_vs_short_context: ratio}. Receipts-verify constraint: ratio < 0.9 → fail. If probe errors (e.g., prompt too long for server), record fail status honestly.\n\nAll receipts: {\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"}.\n\nClient: python3 with urllib only. Write helper python inline in run-tp2.sh, taking JSON paths etc. The script may run overnight without network, so keep it stdlib-only.\n\nNow tests/test_host_tooling.py:\n\n```python\nclass HostTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    test_fn_env_doctrine_lines: read host/fn-env.sh. assertIn PYTHONHASHSEED=0, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85, HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME, thunderbolt0. Assert computed not hardcoded: assertNotIn \"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1\", presence of `ip -br addr` too.\n    test_fn_env_no_probe_default_exports: F.9—assert no lines exporting VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER.\n    test_unit_stoppost_no_install: flashnext-pair.service contains ExecStopPost, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes. Does not contain WantedBy or [Install].\n    test_host_scripts_syntax_clean: bash -n over host/*.sh + scripts/run-tp2.sh.\n    test_preflight_and_runner_exist + exec bit.\n```\n\nNote: acceptance also requires the glob `host/*.sh` to cover fn-env.sh, fn-cluster-up.sh, fn-cluster-down.sh, fn-preflight.sh. host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh already exists (syntax clean? repo-invariants tests probably already pass at baseRev, so yes).\n\nWrite boundaries: host/, scripts/run-tp2.sh, tests/ only. All files fit.\n\nCommit message style: let me check git log message style: \"instruments: ...\", \"container-recipe: ...\", \"receipts: weights staged...\". So prefix with task ID: \"host-tooling: Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\". Commit on branch tally-work/flashnext-57f87640bc5b/host-tooling. Don't push.\n\nAnother check: fn-env.sh's NCCL rail computation must mention thunderbolt0 (grep requirement)—candidate list includes thunderbolt0, thunderbolt1. Log which one was chosen.\n\nEnv directory: FN_STATE_DIR default: /var/lib/flashnext? A user-writable directory needed for the cache under the state directory. Use ${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}. Inside the container, HOME is root's home (podman default runs as root in image?). Pass via env file. Cache directory must be on-disk (not tmpfs)—$HOME/.local/state is on-disk on both nodes. Inside the container mount the host directory: -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\". mkdir -p on host first.\n\nAlso HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 default via ${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}? The brief says HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1. Keep plain export with override default.\n\nWorker pool cap: export RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} in fn-env.sh, consumed by cluster-up (ray start --num-cpus on both nodes).\n\nNow—does the repo have a container/rootfs/.keep etc? Not relevant.\n\nCheck `tests/test_instruments.py` for the test style (and to confirm tests/ is a package: unittest tests.test_host_tooling — need tests/__init__.py? python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling requires tests to be a package, or at least importable. If tests/ has no __init__.py, python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling — unittest treats \"tests.test_host_tooling\" as a module path. Import via file-path discovery needs the package. Actually `python -m unittest tests.test_foo` works without __init__.py in py3? unittest imports module tests.test_foo—importlib needs tests to be importable as a namespace package—in Python 3, namespace packages work without __init__.py, so with cwd=repo root `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling` works. The repo-tests gate uses discover -s tests, which works either way. Let me check whether __init__.py exists.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me check the tests dir structure and the git log style, then look at the instruments test for conventions:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e70603e5660546bfad78050c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && ls -la tests/ && head -30 tests/test_instruments.py && git log --format='%s' -8"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1865,"output":4425,"cacheRead":49152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4314,"totalTokens":55442,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988898114,"responseId":"chatcmpl-216e4819-7d48-4f91-a7d1-5809e6e9c407","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
