{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright. No __init__.py; namespace packages will work. Commit style: \"taskid: Title\".\n\nLet me double-check a few remaining details too:\n\n1. How does the worker get the repo? fn-env.sh gets shipped via ssh stdin — no problem.\n2. `fn-cluster-down.sh` should also stop the systemd-run unit if donor was using one — here serve runs via podman exec -d, so teardown is: kill the serve process on both nodes, ray stop, podman rm -f the containers. Idempotent, always exit 0.\n\n3. For fn-preflight's \"byte-level diff of FN_ env vars on both ranks\": compute per-node env dumps by sourcing fn-env.sh via ssh. But fn-env.sh sources from a repo path — on the worker the repo might not exist at the same path. Ship the file via stdin to both nodes: `bash -s <<EOF`? Cleaner: `ssh worker 'cat > \"$t/fn-env.sh\"' < host/fn-env.sh`. Wire (ssh) delivers it. Then dump: `bash -c 'set -a; source $t/fn-env.sh; env'` filtered on FN_ and doctrine keys, sorted → byte diff. NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is computed identically on both nodes (both have the same rails), so the diff should match. PYTHONHASHSEED=0 is static. Good.\n\nBut wait: fn-env.sh logs the chosen rails on source (to stderr) — capture stdout only for the diff. Log to stderr so the diff stays clean.\n\n4. Latency-hold device read: `sudo -n od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` — requires root access to read /dev/cpu_dma_latency. Use sudo -n and tolerate failure (record \"unreadable\"). Check `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster` too. Expected value is 0 (held).\n\n5. Per-rail link speed: /sys/class/net/thunderbolt0/speed (Mb/s) on both nodes. Record.\n\n6. Round-trip probe: for each rail with a routable IP: ping peer's rail IP. Rail 0 peer: coordinator→10.99.0.2, worker→10.99.0.1. Determining peer depends on hostname. Run preflight on coordinator (driver) and ssh to worker to run the probe there too (\"on both ends\"). Each node pings its peer on each rail. Record avg rtt µs, budget 200.\n\n7. tp2.json needs U.2 \"record round-trips within budget, held, on both ends\": include the held device value and rtt vs. budget.\n\nNext receipts-verify: preflight step name \"preflight\" — no bound checker, fine.\n\nNow writing files. Attention to detail:\n\nfn-env.sh:\n- `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, set -u? It's sourced — avoid set -e in a sourced file? Donor's env files generally don't set -e. Defensive but source-safe: no set -e (caller owns it). Use set -u? If caller hasn't set -u, referencing unset ${FN_X:-} is fine. Skip set entirely, document.\n- Rail computation:\n\n```bash\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    [ -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ] || { echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent; not listed\" >&2; continue; }\n    # routable /30 = a global IPv4 that is not link-local 169.254/16\n    local addr\n    addr=$(ip -br addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | awk '{for(i=3;i<=NF;i++) print $i}' | cut -d/ -f1 \\\n      | grep -Ev '^$|^169\\.254\\.' | head -n1 || true)\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED ($addr routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  ...\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"$(fn_choose_rails)\"\n[ -n \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ] || { echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no routable Thunderbolt rail; refusing\" >&2; return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1; }\n```\n\ngrep 'thunderbolt0' must hit — it's in the candidate list. Good.\n\nNote: `ip -br addr show dev thunderbolt1` line format: \"thunderbolt1  UP  169.254.x.x/16 ...\" — fields 3+. If no address, fields are just name + flags. Also filter fd00:? IPv4 only: match ^[0-9.]+$. Refine: `grep -E '^[0-9.]+$' | grep -Ev '^169\\.254\\.'`.\n\n- return 1 vs. exit 1 on source failure: use `return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1` trick to work both sourced and executed.\n\nfn-env.sh content plan (mirroring donor structure, commented):\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# flashnext pair-service env doctrine (sourced by ray head, ray worker, vllm serve...)\n# ... header explaining what this is\n# DO-NOT-EXPORT probe defaults comment (F.9) — big and loud.\n```\n\nVariables:\n- FN_STATE_DIR, FN_MODEL_DIR=/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, FN_SERVED_NAME=flashnext, FN_PORT=8000? Donor used 1234. Let's default FN_PORT=8000. FN_MAX_CTX=262144 (claim 4.5), FN_WORKER_HOST=10.99.9.2, FN_HEAD_IP=10.99.9.1, FN_CONTAINER=flashnext-pair, FN_IMAGE=flashnext:dev, FN_RAY_PORT=6379.\n- Determinism: PYTHONHASHSEED=0 (with donor rationale comment).\n- PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF.\n- HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 with comment.\n- Cache dirs: TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR/TRITON_CACHE_DIR under $FN_STATE_DIR, mkdir -p, never on tmpfs.\n- NCCL block: computed NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME + NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 unconditional + timeout block + NCCL_DEBUG=WARN? Keep minimal: TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2400, TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=0, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=2400000 (cold kernel cache ~25min rationale).\n- Ray block: VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1, RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=0, RAY_NUM_CPUS=4 default.\n- VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 with mmap-path comment.\n- VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1, HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1? Runtime hub downloads forbidden (F.6) — HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 enforces. VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1. Good doctrine, from dotfiles vllm renderer's offlineModelEnv. Include.\n\nfn-cluster-up.sh plan:\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Bring the flashnext pair service up: containers on both nodes (wire ssh to\n# worker), ray head+join, 2-GPU gate, then TP=2 eager serve. REAP FIRST, GATE AFTER.\nset -euo pipefail\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n\nlog() { echo \"fn-cluster-up: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# Ship fn-env.sh to worker via wire; write per-node env-file for containers\nship_env() { local dest=\"$1\"; ... }\n\nmake_env_file(): source fn-env.sh in a subshell, filter env, write file\n```\n\nEnv-file generation: `( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"; env ) | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_|TORCHINDUCTOR|TRITON_|HF_)' | sort > \"$f\"`. On worker: pipe the script via ssh.\n\nReap (host-side, covers podman exec'd processes):\n\n```\nreap_serve() {  # $1 = \"local\" | ssh-prefix...\n  pattern='[v]llm serve'\n  pids=$(pgrep -f \"$pattern\" || true)\n  [ -n \"$pids\" ] && kill -TERM $pids; sleep 3\n  pids=$(pgrep -f \"$pattern\" || true); [ -n ] && kill -KILL\n  residue=$(pgrep -cf \"$pattern\" || true)\n  [ \"$residue\" -eq 0 ] || { log FATAL; exit 1; }\n}\n```\nWait: pgrep -cf prints count; pgrep returns 1 on no match; `|| true`. Residue check: use `pgrep -f \"$pattern\" | wc -l`.\n\nRemote: `worker 'pids=$(pgrep -f \"[v]llm serve\" || true); ...'` — encapsulate as reap_node with local flag.\n\nContainers:\n```\nstart_container() { # node, envfile\n  podman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  podman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --ulimit memlock=-1 --ulimit memlock=-1 ... \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n    --env-file \"$envfile\" \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity\n}\n```\nWorker: `worker \"podman rm -f ... && podman run ... --env-file $envfile ...\"` — envfile is on worker side (made there via ssh). Model dir staged on both nodes (weights receipt).\n\nRay:\n```\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" --include-dashboard=false\nworker \"podman exec $FN_CONTAINER ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT --num-cpus $RAY_NUM_CPUS\"\n```\n\nTwo-GPU gate:\n```\nfor i in $(seq 1 60); do\n  gpus=$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^\\s*[0-9.]+/([0-9.]+) GPU' ...)\n```\nParse: ray status outputs \" 0/2 GPU\". grep -oE '[0-9.]+/[0-9.]+ +GPU' and take total after '/'. Simpler: `podman exec ... python3 -c \"import ray; ray.init(address='auto'); print(ray.cluster_resources().get('GPU',0))\"` — heavy but deterministic. Donor grepped `ray status` for \"2.0 GPU\". Use grep: `ray status | grep -qE '(^|[^0-9.])2\\.0/?[0-9.]* *GPU'`... hmm, format of ray status resource lines is: ` 0/2 GPU`. So grep -E '^[[:space:]]*[0-9.]+/2(\\.0)?[[:space:]]+GPU'. To be robust: extract with sed then compare via awk:\n```\ntotal=$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null | awk '/ GPU$/ {print $2}' | cut -d/ -f2 | head -n1)\n[ \"${total:-0}\" = \"2\" ] || [ \"${total:-0}\" = \"2.0\" ] && break\n```\nawk on \"0/2 GPU\": $2 = \"0/2\"? Line looks like \" 0/2 GPU\" → $1=\"0/2\", $2=\"GPU\". So awk '$2==\"GPU\" {split($1,a,\"/\"); print a[2]}'. Good.\n\nTimeout 120 iterations × 2s = 4 min; on fail: dump ray status, exit 1.\n\nServe: via podman exec -d with log:\n```\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83} \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log 2>&1\"\n```\nWarning about comments inside backslash continuation (donor's minefield) — do not put comments in, carry the warning over.\n\nWait — vllm serve with positional arg model dir: `vllm serve <model>`. Yes.\n\nReadiness poll: `curl -fsS localhost:$FN_PORT/v1/models | grep -q flashnext` for FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT=2700, step 20s.\n\nPrint summary + exit 0.\n\nfn-cluster-down.sh:\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Idempotent teardown...\nset -uo pipefail   # no -e: must not die mid-teardown\nsource fn-env.sh tolerantly: `source ... 2>/dev/null || true`, then fallbacks: FN_WORKER_HOST=${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2} etc.\nReap serve local+remote, ray stop --force local+remote (inside containers? ray stop needs to run where ray is running: inside container podman exec ray stop --force; but if the container is gone, then host pgrep reap covers it. Order: reap serve (host pgrep, kills processes in container via host pidns — podman shares pidns with host by default, so processes are visible), then podman exec ray stop --force || true on both nodes, then podman rm -f container on both nodes.\nexit 0\n```\nNote: does rootless podman on the host have container processes visible from host /proc? Rootless podman: container processes run inside user namespace but are visible from host /proc (with mapped uids). pgrep -f \"vllm serve\" on the host can see them. Donor relied on exactly this. Good.\n\nfn-preflight.sh:\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Readiness: byte diff of env across both ranks, latency-hold reads on both ends,\n# per-rail link speed, round-trip probe on both rails.\nset -euo pipefail\nsource fn-env.sh\nRECEIPT=${REPO_ROOT}/results/receipts/preflight.json\n```\nSteps:\n1. Per-node env dump: local via bash; remote via ssh. Filter doctrine + FN keys, sort. cmp. Record pass/fail.\n2. Latency hold: local `od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` via sudo -n (may need root); fallback: cat? /dev/cpu_dma_latency is root rw. Try `sudo -n od ...`, on failure → \"unreadable\". Also systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster. Remote same via ssh. \"held\" = value 0.\n3. Link speed: /sys/class/net/$rail/speed on both nodes for thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1 (skip if absent).\n4. RTT probe: per node, per rail with routable IP: peer IP: coordinator rail0 peer 10.99.0.2; worker rail0 peer 10.99.0.1. How to compute? Derive: read own IP a.b.c.1/.2 → peer = flip last octet, keep /30. General: for /30, peer of x.1 is x.2 and vice versa. Implement in awk: last octet 1→2, 2→1, else skip. ping -c 20 -i 0.01 -W 2 peer → parse avg from rtt line, µs = avg*1000. Budget FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=200.\nAggregate status: pass if env diff matches and rail0 rtt on both ends within budget and latency-hold held (value 0) on both ends (fail with reason on unreadable? U.2 requires hold armed — if unreadable, mark status fail? That risks failing the night for a mere sudo config. The manifest says device reads 0 when held; sudo -n should work for tom (fleet sudo is configured). Soft-fail: record as \"unknown\", overall status fail only if env diff or rtt budget violated; hold unknown → warn but pass? Donor's doctrine is strict: preflight is a gate. But a permission issue is not hardware truth. Compromise: if is-active lowlat-cluster is active AND device value is readable → record held:true. If unit is active but device is unreadable → held:\"unreadable\" (don't fail). If unit inactive → held:false → fail. That's principled: the unit's state is authoritative evidence of arming; the device read corroborates.)\n\nWrite preflight.json {\"step\":\"preflight\",\"status\":...,\"ts\":...,\"data\":{...}}. exit 0/1.\n\nrun-tp2.sh — orchestrates everything as designed. Python client via heredoc. Fixed prompt: a constant string, no family name. Use `FN_FIXED_PROMPT` default: \"The fleet boots before the operator arrives.\" Fine.\n\nTwo greedy completions: temperature 0 → greedy. Compare .choices[0].text.\n\nFidelity: 3 fixed prompts; for each: completion with max_tokens=64, logprobs=5, echo=true? /v1/completions supports echo + logprobs. Compute loss = -mean(token logprob of continuation). Frontier logits = final token's top-5 logprobs. Save to results/fidelity/prompt-<i>.json. sha256 of each file → receipt.\n\nContext probe: short: prompt ~32 tokens, max_tokens 64 → decode tps via usage + timing (measure walltime for generation; tps = completion_tokens / (elapsed - prefill)? Can't measure prefill directly; use overall tps as proxy on both arms — the ratio cancels the bias if prefill is small on both... short-prompt long-decode, and long-prompt short-decode: short arm: prompt 32, decode 128. Long arm: prompt ~262144-128, decode 128. Decode-heavy ratio: measure (completion_tokens)/(elapsed_total - prefill_estimate)? Keep simple: measure tokens/sec over decode by streaming? Use stream=true with SSE timing: time from first chunk to last chunk ≈ decode time for both arms. That's the honest decode separation (bench harness task does the proper client; here the ratio probe is best-effort: use streaming, measure inter-chunk decode window). Compute decode_tps = (completion_tokens - 1)/decode_elapsed. Ratio = long/short.\n\n262144-token prompt: build by repeating text. Token count estimate: use a rough 3.5 chars/token for English; target 262144 tokens → ~917k chars. Fine in-memory. Set an FN_CONTEXT_TARGET=262144 constant; note exact tokenization is engine-side; verify server accepted full prompt (usage.prompt_tokens reported) and record the actual prompt_tokens. If the server truncates/refuses → fail receipt.\n\nResidency python (host-side): run on coordinator locally and via ssh on worker:\n```\nssh worker python3 - <<'PY' ... \n```\nCollect JSON per node: {gtt_bytes, gtt_source, rss_bytes, table_pages_cached, table_files_matched}. GTT candidates: /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used; fallback mem_info_vram_used (with source name recorded). RSS: walk /proc, cmdline contains \"vllm serve\" or ray:: — actually rank processes = vllm serve main + ray workers. Sum VmRSS of processes whose cmdline matches \"vllm serve\" or \"ray::\" ... ray worker processes' cmdline is like \"ray::IDLE\"? On this pair, one rank per node: the TP rank on that node = the vllm/ray python worker running the model. Simpler: sum RSS of processes matching 'vllm serve' or 'ray::Worker'? Use pgrep -f 'vllm serve|ray::' — caveat. Use regex 'vllm serve' and 'ray::' to sum RSS. OK.\n\nTable page cache: files under FN_MODEL_DIR matching os.environ.get FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*ple*\" (case-insensitive?) — the PLE table shards. fincore via linux-fincore binary may not exist; use python mincore: open file, mmap, mincore per page chunk — heavy for 185GB file! Use `fincore` if available; else fall back to /proc/meminfo? Alt honest and cheap probe: for matched table files, use `mincore` over the mapped region — mmap a 185GB file and touch pages in 4KB steps is a page-table walk only (mincore doesn't fault). Python mmap over huge file + mincore in 4KB steps = 48M syscalls — too slow. Stride by page checking 1 page per 2MB region? Loses resolution. Realistic: use `fincore` (util-linux) if available, else `pcstat` if available, else record null with reason. This pair's fleet probably has util-linux fincore. Implement fincore-first, graceful null fallback.\n\nWhich files: the table is 51.2B params FP8 ≈ 51GB. Detect: files ≥ 1GiB that are NOT *.safetensors? The shards are safetensors (131 of them) — the table might be in safetensors shards... For the mmap path, ds4 had a dedicated table file (mmap requires raw layout). ple_mmap.validate_shards_for suggests shards. Honestly: FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*ple*,*ngram*,*.table\" — if zero matches → record files_matched 0 and page_cache null; the receipt still records table_gpu_resident_bytes based on serve log probe. Enough; morning operator can set FN_TABLE_GLOB.\n\ntable_gpu_resident_bytes: probe serve log: grep -i 'ple_mmap' $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log for engagement marker (e.g., \"ple_mmap\" + \"engaged\"/\"build_tables\"). Marker not precisely known; accept any line matching 'ple_mmap' AND VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 → 0; else null (receipt will fail loudly → morning operator confirms). Also, log the matched lines into data.\n\nHmm wait — the fork's ple_mmap logs loudly (patch 0010 test says it logs and defers). Accept grep -ci 'ple_mmap' > 0.\n\nresidency.json data:\n```\n{\"gtt_gib_per_rank\": {\"coordinator\": x, \"worker\": y},\n \"gtt_source\": ..., \"rss_gib_per_rank\": {...},\n \"table_page_cache\": {...},\n \"table_gpu_resident_bytes\": 0,\n \"read_after_warmed_decode\": true}\n```\nUpper bound: gtt ≤ 80 GiB per rank.\n\nWarm decode requirement: before reading residency, issue a completion with max_tokens=64 (after warmup run). Set flag true only after that request succeeds.\n\nNow, unit file:\n\n```\n# flashnext pair service — one command up, one command down, no boot-time install.\n[Unit]\nDescription=flashnext TP=2 pair service (coordinator + worker)\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nRemainAfterExit=yes\nTimeoutStartSec=45min\nTimeoutStopSec=5min\n# The model takes tens of minutes to load and claims both GPUs...explicit start only.\nExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh\nExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n# ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails; StopPost does, so a failed\n# bring-up still tears the pair down instead of stranding ray daemons and\n# half-up containers behind a 'failed' wrapper.\nExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n\n# Deliberately NO [Install] section: ...\n```\n\nTest file asserts:\n- env doctrine lines\n- `ip -br addr` in fn-env.sh (computed), no hardcoded `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1`\n- no F.9 probe-default exports\n- unit: ExecStopPost present, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, no WantedBy, no [Install]\n- bash -n on host/*.sh and scripts/run-tp2.sh\n- fn-cluster-up.sh contains reap-before-gate: assert reap function defined before ray start? Textual order check: file.index(\"reap\") < file.index(\"ray start\"). Nice determinism.\n- scripts/run-tp2.sh writes 4 receipts (grep step names tp2/residency/fidelity/context).\n\nNow, also re-verify acceptance command: `for f in host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh; do bash -n \"$f\"; done` — all four new .sh in host/ + run-tp2.sh must parse. And greps. Then `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v` from repo root.\n\nAlso make sure repo-invariants still passes: my scripts must not trigger secret scan (no hf_ tokens etc.), GPL scan is fine.\n\nOne more point: run-smoke.sh has IMAGE default \"flashnext:dev\"; my scripts use FN_IMAGE with same default — consistent.\n\nFN_PORT default: donor used 1234; llama-swap runs on its own port on this fleet; pick 1234 for consistency with donor doctrine? Spec/worklist doesn't pin. FN_PORT default 1234? Hmm, risk of clashing with leftover ds4 stack? ds4 not on this fleet (this is coordinator/worker of the same fleet where ds4-vllm ran? Donor env from ds4 campaign on same hardware? ds4-vllm-manifest is donor material from another estate (hy3?). Actually evidence came from \"AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm\" upstream — different rig. Our fleet: coordinator/worker Strix Halo pair. Port 1234 probably free. Pick FN_PORT=${FN_PORT:-1234}. OK.\n\nAlso let me verify the ssh nicknames: §2.3 dotfiles has ssh nicknames — \"worker\" probably aliased. But steering says worker-side ops go over wire 10.99.9.2. Default FN_WORKER_HOST=10.99.9.2. Good, explicit and correct.\n\nEnv-file filtering at container start: include LD_LIBRARY_PATH? No surgery needed here (no patched ROCr preload — that's donor's custom build). Skip.\n\nAlso `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, `VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1`, `VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1`, `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1`? Tokenizer loading from local dir anyway. Add HF_HUB_OFFLINE + VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS + VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK (F.6 doctrine).\n\nNote grep acceptance: `grep -q 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' host/fn-env.sh` ✓; `grep -q 'thunderbolt0' host/fn-env.sh` ✓ (in candidates + log).\n\nOne subtlety: fn-env.sh computes rails and logs them on source. In env-file generation (`env` dumps), we must ensure logging goes to stderr only — yes.\n\nIn fn-cluster-up, env file written to $FN_STATE_DIR/ (on disk). Worker: create via ssh heredoc. Worker-side container start also needs model volume + state dir on worker: mkdir -p $FN_STATE_DIR on worker via ssh.\n\npodman rootless + /dev/kfd: needs --group-add keep-groups (rootless podman standard for kfd access). run-smoke.sh used plain podman run with --device; follow existing repo style (run-smoke.sh does not add --group-add). Add `--group-add keep-groups` anyway? deviates from smoke script... smoke runs `podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host`. Mirror that exactly, plus --network host, -d, volumes, env-file, ulimit memlock=-1 (RCCL/RDMA-ish; harmless), sleep infinity.\n\nOK, one more thing: 2-GPU gate counts GPU resources ray sees. Container with /dev/kfd → ray sees 1 GPU each → 2.0 total.\n\nEdge: `ray start --head --port`: also need --num-gpus? Ray autodetects via /dev/kfd? Ray's GPU detection uses nvidia-smi / rocm-smi. Donor got \"2.0 GPU\" on a rig where ray autodetects. Container has rocm stack (vendor wheels) → rocm-smi present → detects. Trust it; gate loudly enforces.\n\nServe host binding: --host 0.0.0.0 to be reachable from... Only coordinator-local client (run-tp2 runs on coordinator). 127.0.0.1 suffices; but worker rank connects to head API? No—only ray. Use 0.0.0.0 as donor does (they had external checks). Keep 0.0.0.0.\n\nLet me also design run-tp2.sh receipts writing via python heredocs. Structure:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# First-light runner: preflight -> pair up -> greedy byte-compare -> residency\n# -> fidelity -> 262144-context probe. Writes tp2.json, residency.json,\n# fidelity.json, context.json per the receipts-verify.py schema.\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts\"\nmkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$REPO_ROOT/results/fidelity\"\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nAPI=\"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT\"\n```\n\nSteps with fail-fast: preflight; cluster-up.\n\nPython helper for completions: `fn_api()` heredoc python with urllib, JSON arg via argv, prints response JSON. Write one python block that does everything? Cleaner: multiple smaller heredocs. Long but explicit. Or embed one python program that handles steps 3–7 with subprocess calls to host measurements? The residency host measurement needs ssh + sysfs — do that in a separate heredoc python (host side, python 3 stdlib: subprocess for ssh).\n\nPlan run-tp2.sh sections:\n\nA. preflight + up (bash).\nB. greedy byte-compare → write tp2.json via python heredoc (also embeds preflight's rail info from preflight.json).\n   Actually simpler: tp2.json written from bash: byte_identical is python compare result; rail speed and rtt pulled from preflight.json via python. Make it one python heredoc \"step_tp2\" that reads preflight.json, makes two completions, compares, writes tp2.json.\nC. Warmup + residency: python heredoc \"step_residency\": warmup req (300 tokens once), then 64-token decode; gather per-node metrics (local + ssh); grep serve.log for ple_mmap; write residency.json.\nD. Fidelity python heredoc.\nE. Context probe python heredoc.\n\nFinal summary line. Any step exits non-zero → script fails (ladder sees it). Receipts written with status fail where meaningful (fidelity/context write receipts with status fail internally on error; tp2/residency are fatal exits after receipt writing).\n\nStreaming timing for context probe: urllib streaming SSE parse — read lines, mark t_first at first data chunk with content, t_last at end. Implement carefully.\n\nFixed prompts (no family name):\nP1 \"The pair boots before the operator arrives, and the ledger records what the night proved.\"\n2–3 variants.\n\n300-token greedy completion max_tokens=300.\n\nToken estimates for context probe: build prompt by repeating a paragraph; request with max_tokens=128; read usage.prompt_tokens from response; require prompt_tokens >= 0.95*target, else fail. Target 262144. To avoid over-shooting max-model-len: aim prompt tokens ≈ target - 160. Estimate chars/token ratio 3.6; iterate: build, then just accept actual prompt_tokens (engine counts). If over → server errors out → fail honestly. Slight margin: target_chars = (262144-192)*4 → ~1,047,808 chars ~1MB. OK.\n\nResidency GTT sysfs: card paths: /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_gtt_used — iterate cards, prefer card0; also try /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_vram_used. Convert bytes→GiB.\n\nRSS: sum via os.scandir of /proc/*/cmdline matching 'vllm serve' or 'ray::'.\n\nfincore: `fincore --json`? plain: `fincore <file>` outputs \"RES PAGES ...\" lines. Parse sum of pages? fincore output per file: `RES    PAGES  SIZE SIZE% FILE`. Parse PAGES column (2nd). Use subprocess; skip if fincore not present (shutil.which).\n\nTable glob default: \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*,*table*\" split on comma; fnmatch case-insensitive. Files under FN_MODEL_DIR (one level + nested walk). Cap: only consider files ≥ 100 MiB for page-cache? No — count whatever matched.\n\nNote: fincore on 185GB files — fine, reads statx-like info.\n\nssh remote execution of this python: `ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" python3 - <<'PY' ... PY`. Heredoc over ssh works (stdin).\n\nServe.log location: $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log on coordinator (inside mounted dir = host path same). ple grep there.\n\nAlright, also \"worker python pool kept small\": RAY_NUM_CPUS=4 → ray start --num-cpus on both nodes. Done.\n\nAlso re-verify the acceptance grep `grep -q 'VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_' host/fn-env.sh` — my line: `export VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_` ✓.\n\n`grep -q 'PYTHONHASHSEED=0'` → `export PYTHONHASHSEED=0` ✓.\n`grep -q 'NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1'` ✓.\n\nTest unittest import: tests/test_host_tooling.py with ROOT relative pathlib — same as siblings.\n\nAlso: don't create results/receipts/preflight.json during test runs — tests don't run preflight.\n\nAlright — also verify `bash -n` for each script after writing.\n\nAnother consideration: conflictDomains requires all paths touched to be inside host/, scripts/run-tp2.sh, tests/. results/fidelity/*.json is created by run-tp2.sh at runtime, not by my commit — fine (git commit touches only my files). Not committing receipts.\n\nAlso — does repo-invariants' test_receipts_verify_runs_clean pass with existing receipts? Existing results/receipts has build.json, weights-*.json — they were already passing. I add no receipts. Good.\n\nLet me also read the tail of the CONTINGENCY doc for anything? Not needed. ROUTING.md — the steering says read ROUTING for compute routing doctrine; the brief says read cited spec sections and style references — done. ROUTING might contain host-tooling-specific guidance; let me skim quickly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me quickly skim `handoff/ROUTING.md` for any host-tooling-specific doctrine, and check dotfiles §2 addressing details:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c5dade61147342ddbd2589f0","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && grep -n -A6 \"host-tooling\" handoff/ROUTING.md | head -40"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f72a694692df4f989c3256f8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && sed -n '175,260p' specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5350,"output":8335,"cacheRead":50688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8135,"totalTokens":64373,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988999036,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ded87e86-00eb-4035-ba3e-7d5cfa19d73f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
