{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Everything confirmed:\n- From the coordinator, ssh to the worker is done via the fleet identity `10.99.9.2` (the wire, eth metric 20).\n- Coordinator 10.99.9.1, worker 10.99.9.2. Rail 0: coordinator 10.99.0.1, worker 10.99.0.2.\n- Latency budget 200 µs; while held, RTT is roughly 58–90 µs.\n\nNow I'll write the files. First, let me write `host/fn-env.sh`.\n\nDetails to be careful about in fn-env.sh:\n- Must be both sourceable and executable (bash -n only checks syntax).\n- Rail calculation: parse `ip -br addr show dev $rail`.\n\nFormat of `ip -br addr show dev thunderbolt0`: `thunderbolt0 UP 10.99.0.1/30` — fields: name, flags, address. Flags may contain multiple words (UP,LOWER_UP). Safer approach: use `ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\"` and grab tokens matching the IPv4 pattern: `grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}/[0-9]+'`. Then strip /prefix. Exclude 169.254.*. Exclude 127.*. Then head -1.\n\nfn-env.sh contents:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: ray head, ray worker, `vllm serve`, and\n# the bring-up/teardown scripts. Both ranks MUST see the same doctrine —\n# run-tp2's preflight byte-diffs the FN_ env across the pair before first\n# light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below is what carries\n# FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default copy prefixes;\n# without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh discipline\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1,2).\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: never export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If you think a\n# knob here is \"just pinning the default\", it is not — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n```\n\nThen the FN_ config block, then determinism/alloc/hsa, caches, NCCL (computed rails, IB disable, timeouts), ray, table path, offline.\n\nNext, the rail function. Also, when the candidate list ends up empty, log loudly and fail. Note: acceptance `grep -q thunderbolt0` — the function body contains the literal thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1. Good.\n\nWriting all files now. Executable bit too: chmod +x.\n\nfn-cluster-up.sh — write it carefully. Full text:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-cluster-up.sh — stand the flashnext pair service with one command.\n#\n# Order is load-bearing (reap-then-gate, ds4-cluster-restart.sh doctrine):\n#   1. REAP stranded serve processes on BOTH nodes first, gate on zero residue\n#   2. containers on both nodes (worker over ssh on the WIRE — the rails\n#      carry tensors only, never control traffic)\n#   3. ray head on the coordinator, worker join (python pool capped small)\n#   4. hard two-GPU gate before serve\n#   5. the TP=2 eager serve, then a wait-for-reality API poll\n#\n# Failure cannot strand ranks: flashnext-pair.service runs this as a oneshot\n# with ExecStopPost=fn-cluster-down.sh, so even a failed bring-up tears down.\nset -euo pipefail\n```\n\nFunctions: log, worker(), ship scripts via ssh stdin, env dump function.\n\nLocal env dump:\n```bash\nenv_dump() {  # $1 = output file\n  ( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n    | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)' \\\n    | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$1\"\n}\n```\nfn-env.sh logs to stderr; >/dev/null only suppresses stdout. Keep stderr visible (journald). No problem: `source ... >/dev/null`.\n\nWait — sourcing fn-env.sh requires `ip` etc. On the coordinator no issue.\n\nRemote env dump + container:\n```bash\n# Ship fn-env.sh to the worker over the wire; it computes ITS OWN rail list there.\nremote_tmp=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\nworker \"cat > '$remote_tmp/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nworker \"( set -a; source '$remote_tmp/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) | grep -E '...' | LC_ALL=C sort > '$remote_tmp/env.list'\"\n```\n\nContainer startup helper (same args for both nodes):\n```\npodman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\npodman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" \\\n  --network host --ipc host \\\n  --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n  --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n  -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n  -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n  --env-file \"$env_file\" \\\n  \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity\n```\n\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR\" on both nodes.\n\nReap:\n```bash\nreap_serve_node() {  # runs reap locally on whichever node; prints residue count\n  local pids\n  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n  if [ -n \"$pids\" ]; then\n    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 3\n    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n    [ -z \"$pids\" ] || kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 1\n  fi\n  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n}\n```\nHmm, pattern: vllm serve's cmdline is \"python3 /usr/local/bin/vllm serve ...\" or \"/opt/venv/bin/vllm serve\". Donor used `bin/[v]llm serve`. Use the same. But bare `vllm serve` via podman exec — cmdline contains the \"bin/vllm\" path. OK.\n\nLocal residue gate:\n```bash\nresidue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\n[ \"$residue\" -eq 0 ] || { log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) ...\"; exit 1; }\nremote: residue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\n```\nPassing the function over ssh: `worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\"` — ssh concatenates args into a single command string; fine.\n\nReaping the ray daemons too? Reap only targets serve, and ray stop comes at down time. For fresh startup: `podman exec ... ray stop --force || true` before ray start on each node? If a previous ray lingers inside the container and the container was rm -f'd, it dies with the container (does podman kill processes on rm -f? rootless podman with cgroups — yes, rm -f kills). So rm -f the containers before starting ray. Order: reap serve (host side, so that serve doesn't survive container removal in a stranded state?) — serve runs inside the container via podman exec -d; when rm -f kills the container, does the serve process die? podman exec -d process: when the container is stopped/killed, exec'd processes are killed via conmon/cgroup. Donor's husk issue was systemd-run unit + SIGKILL'd conmon leaving the wrapper behind. Belt and suspenders: reap host-side first (in line with steering \"reap then gate\"), then rm -f containers, then fresh ones.\n\nRay head start:\n```bash\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head \\\n  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n  --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" \\\n  --include-dashboard=false\n```\nRAY_NUM_CPUS from env (sourced fn-env.sh).\n\nWorker join:\n```bash\nworker \"podman exec $FN_CONTAINER ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT --num-cpus $RAY_NUM_CPUS\"\n```\n\n2-GPU gate:\n```bash\ngpus_total=\"\"\nfor _ in $(seq 1 120); do\n  gpus_total=\"$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null \\\n    | awk '$2 == \"GPU\" { split($1, a, \"/\"); print a[2]; exit }')\" || true\n  case \"$gpus_total\" in 2|2.0) break ;; esac\n  sleep 2\ndone\ncase \"$gpus_total\" in\n  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed\" ;;\n  *) log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (got '$gpus_total')\"; podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status || true; exit 1 ;;\nesac\n```\n\nServe launch + log + readiness:\n```bash\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83} \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n```\n(No comments inside the continuation — include the donor warning as a comment above.)\n\nReadiness:\n```bash\nattempts=$(( FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT / 20 ))\nfor i in $(seq 1 $attempts); do\n  if curl -fsS \"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$FN_SERVED_NAME\\\"\"; then ready=1; break; fi\n  sleep 20\ndone\n[ \"${ready:-0}\" -eq 1 ] || { log FATAL serve did not answer; tail serve.log; exit 1; }\n```\n\nfn-cluster-down.sh:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Idempotent teardown of the flashnext pair service on BOTH nodes.\n# Tolerates broken config, dead ssh, half-dead containers; always exit 0.\nset -u\nSCRIPT_DIR=...\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n: \"${FN_WORKER_HOST:=10.99.9.2}\" \"${FN_CONTAINER:=flashnext-pair}\" ...\n```\n`set -u` and ${FN_X:=default}: the file may only partially source fn-env.sh. Use explicit defaults for every variable used.\n\nSteps (each line `|| true`):\n1. On both nodes reap serve (TERM→KILL), no gate (best effort, though we do log residue).\n2. On both nodes podman exec ray stop --force || true.\n3. On both nodes podman rm -f container || true.\nexit 0.\n\nfn-preflight.sh — the heavier one. Structure:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-preflight.sh — first-light readiness record for the pair.\n#\n#   1. byte-diff of both ranks' FN_/doctrine env (the two TP ranks must not diverge)\n#   2. latency-hold device read on BOTH ends (/dev/cpu_dma_latency == 0)\n#   3. per-rail link-speed record, both nodes\n#   4. round-trip probe on both rails, both ends, vs the 200us budget\n#\n# Writes results/receipts/preflight.json; exits non-zero on any hard failure.\nset -euo pipefail\nSCRIPT_DIR=...; REPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nRECEIPT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/preflight.json\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$RECEIPT\")\"\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"; trap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\nstatus=pass\nfail() { echo \"fn-preflight: FAIL: $*\" >&2; status=fail; }\n```\n\nEnv dump like cluster-up: local + remote. `cmp -s` and record.\n\nLatency hold:\n```bash\nlatency_hold() {  # print \"<held>|<unit-state>|<device-value>\"\n  local unit val=\"\"\n  unit=\"$(systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n  if [ -r /dev/cpu_dma_latency ]; then val=\"$(od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency | tr -d ' \\n')\"\n  else val=\"$(sudo -n od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \\n' || true)\"; fi\n  echo \"${unit}|${val:-unreadable}\"\n}\n```\nheld = unit active && val == 0. unit active but unreadable → record and warn, don't fail. unit not active → fail.\n\nRemote via declare -f over ssh.\n\nLink speed: for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1: on both nodes cat /sys/class/net/$rail/speed (Mb/s), or \"absent\".\n\nRTT probe: for each rail that has a routable IP on that node: compute peer (/30 flip), ping -c 20 -i 0.05 -W 2, parse: `ping -q`'s last line `rtt min/avg/max/mdev = a/b/c/d ms` → avg ms → µs. Record per rail per end. Only rails that have a routable IP (i.e. NCCL rails) are subject to the budget check; rail 1 has no IP → record \"no-peer-ip\" and don't fail.\n\nThen write the receipt JSON via python heredoc that takes the temp files. Simpler: assemble the data as shell vars and write JSON via python reading env. Write small key=value pairs to $TMP/kv and let python construct nested JSON. Simplest, most robust: emit JSON fragments per probe from python? Keep it pragmatic: collect values into shell vars (env_ok, coord_hold, worker_hold, speeds..., rtts...), write JSON in python - <<PY with env-var interpolation via sys.argv. Pass a compact TSV $TMP/report and let python read it:\n\nreport file lines:\n```\nenv_diff\tpass\nhold_coordinator\tactive|0\nhold_worker\tactive|0\nspeed_coordinator_thunderbolt0\t40000\nspeed_coordinator_thunderbolt1\tabsent\n...\nrtt_coordinator_thunderbolt0_us\t87\nrtt_coordinator_thunderbolt1\tno-peer-ip\nrails_chosen\tthunderbolt0\n```\nThen python builds the receipt. Clean.\n\nPeer IP computation: from own IP on rail: a.b.c.1 → peer a.b.c.2; .2 → .1 (a /30 has usable addresses .1 and .2). Implement with awk.\n\nRTT parse: `ping -q -c 20 -i 0.05 -W 2 \"$peer\" | tail -n1 | awk -F'[/ ]' '{print $8}'` is ms avg (following the dotfiles sensor pattern: awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d\", $8*1000 }'). Use that.\n\nBudget: FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}. RTT (µs) > budget on a routable rail → fail.\n\nrun-tp2.sh — full runner. Let me write it with sections. Python heredocs using urllib.request.\n\nCommon API helper inlined in each heredoc:\n\n```python\nimport json, urllib.request\nAPI = ...\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API+path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n```\n\nstep_tp2 heredoc: load preflight.json, do 2 completions, compare, write tp2.json:\ndata: {byte_identical_repeat, completion_chars, rails (from preflight: speeds+rtts), rails_chosen, latency_hold both ends, rtt_budget_us}. Receipts-verify's _tp2 only checks byte_identical_repeat.\n\nstep_residency heredoc:\n- warmup: completion max_tokens=16 \"compile pass\"\n- warmed decode: completion max_tokens=64 (>50)\n- per-node metrics: coordinator locally; worker via ssh python over stdin (same metrics script). Metrics script:\n```python\nimport glob, json, os, subprocess\ndef gtt():\n    for cand, src in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\",\"gtt\"),(\"mem_info_vram_used\",\"vram\")):\n        for p in sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\"+cand)):\n            try: return int(open(p).read().strip()), src, p\n            except Exception: continue\n    return None, None, None\ndef rss():\n    total=0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit(): continue\n        try:\n            cmd=open(f\"/proc/{pid}/cmdline\",\"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception: continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" in cmd or \"ray::\" in cmd:\n            try:\n                with open(f\"/proc/{pid}/status\") as s:\n                    for line in s:\n                        if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                            total += int(line.split()[1])*1024\n            except Exception: pass\n    return total\ndef table_pages(patterns, root):\n    import fnmatch, shutil\n    if not shutil.which(\"fincore\"): return None, []\n    matched=[]\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), p) for p in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath,f))\n    pages=0\n    for m in matched:\n        out=subprocess.run([\"fincore\", m],capture_output=True,text=True).stdout\n        # fincore: header? lines: RES PAGES SIZE SIZE% FILE\n        for line in out.splitlines():\n            parts=line.split()\n            if len(parts)>=2 and parts[1].isdigit(): pages+=int(parts[1]); break? \n```\nHmm, fincore output format: `fincore file` → one line: `RES  PAGES  SIZE  SIZE%  FILE`, e.g. `10.9G  2863452  10.9G  100  /path`. Actually RES comes first. PAGES is the 2nd token. Take int(parts[1]).\n\nPatterns: os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\",\"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").lower().split(\",\").\n\nEmit JSON.\n\n- coordinator side: run the metrics script locally via python subprocess [sys.executable, \"-c\"?] — easier: write the metrics script once to $TMP/rank_metrics.py and: local `python3 $TMP/rank_metrics.py`; remote: `ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" python3 - < $TMP/rank_metrics.py`. Both emit JSON.\n- ple probe: grep -ci \"ple_mmap\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" > 0 → table_gpu_resident_bytes=0, otherwise None → receipt fails (loud, honest).\n- Write residency.json.\n\nNote: rank → node mapping: rank 0 = coordinator (head), rank 1 = worker. Keys in gtt_gib_per_rank should be \"coordinator\"/\"worker\" (the receipts-verify loops keys generically).\n\nstep_fidelity heredoc:\n- Prompts: 3 fixed. For each: completion request {model, prompt, max_tokens:64, temperature:0, logprobs:5, echo:true?}. echo is allowed in /v1/completions. loss = mean of -logprob over generated tokens (exclude the echo'd prompt tokens: response's choices[0].logprobs.token_logprobs — the first is None if not echo; with echo=true the first element corresponds to the first prompt token... Simpler: don't echo; then token_logprobs corresponds only to the generated tokens. loss = -sum/len. Frontier logits: logprobs of the final generated token {token: logprob, top5}.\n- Save results/fidelity/fidelity-<i>.json {prompt, text, loss_nll, frontier_logits, model}. sha256 each. Receipt fidelity.json {status, files:[names], sha256:{...}, prompts:3}.\n\nstep_context heredoc:\n- Short arm: prompt is a small fixed string (~30 tokens), max_tokens=FN_CTX_DECODE (128), stream=true → decode_tps via SSE timing.\n- Long arm: build long prompt: repeat paragraph until estimated tokens ≈ target-192. target = 262144 (spec says \"given\" so hardcode with comment; override via FN_CONTEXT_TARGET).\n- After response, check usage.prompt_tokens (the last stream chunk includes usage if you request it? Not guaranteed with SSE). Alternative: non-streaming for the long arm, compute elapsed total; approximate subtracting prefill... no.\n\n  Cleaner approach: measure decode-only via streaming on both arms. Streaming chunks in vLLM include usage in the last chunk only with stream_options={\"include_usage\":true}. Include that. decode_tps = (completion_tokens - 1) / (t_last_data - t_first_data). Both arms use the same method → ratio is fair.\n- SSE parse: line starts with \"data: \", json.loads, delta text? /v1/completions streaming returns {\"choices\":[{\"text\": \"...\"}]}. Track first text chunk time and final time.\n- Long prompt size: 262144-192 tokens ≈ chars = tokens * 3.6? For safety, build the text, and after the request check actual usage.prompt_tokens; if < 0.9*target → still record ratio? Receipt needs \"context rose to 262144\": status fail if prompt_tokens < 262144-1024... but token estimate uncertainty: overshoot risks server rejection (max-model-len). Strategy: aim for target * 0.98 with conservative 4.5 chars/token → fewer tokens than the estimate → safe on the length side; then verify actual ≥ 262144-1024. If short, pad and retry once (multiply text length by target/actual ratio). Implement one retry adjustment.\n\n- Write context.json: {target_context, actual_prompt_tokens_short/long, short_decode_tps, long_decode_tps, decode_ratio_vs_short_context}.\n\nIf any step fails → the receipt records fail where meaningful; the script exits non-zero.\n\ntp2.json needs preflight data — step_tp2 heredoc reads results/receipts/preflight.json.\n\nFinal bash: echo a summary of the receipt paths.\n\nNext, the test file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Host-tooling doctrine tests — env file, unit file, script hygiene.\n\nThe host-doctrine grep gate is satisfiable by wrong code (ROUTING.md calls\nthis out); these assertions aim at the semantic traps the gate cannot see:\nthe rail list is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 is\nunconditional, no probe-read engine default is exported (spec F.9), the unit\ntears down via ExecStopPost and installs at no boot target.\n\"\"\"\nimport pathlib, re, subprocess, unittest\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nHOST = ROOT / \"host\"\n\nclass HostTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp: read texts once (classmethod)\n\n    test_fn_env_doctrine_lines: lines containing PYTHONHASHSEED=0 etc.\n    test_fn_env_rails_computed_not_hardcoded: assertIn(\"ip -br addr\", env); assertNotIn(\"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1\", env); assertIn(\"thunderbolt0\", env)\n    test_fn_env_ib_disable_unconditional: re.search(r\"^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\", env, re.M) — must be a plain export, not inside an `if`: check the line is exactly `export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` (no ${...:-} guard).\n    test_fn_env_no_probe_read_defaults (F.9): for each probe in (VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER): assertNotRegex(env, rf\"^\\s*export {probe}=\")\n    test_unit_file: text = flashnext-pair.service: assertIn ExecStopPost, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes; assertNotIn WantedBy; assertNotIn(\"[Install]\"); assertIn fn-cluster-down.\n    test_scripts_syntax_clean: for host/*.sh + scripts/run-tp2.sh, bash -n.\n    test_cluster_up_reaps_before_gate: text.index(\"reap\") < text.index(\"ray start\"); reap... and residue gate present.\n    test_run_tp2_writes_four_receipts: for each of tp2/residency/fidelity/context, assertIn step name.\n```\n\nAlso the preflight script exists and is referenced from run-tp2.\n\nKeep it moderate.\n\nNow let's write the files. Executability too: chmod +x.\n\nOne more check — acceptance runs `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v` from repo root. Fine.\n\nAlso — the repo has a .gitignore; verify results/ isn't ignored... results/receipts already holds committed files, so not ignored. Files in results/fidelity are created at runtime — don't commit them now.\n\nEdge case: the heredoc python in run-tp2.sh must avoid `$` expansion conflicts — use quoted 'PY'.\n\nNow let's write the files. Starting with fn-env.sh.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All research done. Now I'll write the six deliverables. Starting with `host/fn-env.sh` — the env doctrine:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bf32c01f36b7467a98c67335","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-env.sh","content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: the ray head, the ray worker, `vllm\n# serve`, and the bring-up/teardown/preflight scripts. Both TP ranks MUST see\n# the same doctrine — fn-preflight.sh byte-diffs the exported env across the\n# pair before first light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below\n# is what carries FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default\n# copy prefixes; without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Discipline lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1–2).\n# Safe to source repeatedly; every default is override-able via the environment.\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: NEVER export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If a knob here\n# looks like \"just pinning the default\", it is not that — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# --- the FN_ configuration surface (propagated to the worker rank by ray) --\nexport FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"\nexport FN_MODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nexport FN_SERVED_NAME=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\nexport FN_PORT=\"${FN_PORT:-1234}\"\nexport FN_RAY_PORT=\"${FN_RAY_PORT:-6379}\"\nexport FN_MAX_CTX=\"${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}\"\nexport FN_GPU_UTIL=\"${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}\"\nexport FN_IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nexport FN_CONTAINER=\"${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}\"\n# Worker-side actions ride the ethernet wire on the STABLE fleet identity,\n# never a 10.99.0.x Thunderbolt rail — the rails carry tensors only\n# (dotfiles-observed.md §2.3: admin traffic prefers the 5GbE cable).\nexport FN_WORKER_HOST=\"${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}\"\nexport FN_HEAD_IP=\"${FN_HEAD_IP:-10.99.9.1}\"\n# The fleet latency budget from modules/lowlat-cluster.nix: 200 us sits well\n# above the measured held figure and well below the unheld one.\nexport FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=\"${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}\"\n\n# --- determinism -------------------------------------------------------------\n# vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from PYTHONHASHSEED:\n# without a fixed value identical token content produces different block\n# filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart.\n# The greedy byte-compare in run-tp2.sh leans on this too.\nexport PYTHONHASHSEED=0\n\n# --- allocator ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# GC is off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB means reclaim starts at\n# ~105 GiB. expandable_segments is the fragmentation half. Safe with\n# --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs.\nexport PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=\"${PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF:-expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85}\"\n\n# --- HSA ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) otherwise, i.e.\n# ~2-3 cores spinning during inference and CPU thermal throttling on a pair\n# whose PM QoS is held for latency. Set FN_HSA_INTERRUPT=0 to revert to spin.\nexport HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\"${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}\"\n\n# --- compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs ------------------\n# The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bring-up after a boot\n# then recompiles every kernel — ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can\n# answer. The state directory is on-disk on both nodes.\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/triton\" 2>/dev/null || true\nexport TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\"${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor}\"\nexport TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\"${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/triton}\"\n\n# --- NCCL: the transport-of-record block --------------------------------------\n# Socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record (spec\n# ruling P7); the RDMA package under host/rdma is operator-attended only, and\n# only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked.\n#\n# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`: only Thunderbolt rails\n# that actually carry a routable /30 IP qualify. Rail 0 (thunderbolt0,\n# 10.99.0.x) is guaranteed; rail 1 (thunderbolt1) is trained but\n# IP-unconfigured today (dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix parks it) and\n# MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP — a peerless link-local rail in\n# the list hangs RCCL bootstrap. We log which rails we chose rather than\n# hardcoding both.\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail addr chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    if [ ! -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ]; then\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent on this node; not listed\" >&2\n      continue\n    fi\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \\\n      | grep -Ev '^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)' | head -n1 || true)\"\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME ($addr is routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  printf '%s' \"$chosen\"\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"\nif [ -z \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ]; then\n  echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no Thunderbolt rail carries a routable IP; refusing to name a phantom transport\" >&2\n  return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1\nfi\n\n# NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY. Since the pre-arm bake an ibverbs device\n# exists on the rails of BOTH nodes (usb4_rdma0 + usb4_rdma5, by design);\n# without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA.\n# Sockets stay the transport of record until the attended morning A/B lands a\n# counterbalanced verdict (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md). Do NOT conditionalize\n# this on device detection — the devices are present by design.\nexport NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\n\n# Cold-kernel-cache bring-up is ~25 min of LLVM before the first collective;\n# the collective timeouts must survive that without a watchdog kill.\nexport TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=\"${TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-2400}\"\nexport TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=\"${TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING:-0}\"\nexport NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=\"${NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS:-2400000}\"\n\n# --- ray ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Propagate FN_* to the worker rank through ray. ESSENTIAL: without this the\n# two TP ranks diverge on every FN_ knob above.\nexport VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\n# Ray and ROCr visible-device handling fight on multi-rank boxes; opt out.\nexport RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"${RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-1}\"\n# On a 128 GB unified-memory box the serve legitimately claims most memory;\n# ray's OOM monitor must not reap the rank. Refresh 0 disables the monitor.\nexport RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=\"${RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms:-0}\"\n# `ray start` pre-starts one idle python worker per CPU (~40 MB each); on a\n# 32-thread box that is over a gigabyte vLLM never touches. Cap the pool\n# small; fn-cluster-up.sh passes this as --num-cpus on both nodes.\nexport RAY_NUM_CPUS=\"${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}\"\n\n# --- the table path ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Serve the engram table from NVMe via mmap: page-cache faults serve gathers,\n# zero table bytes GPU-resident (spec ruling P11's zero-GPU-residency bound\n# leans on this). VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 is the fork's kill switch.\nexport VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\n\n# --- estate hygiene -------------------------------------------------------------\n# Runtime hub downloads stay forbidden (spec F.6): the checkpoint is staged\n# library-to-node, and the engine must never reach for the hub at runtime.\nexport HF_HUB_OFFLINE=\"${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=\"${VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=\"${VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK:-1}\"\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":11993,"output":8521,"cacheRead":55296,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6145,"totalTokens":75810,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989190374,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f132b478-a97b-457c-8a5e-f9ec274043d7","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bf32c01f36b7467a98c67335","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 8288 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-env.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989355782}]}
