{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There are three problems (three failures — the third one was cut off, let's check everything):\n\n1. `test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded`: \"169.254\" is not found — my fn-env.sh uses the escaped form `169\\.254\\.` inside grep -Ev. That assertion is overly strict on my own test side: the env file contains `169\\.254` (escaped dots). Either fix the test to check for \"169\", or just delete that assertion… Actually the semantic intent is: link-local filtering exists. Check for `169\\\\.254` — the raw text contains `169\\.254`. assertIn(\"169\", ...) passes. Let's check for `grep -Ev '^(169`… More simply: assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\", self.env) — Python string \"169\\\\.254\" = literal `169\\.254`. This will match.\n\n2. `test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free`: \"WantedBy\" is found — it's in my own comment (\"never via WantedBy\")! And the acceptance criteria run `! grep -q 'WantedBy' host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service` — the acceptance criteria also fail! I have to remove the word WantedBy from the unit file's comments. Rewrite the last comment without using that word: \"never at boot, no [Install] section\"... wait, does \"[Install]\" appear in a comment too? Acceptance only greps for WantedBy. But my test also asserts NotIn(\"[Install]\") — my comment says \"Deliberately NO [Install] section\". That's fine for acceptance, but my test fails on it. To keep things clean, remove mentions of both literals from the comments: phrase it as \"Deliberately no install section: ...\".\n\nAlso check the third failure — the output was truncated (\"failures=3\"). Let's re-run after the fix to see the remaining failure. Probably test_cluster_up_reaps_before_it_gates: \"zero residue\"? It's there. Or test_fn_env_cache_dirs_are_not_tmpfs: the regex `CACHE_DIR=[^ ]*/tmp\\b` — my line has `CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor` — no /tmp. Hmm, but the comment says \"The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs)\" — the regex `CACHE_DIR=[^ ]*/tmp\\b` — the comment has \"under /tmp (tmpfs)\", but the line before it has no \"CACHE_DIR=\"… Actually the section header comment \"compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs\" has no \"CACHE_DIR=\". OK.\n\nLet's just re-run after the fix to see all the failures.\n"}}
