{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The plumbing works end-to-end: env-var byte diff, hold probe, link speed, RTT probe, receipt JSON. But my awk parse is exposing a bug:\n\nRTT: the ping shim outputs `rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 58.0/77.2/112.0/9.1 ms`. Expected average is 77.2 ms → 77200 µs? No wait — 77.2 ms = 77200 µs. But the actual fleet RTT is around 77 µs, not 77 ms! My shim wrote \"ms\" with the value 77.2 — in real ping, when the RTT is sub-millisecond, does it report e.g. `rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 0.058/0.077/0.112/0.009 ms`? ping prints in ms, and sub-ms values show as fractional ms like 0.077. The dotfiles sensor: `awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d rtt 1\\n\", $8 * 1000 }'` — avg × 1000 → µs. Same as mine. So the parse is correct, and my shim was unrealistic (I wrote 77.2 ms instead of 0.077 ms). Not a bug.\n\nAlso note \"speed_mbps\": \"40000\" — this comes from the actual /sys/class/net/... wait, no, rail_speeds reads the literal /sys/class/net/$rail/speed — I didn't redirect this! This machine (tom's dev machine? no — the lane runs on the coordinator host... this shell is on the actual coordinator machine, and thunderbolt0 exists at 40000 Mb/s!). Interesting — the lane's shell runs directly on the coordinator, where thunderbolt0 exists. Good to know: /sys/class/net/thunderbolt0/speed = 40000. And for thunderbolt1 too... it also reported 40000 (present, negotiated). OK — that's the actual hardware under test. In that case, it might also be possible to source fn-env.sh on this machine and check the actual `ip` output! Let me test the actual rail computation on the coordinator (read-only).\n\nNote: the `speed_mbps` value type is a string inside the JSON — no problem for the record.\n\nLet me re-run the dry run with a realistic ping shim (0.077 ms) and confirm budget pass. Then test the actual fn-env.sh sourcing on this actual machine (the coordinator) — read-only, writes nothing other than mkdir of $HOME/.local/state/flashnext (harmless; that's a cache dir anyway; well, it creates a directory — tolerable? that's the intended state directory on this node; harmless). Actually, let me avoid side effects: set HOME to a temp dir and source the real fn-env.sh without the sysfs redirect. Real ip/iproute2 will show the real rail."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"The plumbing works end-to-end (my shim's 77.2 *ms* was unrealistic — the actual fleet RTT is around 77 *µs*, which comes out as `0.077 ms` in the ping output, and `×1000 → µs` handles this correctly). Interestingly, this lane's shell is running on the actual coordinator — `thunderbolt0` exists at 40000 Mb/s. I'll verify by cross-checking against the **actual** `ip` output here, and re-run the dry run using realistic RTTs:\n\n"}}
