inventory touched this session\n658:## Appendix B — commands run, for reproduction\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988680722},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me try reading section 5 (lines 654–818) of ds4-vllm-manifest.md and section 5 (lines 438–498) of dotfiles-observed.md. I might also read sections 1.1–1.4 of dotfiles-observed (details on latency hold, lowlat-cluster). Since fn-preflight.sh requires \"latency hold device reads from both ends\" and \"per-rail link speed records, round-trip probes on both rails.\"\n\nAlright, let me read ds4 section 5 first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_461f9fb0f898420a8a422d15","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md","offset":654,"limit":170}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1600,"output":227,"cacheRead":16896,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":126,"totalTokens":18723,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988680726,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0c3eb76b-a2e3-4b4d-8467-5d4f80206067","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_461f9fb0f898420a8a422d15","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## 5. `host/` — every file, one line each, plus the env/perf inventory\n\n### 5.1 File inventory\n\n| Path | Lines | Purpose (pinned) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `host/ds4-config.yaml` | 18 | Site config; flat `key: value` only, \"parsed by ds4-config with the Python stdlib, no pyyaml\" (:3-4) |\n| `host/ds4-config` | 27 | `#!/usr/bin/python3`; each `key: value` → `export DS4_<KEY>=<shlex.quote(value)>` (:23-27). Used as `eval \"$(\"$HOME/ds4-config\")\"` (:4) |\n| `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh` | 148 | **Canonical env**, sourced by ray head, box2 ray worker, and `vllm serve` (:2-3) |\n| `host/ds4-cluster-env.rdma.sh` | 25 | `source`s base then pins one unambiguous `NCCL_IB_HCA` (:8-9) |\n| `host/ds4-cluster-env.tcp.sh` | 13 | `source`s base, disables IB + both tbv all-reduces (:7-13) — correctness/fallback profile |\n| `host/ds4-cluster-restart.sh` | 145 | Full bringup: teardown → containers → ray → serve → verify (:2) |\n| `host/ds4-cluster-down.sh` | 35 | The stop half, idempotent (:2-5) |\n| `host/container-heal.sh` | 29 | Reconciles \"podman says Up but crun is dead (conmon SIGKILLed)\" (:3-4); always exits 0 (:7) |\n| `host/ds4-vllm-manual-serve.sh` | 130 | The `vllm serve` launcher; runs **inside** the container (:2). \"THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH\" (:6) |\n| `host/ds4-vllm-warmup.py` | 77 | 2-phase post-start warmup (tiny request, then one `DS4_WARMUP_CTX` prefill) |\n| `host/systemd/ds4-vllm.service` | 23 | `Type=oneshot`, `RemainAfterExit=yes`, ExecStart/Stop/StopPost |\n\n### 5.2 `ds4-config.yaml` keys — all 14 (`:5-18`) [S]\n\n| Key | Line | Value | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `model` | :5 | `deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731` | \"weights needed on BOTH boxes\" |\n| `transport` | :6 | `rdma` | `rdma \\| tcp` — selects `ds4-cluster-env.<transport>.sh` |\n| `head_ip` | :7 | `192.168.100.1` | |\n| `worker_ip` | :8 | `192.168.100.2` | |\n| `container` | :9 | `vllm` | podman container name, same both boxes |\n| `rdma_hca` | :10 | `usb4_rdma0` | `NCCL_IB_HCA` pin, rdma only |\n| `api_port` | :11 | `1234` | |\n| `disk_kv` | :12 | `true` | fs_lru NVMe prefix-KV tier |\n| `disk_kv_gib` | :13 | `30` | **per-NODE** disk cap |\n| `max_ctx` | :14 | `524288` | `--max-model-len`, \"the validated profile\" |\n| `kv_pin_gib` | :15 | `6` | pinned GPU KV pool |\n| `gpu_mem_util` | :16 | `0.83` | **\"IGNORED while the KV pin above is set\"** |\n| `warmup_ctx` | :17 | `2048` | 0 disables |\n| `cables` | :18 | `1` | TB cables; 2 dedicates second NHI to RX zero-copy rail |\n\n### 5.3 The env/perf settings inventory the campaign asked for — each pinned\n\n| Setting | Pin | Value | Why (quoted/paraphrased from the file) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **`PYTHONHASHSEED`** | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:13` | `0` | :9-12 — \"vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from it, so without a fixed value identical token content produces different block filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart\" |\n| **`PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF` expandable_segments** | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:27` | `expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85` | :14-26 — GC off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB ≈ reclaim at ~105 GiB; expandable_segments is \"the fragmentation half\"; \"Safe with `--enforce-eager` (no graph capture)\" |\n| **`HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT`** | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:83` | `${DS4_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}` | :79-82 — \"TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) -> ~2-3 cores spinning during inference -> CPU thermal throttle. Set 0 to revert to spin\" |\n| **Cache dirs off tmpfs (Triton/Inductor)** | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:49-50` | `TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=$HOME/.cache/torchinductor`, `TRITON_CACHE_DIR=$HOME/.triton/cache` | :45-48 — \"the defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bringup after a boot then recompiles every kernel -- ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can answer\" |\n| **Cache dir off tmpfs (KV staging)** | `host/ds4-vllm-manual-serve.sh:104` | `DS4_OFFLOAD_MMAP_DIR=${KVDIR%/}-stage` | :99-103 — must be a **SIBLING** of the cache dir, never inside it: \"fs_lru owns root_dir exclusively… will `os.remove()` it when it becomes the LRU victim. Nesting staging under root_dir therefore hands the evictor the live mmap.\" Implementation: `patch:2443` |\n| **Per-call-invariant latching (MoE)** | `patch:1261-1288` + `opt_flags_amd.py:13-28` + `patch:1384-1433` | 3 device queries + 7 env reads + 4 target queries | patch:1249-1260; justified by \"must be exported before `ray start` so both TP ranks agree, so a per-call read cannot observe anything a first-use read would not\" |\n| **Per-call-invariant latching (W8A8 flags)** | `patch:1449-1459` | `_DS4_W8A8_BF16`, `_DS4_W8A8_BF16_DIRECT` at import | patch:1449-1451 — \"a per-call lookup buys nothing and costs ~255 os.environ hits per decode step\" |\n| `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` surgery | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:89-96` | strips **only** `/opt/rocm/llvm/lib` | :84-88 — that path's runtimes \"change ROCr's initialization order and reproduce the AsyncEventsLoop spin even with the patched DSO preloaded\" |\n| `LD_PRELOAD` of patched ROCr | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:103-116` | prepends `…/_rocm_sdk_core/lib/libhsa-runtime64.so.1` | :97-102; `DS4_HSA_PRELOAD=0` disables only the preload |\n| `HSA_ENABLE_MWAITX` | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:121` | `${DS4_HSA_MWAITX:-0}` | :117-120 — optional fallback for *finite* active waits |\n| `PYTHONWARNINGS` | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:28` | append `ignore::FutureWarning` | :5-8 — per-step `all_gather_into_tensor` deprecation → journal spam |\n| `VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY` | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:78` | `DS4_` | :77 — \"propagate DS4_* to box2 ray workers (not in ray's default copy prefixes)\" — **essential**, without it the two TP ranks diverge |\n| NCCL/RDMA block | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:29-41` | `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0`, `NCCL_IB_HCA=usb4_rdma`, `NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=1`, `NCCL_IB_DISABLE=0`, `NCCL_NET_GDR_LEVEL=0`, `NCCL_IB_TIMEOUT=23`, `NCCL_PROTO=LL`, `NCCL_ALGO=Ring`, `NCCL_IB_RETRY_CNT=7`, `TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2400`, `TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=0`, `NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=2400000` | — |\n| Ray block | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:42-44` | `RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1`, `RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=0`, `RAY_memory_usage_threshold=0.99` | — |\n| Misc | `host/ds4-cluster-env.sh:51-53` | `HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`, `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=0`, `VLLM_EXECUTE_MODEL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800` | — |\n\n### 5.4 ⚠ `ds4-cluster-env.sh` MoE knobs — record cite `:127` RE-PINNED\n\nRecord cited \":127 MoE knobs\". At `:127` sits the **comment header**, not the exports:\n\n- `:127-130` — comment: *\"MXFP4 matmul_ogs DECODE kernel config (tuned on this hardware;\n  block_k 256 + num_stages 2 is the bandwidth lever the stock heuristic never picks).\n  Decode-scoped in opt_flags (only under block_m<128) so prefill is untouched. MUST live\n  here so BOTH TP ranks match. Unset the 5 vars to revert.\"*\n- `:144-148` — the **actual exports** (five of them) [S]:\n\n```bash\nexport DS4_MOE_BN=${DS4_MOE_BN:-32}\nexport DS4_MOE_NW=${DS4_MOE_NW:-2}\nexport DS4_MOE_NS=${DS4_MOE_NS:-2}\nexport DS4_MOE_BK=${DS4_MOE_BK:-256}\nexport DS4_MOE_WPE=${DS4_MOE_WPE:-1}\n```\n\n`opt_flags.py` reads **seven** `DS4_MOE_*` keys (patch:1288: `BM, BN, NW, NS, BK,\nKEEP_MFMA, WPE`) but the env file exports only **five**. `DS4_MOE_BM` and\n`DS4_MOE_KEEP_MFMA` are implemented-but-never-deployed. The comment at :130 saying\n\"the 5 vars\" is consistent with the env file, not with the reader. [M/S]\n\nAlso note :131-135 places the `DS4_IDX_OFFICIAL` comment **inside** the MoE comment\nblock — the two are textually interleaved and easy to misread when lifting.\n\n### 5.5 Cluster restart script — the two things it encodes (`ds4-cluster-restart.sh:8-23`)\n\nBoth are quoted verbatim in the header and both are non-obvious [S]:\n\n1. **REAPING** (:10-15). `ds4-vllm-manual` is a transient `systemd-run` unit supervising\n   a `podman exec` **wrapper**, not the process inside the container. Stopping the unit\n   \"leaves `vllm serve` running, holding its port and ~0.4 GB. Repeated restarts strand\n   one husk each.\" Implementation :63-69: `ps` + bracket-grep `bin/[v]llm serve deepseek`\n   (:63, trick documented at :62), SIGTERM → 3s → SIGKILL, then a **hard gate**:\n   `[ \"$residual\" -eq 0 ] || { … exit 1; }` (:69).\n2. **RAY WORKER POOL** (:17-23). `ray start` passes\n   `--num_prestart_python_workers=<num_cpus>` through with \"no env override\n   (ray/_private/services.py:1980)\"; unbounded that is \"32 idle Python workers at ~40 MB\n   each, ~1.28 GB that vLLM never touches\". Fix: `RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}` (:38)\n   → `--num-cpus=$RAY_NUM_CPUS` (:92). Dashboard off for the same reason (:93).\n\nOther pinned structure [S]: config load :31 · `box2()`/`inbox()` ssh/podman helpers\n:54-55 · memory drain loop with `<45G` gate :74-80 · `container-heal.sh` on both boxes\n:84-85 · head-only `--include-dashboard=false` (:93, \"ray PANICs if it is passed to a\nworker\" :91) · **2-GPU gate** :103-108 (aborts if `ray status` never reports `2.0 GPU`)\n· `systemd-run --user --unit=ds4-vllm-manual` :112-115 · API poll 105×20s ≈ 35 min\n:119-125 (warm ≈4 min, cold-kernel-cache ≈+25 min, :117-118) · warmup dispatched as a\nseparate `--collect` transient unit :130-135 · verify block :137-145 incl. the RDMA\nreadiness grep `tbv_ar2: rank[0-9] ready \\(qpn=…\\)` (:141) which prints\n`!! tbv_ar2 NOT ready -- decode all-reduce is not on RDMA` on miss (:142).\n\n### 5.6 Teardown (`ds4-cluster-down.sh`) [S]\n\nIdempotent (:5). Tolerates a broken config: `eval … 2>/dev/null || true` with hardcoded\nfallbacks (:12-16). Same reap loop (:23-27), then `ray stop --force` locally (:29-30) and\nover ssh (:31-32). `exit 0` unconditionally (:34).\n\n### 5.7 Warmup (`ds4-vllm-warmup.py`) [S]\n\nEnv: `DS4_VLLM_PORT` (:13, dflt `1234`), `DS4_WARMUP_MODEL` (:14, dflt\n`deepseek-v4-flash`), `DS4_WARMUP_CTX` (:17, dflt `2048`), `DS4_WARMUP` (:50, dflt `1`;\n`0` disables). `wait_ready(timeout_s=1200)` :24. Two phases: (1) `fire(\"Say ACK.\", 12,\n300)` :60 — \"compiles the ~50 vLLM Triton kernels (topk/w8a8/sparse-attn/metadata) +\ndecode + MTP drafter\" (:55-57); (2) one `WARMUP_CTX`-sized prefill :69 — \"one long\nprefill grows context through every 8192-bucket\" (:64), which matches\n`_PREFILL_KV_BUCKET = 8192` at `ds4_tl_indexer.py:312`. Every phase wrapped in\ntry/except (:58-62, :67-72) — \"Best-effort: never fails the service\" (:9).\n\n### 5.8 systemd unit (`host/systemd/ds4-vllm.service`) [S]\n\n`Type=oneshot` + `RemainAfterExit=yes` (:14-15); `TimeoutStartSec=45min` (:16),\n`TimeoutStopSec=5min` (:17). `ExecStart=%h/ds4-cluster-restart.sh` (:18),\n`ExecStop=%h/ds4-cluster-down.sh` (:19), **`ExecStopPost=%h/ds4-cluster-down.sh`** (:23)\nwith the reason at :20-22: *\"ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails; StopPost\ndoes, so a failed bringup still tears the cluster down instead of leaving the serve unit\nand ray daemons running behind a 'failed' wrapper.\"*\n**No `[Install]` section** — deliberate, :9-11: \"the model takes minutes to load and\nclaims both GPUs, so the stack comes up only on an explicit start, never at boot.\nhy3-llamacpp declares `Conflicts=ds4-vllm.service`\".\n\n### 5.9 Serve flags (`ds4-vllm-manual-serve.sh:111-130`) [S]\n\n`--tensor-parallel-size 2` :113 · `--distributed-executor-backend ray` :114 ·\n**`--enforce-eager` :115** · `--kv-cache-dtype fp8` :116 ·\n`--gpu-memory-utilization ${DS4_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}` :117 ·\n`--kv-cache-memory-bytes ${DS4_KV_BYTES:-6442450944}` :118 ·\n`--max-model-len ${DS4_MAX_CTX:-524288}` :119 · **`--max-num-batched-tokens 512`** :120 ·\n`--tokenizer-mode/--reasoning-parser/--tool-call-parser deepseek_v4` :122-127 ·\n`--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"deepseek_mtp\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":5,\n\"disable_padded_drafter_batch\":true,\"enforce_eager\":true}'` :128.\n\nThree memory notes worth lifting (:9-20): `--kv-cache-memory-bytes` must be pinned\nbecause \"the bf16 weight cache and the RDMA buffers are allocated AFTER the profiling\npass\"; `--gpu-memory-utilization` is \"INERT while the pin above is set … (gpu_worker.py:384)\";\n`--max-num-batched-tokens 512` **not** 2048 because \"the indexer/top-k workspace scales\nwith batch x context and 2048 costs ~10 GiB more at 256K\".\n\n⚠ Syntax landmine, :22-24: *\"Do not add comments inside the backslash-continued `vllm\nserve` command below: a '#' there silently comments out every remaining argument, and\n`bash -n` still reports the file as valid.\"*\n\nPre-flight degrade check :86-92: if the image lacks `lru_manager.py` **or**\n`distributed.py`, warn loudly and set `DS4_DISK_KV=0` rather than let `vllm serve` die on\n`\"Unknown secondary tier type: 'fs_lru'\"` (:81-85).\n\n---\n\n## 6. `AGENTS.md` bring-up doctrine\n\n### 6.1 §0.1 prerequisites boundary — quoted verbatim (`AGENTS.md:35-45`)\n\n> ## 0.1 Prerequisites (verify these exist; do NOT try to synthesize them)\n\n[451 more lines in file. Use offset=824 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988686652},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent — this is the donor material for the host/ discipline (ds4-vllm lift manifest §5). Next, section 5 of dotfiles-observed.md, and also section 1.1-1.4 (latency hold, lowlat-cluster options) since fn-preflight needs the latency-hold device read. Also need to understand the host/rdma A/B protocol evidence (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md is referenced in the brief's fn-env.sh comment: \"evidence host/rdma/ab-protocol.md\").","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d472b615236b42429fabe4c7","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":120,"offset":438,"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":6033,"output":218,"cacheRead":17408,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":117,"totalTokens":23659,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988686657,"responseId":"chatcmpl-c7e1aae5-5df3-4bc7-94de-584383739adf","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d472b615236b42429fabe4c7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## 5. What a new vLLM TP=2 pair module would plug into\n\n### 5.1 Existing systemd patterns for model serving **[S]**\n\nThere is exactly **one** long-running model-serving unit pattern: **`services.llama-swap`** (upstream NixOS module, configured by `modules/llama-swap.nix`), plus its `systemd.services.llama-swap` overlay at `:93-124` (DynamicUser, ProtectSystem=strict, State/Cache dirs, `LimitMEMLOCK=infinity`).\n\n**There is NO existing \"pair-check\" oneshot.** `grep -rni \"pair-check\\|pair_check\\|tp=2\\|tensor-parallel\\|tensor_parallel\" --include=*.nix --include=*.md .` returns only prose: `docs/local-ai/ds4-vllm-recon-2026-08-21.md:14`, `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:52`, `modules/strix-ai.nix:60`, `lib/local-models.nix:774` **[M]**.\n\nThe reusable **oneshot idioms already in the tree** that a pair service would compose with **[S]**:\n- `local-models-sync` — `Type=oneshot`, `RequiresMountsFor`, `restartTriggers`, `TimeoutStartSec=2h` (`modules/local-models.nix:392-412`)\n- `library-reachable` — the #240 \"wait for reality, not for a target's word\" gate, `Type=oneshot`, `RemainAfterExit=true`, `TimeoutStartSec=3min`, body is a `bash -c 'exec 3<>/dev/tcp/nas/2049'` poll loop over 120 s (`hosts/worker/default.nix:189-212`). **This is the pattern a TP=2 rank-1 gate should copy.**\n- `fleet-identity` — `Type=oneshot`, `RemainAfterExit=true`, `after=network-pre.target` (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:44-53`)\n- `tb-link-heal` — `Type=oneshot` + timer + `StateDirectory` (§6)\n- `myTripwire` — declarative sensor/threshold/sustain/act, `modules/tripwire.nix:39-140`; the recon doc already names a tripwire candidate (`docs/local-ai/ds4-vllm-recon-2026-08-21.md:84-86`: journal-grep `tbv_ar2: rank[0-9] ready`, absence after bring-up = silently running on TCP).\n\n### 5.2 Is there a flake input for nix-strix-halo? **YES** **[S]/[M]**\n\n`flake.nix:244` — `nix-strix-halo.url = \"github:hellas-ai/nix-strix-halo\";` (deliberately **no** `inputs.nixpkgs.follows`, rationale `:234-243`). Also in `rollingInputOverrides` at `flake.nix:292-295`.\n\n`flake.lock` locked node **[M]**: `owner hellas-ai / repo nix-strix-halo / rev f0f2048ff842749b363ea3562a98fb0a04bb2e61 / narHash sha256-2HKCQel/UbZkxV6/IiLBhbXV32x8ImRvRmrBM0MOoAs= / lastModified 1787040186`.\n\nI located and **cryptographically confirmed** the realized source:\n```\n$ nix hash path --sri --type sha256 /nix/store/9abiq2k7f7rkkmr1b96r7lrflg9w1jnz-source\nsha256-2HKCQel/UbZkxV6/IiLBhbXV32x8ImRvRmrBM0MOoAs=      ✓ exact match to flake.lock\n```\n\n**What that locked input already carries — this is the single most useful finding for a TP=2 module [S]:**\n\n- **Transitive locked inputs** (from `flake.lock`, `nodes[\"nix-strix-halo\"].inputs`):\n  - `vllm-src` → `vllm-project/vllm` rev `752a3a504485790a2e8491cacbb35c137339ad34`; upstream declares `url = \"github:vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1\"` (its `flake.nix:47-48`), version string `\"0.25.1\"` at `:369`\n  - `thunderbolt-ibverbs` → `hellas-ai/thunderbolt-ibverbs` rev `76ba39b630a70accb72f19388eefe48844b50eb8` — **the exact rev the recon doc names** (`docs/local-ai/ds4-vllm-recon-2026-08-21.md:26`)\n  - `linux-src` → `git.kernel.org/…/westeri/thunderbolt.git`, ref `refs/heads/next`, rev `503c5ae1e72aa9ed91925dafa3d82ee2e992747f` — **the exact patched-core rev the recon doc names** (`:26`)\n  - `ds4` → `antirez/ds4` rev `80ebbc39…`; `ds4-hip` → `ejpir/ds4-hip` rev `3490c2e4…`\n- **`nixosModules`** (upstream `flake.nix:513-532`): `default` (imports `thunderbolt-ibverbs.nixosModules.default` + applies its overlay), `thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `rpc-server`, `benchmark-executor`, `benchmark-runner`, `ec-su-axb35`, `fastflowlm`, `smu-exporter`, `npu-exporter`, `ryzenadj`, `tuning`.\n  ⚠ Note `modules/strix-ai.nix:80-83` deliberately adopts **only** `hardware.firmware = [strixAi.strix-halo-mes-firmware]` from upstream's `tuning` module, because importing it wholesale would regress the GTT ceiling (§4.1).\n- **Packages exposing `linux-thunderbolt`, `linux-thunderbolt-dev`, `linux-thunderbolt-modules`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-linux-thunderbolt`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-bench-tools`, `thunderbolt-ibverbs-perftest`** (upstream `flake.nix:305-318`, `:657-658`, `:701-706`).\n- **A ready-made TP=2 pair harness**: `strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-<s>` (upstream `flake.nix:712-714`, package at `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix`, 9484 B), built on `vllmPairBenchEnv = symlinkJoin [ pkgs.vllm-rocm … ]` \"so the vLLM closure exposes both `vllm` and `ray`\" (`:683-689`). Its scenarios include `qwen-peak | llama-tp2-win | qwen35-122b-awq-capacity | qwen35-122b-awq-prime | minimax-m27-awq-strix-2h`; it takes `MASTER`/`WORKER`, `TRANSPORTS` including `usb4_rdma` and `lan_tcp`, and exports `TB_IFNAME=thunderbolt0`, `USB4_HCA`, and a full NCCL env block (`NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=1`, `NCCL_MIN_NCHANNELS=4`, …) — `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix:140-235` **[S]**.\n\n### 5.3 The #237 escape hatches — what survived **[S]**\n\n`modules/strix-ai.nix:55-64` (the whole diff of `b8b63f41`) removes `ds4-rocm` and `vllm-rocm` from `environment.systemPackages`, leaving a comment: *\"when the DS4 TP=2 bring-up resumes (docs/local-ai/ds4-vllm-recon-2026-08-21.md), `nix build .#ds4-rocm` / `.#vllm-rocm` are the one-command escape hatches, and the renderer backends in modules/local-models.nix stay declared — they only enter a closure when a deployment actually selects them.\"*\n\nVerified, all three legs:\n1. **Flake outputs still exist.** `flake.nix:568-578` — `inherit (strixAi) ds4-rocm ec-su-axb35-monitor llama-cpp-rocm llama-cpp-vulkan mlx-lm mlx-rocm strix-halo-mes-firmware tokenizers-cpp vllm-rocm;` under `packages.${system}`, described at `:563-566` as \"Explicit accelerator escape hatches.\"\n2. **Renderers still declared.** `lib/local-model-runtime.nix:24-45` — `ds4` and `vllm` renderers intact. The `vllm` renderer emits:\n   `${packages.vllm}/bin/vllm serve <modelDirectory> --host 127.0.0.1 --port ${PORT} --served-model-name <deployment.model>`\n   with `env = offlineModelEnv ++ [TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR, TRITON_CACHE_DIR, VLLM_CACHE_ROOT, VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1, VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1]` and `useModelName`.\n   **⚠ This renderer is single-node: `--host 127.0.0.1`, no `--tensor-parallel-size`, no Ray address. A TP=2 pair service cannot reuse it as-is.**\n3. **Backend enum still complete.** `lib/local-model-backends.nix:2-8`, asserted at `flake.nix:1800-1818` and again at `:1827-1831` (exact rendered `vllm` cmd string + `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` in env).\n4. **Devshell** — `flake.nix:589+` `devShells.${system}.default` contains agenix + a `with pkgs` list; **no ds4/vllm devshell exists** (`grep -n \"ds4\\|vllm\" flake.nix` shows no hit inside the devShells block) **[M]**.\n\n### 5.4 What such a module would have to add itself **[S]**\n\n- **Firewall.** `networking.firewall.trustedInterfaces = [ \"enp191s0\" ]` on both boxes (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:80`, `hosts/worker/default.nix:321`) — **`thunderbolt0` is NOT trusted on either host**, and `flake.nix:725-726` asserts `wlp192s0` is never trusted. Ray/NCCL/ar2 ports (recon doc names 18515/18531, `:70`) on 10.99.0.x would need explicit admissions.\n- **A both-ends invariant.** The `modules/strix.nix` import-is-the-gate idiom (§1.5) is the committed precedent for anything that must be symmetric.\n- **The MTU decision.** `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:49-54` explicitly parks the second TB rail and jumbo frames: \"a TP=2 decode all-reduce is a ~5 KB payload, i.e. latency-bound, not bandwidth-bound. When that changes, it belongs in tb-fleet.nix beside the rail it extends.\"\n- **Weights.** `deepseek-v4-flash-0731-bf16` (`lib/local-models.nix:771-…`) is stocked in the Library only and named \"REQUIRED IN FULL ON EACH BOX … Served by vLLM/Ray as NixOS services, never as a llama-swap row.\" **⚠ Its own stated download command is broken — see §7.5.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. `tb-link-heal` / `tb-fleet` units\n\n### 6.1 Where defined — **DUPLICATED, not parameterized** **[S]**\n\n| | coordinator | worker |\n|---|---|---|\n| file | `hosts/coordinator/tb-fleet.nix` (157 lines) | `hosts/worker/default.nix` |\n| heal script | `:50-105` (`let healScript = pkgs.writeShellScript \"tb-link-heal\"`) | `:225-270` (inline `ExecStart = pkgs.writeShellScript \"tb-link-heal\"`) |\n| service | `:113-120` | `:221-273` |\n| timer | `:121-128` | `:274-281` |\n| `boot.kernelModules = [\"thunderbolt-net\"]` | `:110` | `:220` |\n| peer address | `peer = \"10.99.0.2\"` binding at `:49` | hardcoded `10.99.0.1` at `:236`, `:241` |\n| NHI PCI list | `nhiDevices = [\"0000:c4:00.5\" \"0000:c4:00.6\"]` at `:45-48` | hardcoded twice, `:245` and `:249` |\n| tripwire | `myTripwire.tb-fleet-reachability`, `:131-156` | **none — the loud tripwire is coordinator-side only** (`hosts/worker/default.nix:219`) |\n\n**There is no shared module and no option.** The two heal scripts are near-identical text; the coordinator's uses `let`-bound `peer`/`nhiDevices`, the worker's inlines the constants. The worker's version drops the coordinator's `echo` for the rate-limited branch's wording (\"holding\" vs \"holding (replug or peer boot may still land)\") — otherwise byte-equivalent logic.\n\n### 6.2 The escalation ladder (identical on both) **[S]** — `hosts/coordinator/tb-fleet.nix:60-104`\n\n1. `ping -c 1 -W 3 <peer>` succeeds → `rm -f \"$STATE/pd-reset-stamp\"`, `exit 0`.\n2. XDomain peer present (`ls /sys/bus/thunderbolt/devices/ | grep -qE '^[0-9]+-[1-9]'`) → `nmcli connection up tb-fleet || true`.\n3. Retimers present but no peer (`grep -qE '^[0-9]+-[0-9]+:'`) → unbind + `sleep 2` + rebind both NHIs on `/sys/bus/pci/drivers/thunderbolt`.\n4. No retimers at all (the PD-blind signature) → **rate-limited to one shot per 1800 s** via `stat -c %Y \"$STATE/pd-reset-stamp\"`: `framework_tool --pd-reset 2`, `sleep 5`, unbind/rebind `USBC000:00` on `ucsi_acpi`, `touch` the stamp.\n\n`StateDirectory = \"tb-link-heal\"` on both (`:118`, `hosts/worker/default.nix:271`) — that is what makes `/var/lib/tb-link-heal` exist.\n\n### 6.3 Cadences **[S]**\n\n| unit | OnBootSec | OnUnitActiveSec | Accuracy | file:line |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `tb-link-heal.timer` (both hosts) | 2min | **2min** | 30s | `tb-fleet.nix:124-126`, `worker/default.nix:277-279` |\n| `fleet-mtu.timer` (inert) | 1min | 2min | 30s | `lowlat-cluster.nix:170-172` |\n\n| tripwire | interval | onBoot | threshold/cmp | sustain | refractory | file:line |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `tb-fleet-reachability` | 300 s (5 min) | 5min | 1 / ge | **900 s (~15 min)** | 21600 (6 h) | `tb-fleet.nix:133-139` |\n| `eth-fleet-reachability` | 900 s (15 min) | 15min | 1 / ge | **3600 s (~1 h)** | 43200 (12 h) | `eth-fleet.nix:88-94` |\n| `fleet-latency` | 900 s (15 min) | 10min | 200 / ge | **3600 s (~1 h)** | 43200 (12 h) | `lowlat-cluster.nix:185-191` |\n\nSensors: TB pings `10.99.0.2` (`tb-fleet.nix:143`), eth pings `10.99.1.2` (`eth-fleet.nix:98`) — both report `1` when dark. The TB tripwire deliberately keeps pinging the /30, not the fleet IP (`eth-fleet.nix:36-39`: \"it watches the FAST rail specifically\").\n\n**[M] Live coordinator timers, all three present and cycling:**\n```\nFri 2026-08-28 22:10:57 CEST 1min 35s  … tb-link-heal.timer                    → tb-link-heal.service\nFri 2026-08-28 22:11:53 CEST 2min 31s  … tripwire-tb-fleet-reachability.timer  → tripwire-tb-fleet-reachability.service\nFri 2026-08-28 22:17:04 CEST 7min      … tripwire-eth-fleet-reachability.timer → tripwire-eth-fleet-reachability.service\nFri 2026-08-28 22:21:09 CEST 11min     … tripwire-fleet-latency.timer          → tripwire-fleet-latency.service\n```\nAll four services `inactive dead` (oneshots between runs); no failure markers observed. Worker: `systemctl is-active tb-link-heal` → `inactive` (correct for a timer-driven oneshot), `fleet-identity` → `active`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Contradictions with the older campaign record\n\n### 7.1 ⚠ \"deploy-rs dials the worker at 10.99.0.2 over Thunderbolt at flake.nix:536 guarded by asserts at 1194/1227\" — **THIS IS STILL THE CURRENT STATE. It was NOT replaced by #240.**\n\nThis is the most important correction in the dossier. The brief described it as the PRE-#240 state and asked me to confirm it is gone. **It is not gone.**\n\n**[S] `flake.nix:536`, current committed bytes:**\n\n[129 more lines in file. Use offset=558 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988693129},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, read dotfiles-observed.md sections 1.1–1.4 (the surface of the latency hold option, the PM QoS holder, the tripwire), since fn-preflight needs a \"latency hold device read at both ends.\" Also check the lowlat-cluster.nix details, and for host/rdma/ab-protocol.md + attended-bringup.md that fn-env's comment references. After that, the style references: scripts/stage-weights.sh, scripts/run-smoke.sh, scripts/receipts-verify.py, tests/test_repo_invariants.py. Let me read several of them in parallel.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_2a88cd3b866c4fcbb6afaf4a","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":115,"offset":59,"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0c9507ad61ad4dfa94deb8c1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/stage-weights.sh"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_118bfb2eabd14740b68034a3","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-smoke.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5138,"output":353,"cacheRead":23168,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":132,"totalTokens":28659,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988693134,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a6bed8f2-2f7d-452e-8fac-79344e8fb0fc","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_2a88cd3b866c4fcbb6afaf4a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## 1. `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix` — full option surface\n\n**Landed exactly where expected: `/home/tom/mecattaf/dotfiles/modules/lowlat-cluster.nix`, 218 lines** (`wc -l`) **[M]**. Created whole by `b37dea0b`; never edited since.\n\n### 1.1 Option surface (`options.myLowLatCluster`, lines 78–142) **[S]**\n\n| option | line | type | default | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `enable` | 79 | `mkEnableOption` | **false** | \"PM QoS + MTU tuning for the coordinator↔worker fleet rails\" |\n| `peer` | 81–84 | `str` | *(no default — required)* | \"The peer's fast-rail /30 address, watched by the latency tripwire.\" |\n| `latencyBudgetMicros` | 86–95 | `int` | **200** | trip point in µs; doc says it \"sits well above the measured 58-90 us held figure and well below the 577 us unheld one\" |\n| `jumbo` | 97–112 | `bool` | **false** | \"DEFAULT OFF, DELIBERATELY\" — MTU is a both-ends contract |\n| `jumboInterfaces` | 114–141 | `listOf (submodule { name : str; mtu : int; })` | `[{thunderbolt0,65520} {thunderbolt1,65520} {enp191s0,9000}]` | lines 127–139 |\n\nThere is no `enableTripwire`, no `sensorInterval`, no per-rail toggle. Five options total.\n\n### 1.2 The PM QoS holder — it is SHELL, not python **[S]**\n\n`modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:60-64`:\n```nix\npmqosHold = pkgs.writeShellScript \"pmqos-hold\" ''\n  exec 3> /dev/cpu_dma_latency\n  printf '\\0\\0\\0\\0' >&3\n  exec ${pkgs.coreutils}/bin/sleep infinity\n'';\n```\nThe fd-holding mechanism is documented at `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:27-31`: \"A bash `exec 3>` fd is not close-on-exec, so it survives the `exec sleep infinity` that replaces the shell … That is why there is no python3 in this closure, unlike the throwaway transient unit this file replaces.\"\n\nUnit at `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:146-157` **[S]**:\n`systemd.services.lowlat-cluster` · `wantedBy = multi-user.target` · `after = network-pre.target` · `ExecStart = pmqosHold` · `Restart = \"always\"` · `RestartSec = \"5s\"`. No `Type=` (defaults to simple), no hardening stanza.\n\n**[M] Verified live on the coordinator:**\n```\n$ systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster        → active\n$ systemctl show -p MainPID --value lowlat-cluster → 2247685\n$ sudo ls -l /proc/2247685/fd/\n  l-wx------ 1 root root 64 Aug 28 21:51 3 -> /dev/cpu_dma_latency\n$ sudo od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency\n             0\n```\nThe held fd is fd 3 pointing at `/dev/cpu_dma_latency`, and the device reads 0. **[M] worker side: `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster` → `active`.**\n\nUnit path proves the committed source is what runs:\n`/etc/systemd/system/lowlat-cluster.service -> /nix/store/p6xcr9wyjzs8v9xkvksp7yvy2r5iyc79-unit-lowlat-cluster.service`, `ExecStart=/nix/store/qlmkc81x6dlnsivs49baxwz4sdypzk7c-pmqos-hold` **[M]**.\n\n### 1.3 The MTU stanza — implemented but OFF **[S]**\n\n- Script `mtuScript`, `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:66-75`: `PATH=…iproute2`, then per interface `if [ -e /sys/class/net/<i> ]; then ip link set dev <i> mtu <n> 2>/dev/null || true; fi`. Idempotent, silent about down rails.\n- `systemd.services.fleet-mtu` = `lib.mkIf cfg.jumbo` — `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:160-166`, `Type = \"oneshot\"`.\n- `systemd.timers.fleet-mtu` = `lib.mkIf cfg.jumbo` — `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:167-174`, `OnBootSec = \"1min\"`, `OnUnitActiveSec = \"2min\"`, `AccuracySec = \"30s\"` (timer, not oneshot-at-boot, because \"TB interfaces come and go with the link\").\n\n**What turns it on: `myLowLatCluster.jumbo = true`, and nothing sets it.** `modules/strix.nix:153-160` sets only `enable` and `peer`; lines 157–159 are a comment reading \"jumbo stays off: see the two-step deploy note in the module.\" The module's own doc (`:101-111`) prescribes the flip as a TWO-STEP deploy — switch both boxes, then `ping -M do -s 60000 <peer>` from each end.\n\n**[M] Confirmed OFF at runtime:**\n```\ncoordinator $ systemctl list-units --all 'fleet-mtu*'   → 0 loaded units listed.\ncoordinator $ for i in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1 enp191s0; do cat /sys/class/net/$i/mtu; done\n1500 / 1500 / 1500\nworker      $ thunderbolt0 mtu=1500   enp191s0 mtu=1500\n```\nNo 65520 anywhere, on either box.\n\n### 1.4 The fleet-latency tripwire **[S]** — `modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:183-215`\n\nDeclared through the house `myTripwire` submodule (`modules/tripwire.nix`, options at `:39-140`).\n\n| field | value | line |\n|---|---|---|\n| `description` | \"the fast rail's average RTT stays inside the PM QoS budget\" | 184 |\n| `intervalSeconds` | **900** (poll every 15 min) | 185 |\n| `onBootSec` | `\"10min\"` | 186 |\n| `threshold` | `cfg.latencyBudgetMicros` → **200 µs** | 187 |\n| `comparison` | `\"ge\"` | 188 |\n| `sustainSeconds` | **3600** (must hold ~1 h before firing) | 189 |\n| `rearm` | `0` | 190 |\n| `refractorySeconds` | **43200** (12 h quiet window) | 191 |\n| `valueField` | `RTT_US` | 192 |\n| `sensorPath` | `iputils`, `gawk`, `coreutils` | 193–197 |\n\nSensor, `:201-208`: `ping -q -i 0.01 -c 50 -W 2 <peer>`, tail -1, `awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d rtt 1\\n\", $8 * 1000 }'` (avg field × 1000 → µs). A dark rail deliberately reports **0**, not a huge number — `:198-200`: \"darkness is tb-fleet-reachability's job and double-firing on one cable would only bury the signal that matters here.\"\n\n**Alert path**, `:209-215`: writes a failure marker file — `mkdir -p /var/lib/failure-markers` then `printf … > /var/lib/failure-markers/fleet-latency`, with the recovery text naming `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster` and `sudo od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` on BOTH boxes. Marker surfacing is the fleet-wide `modules/failure-surfacing.nix` plane (drop-in `10-fleet-onfailure.conf` → `OnFailure=failure-notify@%N.service`, observed on the live unit **[M]**).\n\n**[M] Live on the coordinator:**\n```\n$ systemctl list-timers --all | grep fleet-latency\nFri 2026-08-28 22:21:09 CEST 11min  Fri 2026-08-28 22:06:09 CEST 3min 12s ago  tripwire-fleet-latency.timer → tripwire-fleet-latency.service\n$ systemctl list-units --all | grep tripwire-fleet-latency\n  tripwire-fleet-latency.service   loaded inactive dead   the fast rail's average RTT stays inside the PM QoS budget\n```\n15-minute cadence confirmed empirically.\n\n### 1.5 Who imports it, and how the gate works **[S]**\n\n`grep -rn \"lowlat\" --include=*.nix .` returns exactly one import site:\n- `modules/strix.nix:35` — `./lowlat-cluster.nix`\n\nand exactly one enable site:\n- `modules/strix.nix:153-160`:\n  ```nix\n  myLowLatCluster = {\n    enable = true;\n    peer = if config.networking.hostName == \"coordinator\" then \"10.99.0.2\" else \"10.99.0.1\";\n  };\n  ```\n\n`modules/strix.nix` is imported by exactly two hosts:\n- `hosts/coordinator/default.nix:60` — `../../modules/strix.nix`\n- `hosts/worker/default.nix:65` — `../../modules/strix.nix`\n\n**The gate is twofold, exactly as the module header states (`modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:33-36`): (a) the option defaults OFF, (b) the sole importer is `modules/strix.nix`, which only coordinator and worker import. The zenbook/NAS cannot see the option at all.** The peer selection is a `networking.hostName` conditional, not a per-host `enable` — so the both-ends invariant is structural, per `modules/strix.nix:147-152`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Deploy / SSH addressing — as committed\n\n[513 more lines in file. Use offset=174 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988702458},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0c9507ad61ad4dfa94deb8c1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Stage the workload checkpoint from the NAS library onto this node's NVMe.\n# Runs on either twin; source path differs per host (worker mounts the\n# library at /mnt/library). Idempotent: rsync skips complete files.\n# Writes a receipt to the path given as $1 (default: stdout summary only).\n#\n# Doctrine: weights arrive Library -> node, never from the hub at runtime.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nARTIFACT=\"flashnext-fp8\"\nUPSTREAM_DIR=\"qwen38-flash-next-fp8\"   # library directory name (upstream-derived)\nDEST=\"/var/lib/local-models/${ARTIFACT}\"\nRECEIPT=\"${1:-}\"\n\ncase \"$(hostname)\" in\n  coordinator) SRC=\"/mnt/nas/models/weights/${UPSTREAM_DIR}\" ;;\n  worker)      SRC=\"/mnt/library/weights/${UPSTREAM_DIR}\" ;;\n  *) echo \"stage-weights: unknown host $(hostname)\" >&2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\n[ -d \"$SRC\" ] || { echo \"stage-weights: library source $SRC absent\" >&2; exit 2; }\n\nexpected_shards=$(ls \"$SRC\" | grep -c 'safetensors$' || true)\necho \"stage-weights: $SRC -> $DEST (${expected_shards} shards visible at source)\"\n\nsudo mkdir -p \"$DEST\"\nsudo chown \"$(id -un):$(id -gn)\" \"$DEST\"\nrsync -a --partial --inplace --info=progress2 \"$SRC/\" \"$DEST/\"\n\n# Local integrity manifest: sha256 of every file, plus count and byte totals.\nlocal_count=$(find \"$DEST\" -type f ! -name '*.sha256' | wc -l)\nlocal_shards=$(ls \"$DEST\" | grep -c 'safetensors$' || true)\nlocal_bytes=$(du -sb \"$DEST\" | cut -f1)\nsrc_bytes=$(du -sb \"$SRC\" | cut -f1)\n\n( cd \"$DEST\" && find . -type f ! -name '*.sha256' -print0 \\\n    | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum ) > \"$DEST/MANIFEST.sha256\"\n\nstatus=\"pass\"\n# The full release is 131 shards; source and destination byte totals must agree.\n[ \"$local_shards\" -ge 131 ] || status=\"fail\"\n[ \"$local_bytes\" -ge \"$src_bytes\" ] || status=\"fail\"\n\nif [ -n \"$RECEIPT\" ]; then\n  python3 - \"$RECEIPT\" \"$status\" \"$local_shards\" \"$local_bytes\" <<'PY'\nimport json, sys, time\npath, status, shards, bytes_ = sys.argv[1:5]\njson.dump({\"step\": \"weights-\" + __import__(\"socket\").gethostname(),\n           \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": {\"shards\": int(shards), \"bytes\": int(bytes_)}},\n          open(path, \"w\"), indent=1)\nPY\nfi\necho \"stage-weights: $status (${local_shards} shards, ${local_bytes} bytes)\"\n[ \"$status\" = \"pass\" ]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988702458},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_118bfb2eabd14740b68034a3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# In-container smoke: platform identity, fp8 storage+cast, model registry\n# resolution, and oracle admission — the cheap questions that must be\n# answered before any weight byte moves. Writes results/receipts/smoke.json.\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nIMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nOUT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/smoke.json\"\n\npodman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n  --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host \\\n  -v \"$REPO_ROOT/results:/results\" \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, time, traceback\nr = {\"step\": \"smoke\", \"status\": \"pass\", \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"), \"data\": {}}\ndef try_(name, fn):\n    try:\n        r[\"data\"][name] = fn()\n    except Exception as e:\n        r[\"data\"][name] = f\"FAIL: {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n        r[\"status\"] = \"fail\"\n        traceback.print_exc()\n\ndef arch():\n    from vllm.platforms import rocm\n    return {\"gcn_arch\": rocm._GCN_ARCH, \"on_cdna\": rocm.on_cdna(),\n            \"supports_fp8_stock_predicate\": rocm.on_cdna() or rocm.on_rdna4()}\ndef fp8_cast():\n    import torch\n    x = torch.zeros(4, dtype=torch.float8_e4m3fn, device=\"cuda\")\n    y = (x.to(torch.bfloat16) + 1).sum().item()\n    return {\"e4m3fn_alloc_and_cast\": y == 4.0}\ndef registry():\n    from vllm.model_executor.models.registry import ModelRegistry\n    archs = ModelRegistry.get_supported_archs()\n    return {\"model_arch_registered\": \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" in archs}\ndef aperture():\n    return {\"ttm_pages_limit\": int(open(\n        \"/sys/module/ttm/parameters/pages_limit\").read().strip())}\ndef admission():\n    # The patched oracle must admit the block-FP8 scheme on this GPU.\n    from vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe.experts.triton_moe import TritonExperts\n    from vllm.model_executor.layers.quantization.utils.quant_utils import (\n        kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym)\n    ok, why = TritonExperts._supports_quant_scheme(\n        kFp8Static128BlockSym, kFp8Dynamic128Sym) \\\n        if hasattr(TritonExperts, \"_supports_quant_scheme\") else (False, \"no hook\")\n    return {\"fp8_block_moe_admitted\": bool(ok), \"reason\": str(why)}\n\ntry_(\"arch\", arch)\ntry_(\"fp8_cast\", fp8_cast)\ntry_(\"registry\", registry)\ntry_(\"aperture\", aperture)\ntry_(\"admission\", admission)\njson.dump(r, open(\"/results/receipts/smoke.json\", \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(json.dumps(r, indent=1))\nPY\necho \"smoke receipt: $OUT\"\npython3 -c \"import json,sys; sys.exit(0 if json.load(open('$OUT'))['status']=='pass' else 1)\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988702458},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, I'll read receipts-verify.py to understand the receipt schema (tp2.json, residency.json, fidelity.json, context.json). I'll also read tests/test_repo_invariants.py. Furthermore, I'll read host/rdma/ab-protocol.md, which is referenced in the NCCL_IB_DISABLE comment. I'll also read the worklist silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json to check the definitions of the receipts-verify gate and the repo-tests gate.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_df13d529116e4c279e1343a0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/receipts-verify.py"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_2bfd10798fc447a798a9fb17","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_repo_invariants.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":14288,"output":222,"cacheRead":18432,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":97,"totalTokens":32942,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988702463,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ec4a138b-602e-4451-ad78-d973d3d4d748","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_df13d529116e4c279e1343a0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate every overnight receipt under results/receipts/.\n\nA receipt is one JSON object: {\"step\": str, \"status\": \"pass\"|\"fail\",\n\"ts\": str, \"data\": {...}}. Missing receipts are legal (the step has not\nrun); a present receipt must be well-formed, and hard bounds are enforced\nper step. Any violation exits 2 — this is the ladder gate that makes the\novernight claims graded rather than narrated.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nRECEIPTS = ROOT / \"results\" / \"receipts\"\n\n# Per-step hard bounds: step-name prefix -> checker(data) -> error or None.\ndef _residency(d):\n    for rank, gib in (d.get(\"gtt_gib_per_rank\") or {}).items():\n        if gib > 80:\n            return f\"rank {rank} GTT {gib} GiB exceeds the 80 GiB bound\"\n    if d.get(\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\", 0) != 0:\n        return \"table has GPU-resident bytes; the mmap path is not engaged\"\n    if not d.get(\"read_after_warmed_decode\", False):\n        return \"residency was not read after a warmed decode\"\n    return None\n\ndef _tp2(d):\n    if not d.get(\"byte_identical_repeat\", False):\n        return \"greedy repeat was not byte-identical\"\n    return None\n\ndef _weights(d):\n    if d.get(\"shards\", 0) < 131:\n        return f\"only {d.get('shards')} shards staged\"\n    return None\n\ndef _bench(d):\n    if d.get(\"loads_per_arm\", 0) < 3:\n        return \"fewer than 3 loads per arm\"\n    if not d.get(\"counterbalanced\", False):\n        return \"arms were not counterbalanced\"\n    return None\n\ndef _context(d):\n    ratio = d.get(\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\")\n    if ratio is not None and ratio < 0.9:\n        return f\"full-context decode ratio {ratio} below the 0.9 bound\"\n    return None\n\ndef _smoke(d):\n    ap = (d.get(\"aperture\") or {}).get(\"ttm_pages_limit\")\n    if ap is not None and ap != 33554432:\n        return f\"aperture {ap} diverges from the 33554432-page ceiling\"\n    return None\n\nBOUNDS = {\n    \"residency\": _residency,\n    \"tp2\": _tp2,\n    \"weights\": _weights,\n    \"bench\": _bench,\n    \"context\": _context,\n    \"smoke\": _smoke,\n}\n\ndef _handoff_patch_ok() -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"When the catalog patch exists it must parse as a git patch.\"\"\"\n    import subprocess\n    patch = ROOT / \"handoff\" / \"catalog-row.patch\"\n    if not patch.is_file():\n        return None\n    proc = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"apply\", \"--stat\", str(patch)],\n                          capture_output=True, text=True)\n    if proc.returncode != 0:\n        return f\"handoff/catalog-row.patch does not parse: {proc.stderr.strip()}\"\n    return None\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    bad = 0\n    err = _handoff_patch_ok()\n    if err:\n        print(f\"receipts-verify: {err}\")\n        bad += 1\n    if not RECEIPTS.is_dir():\n        print(\"receipts-verify: no receipts directory; nothing ran\")\n        return 2 if bad else 0\n    seen = 0\n    for path in sorted(RECEIPTS.glob(\"*.json\")):\n        seen += 1\n        try:\n            r = json.loads(path.read_text())\n        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: invalid JSON: {e}\")\n            bad += 1\n            continue\n        for key in (\"step\", \"status\", \"ts\"):\n            if key not in r:\n                print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: missing '{key}'\")\n                bad += 1\n        if r.get(\"status\") == \"fail\":\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: step '{r.get('step')}' FAILED\")\n            bad += 1\n        for prefix, check in BOUNDS.items():\n            if str(r.get(\"step\", \"\")).startswith(prefix):\n                err = check(r.get(\"data\") or {})\n                if err:\n                    print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: {err}\")\n                    bad += 1\n    print(f\"receipts-verify: {seen} receipts checked, {bad} violations\")\n    return 2 if bad else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988709335},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_2bfd10798fc447a798a9fb17","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"\"\"Repo packaging invariants — the offline half of the discipline.\n\nModeled on ds4-vllm's test_patchset_packaging.py (Apache-2.0), extended:\nnew-file line counts are audited too (the upstream suite skipped them and\n3 of its 12 MANIFEST counts drifted).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\nclass RepoInvariants(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_license_is_apache2(self):\n        text = (ROOT / \"LICENSE\").read_text()\n        self.assertIn(\"Apache License\", text)\n        self.assertIn(\"Version 2.0\", text)\n\n    def test_no_gpl_text_in_tree(self):\n        needle = \"GNU GENERAL \" + \"PUBLIC LICENSE\"  # avoid self-match\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            # Evidence dossiers may QUOTE license headers they inspected.\n            if \"evidence\" in path.parts or path.name == \"test_repo_invariants.py\":\n                continue\n            if path.suffix in {\".safetensors\", \".gguf\"}:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            self.assertNotIn(needle, text, f\"GPL text found in {path}\")\n\n    def test_no_secret_material(self):\n        patterns = [re.compile(p) for p in\n                    (r\"BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY\", r\"hf_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\",\n                     r\"ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\", r\"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\")]\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            for pat in patterns:\n                self.assertIsNone(pat.search(text),\n                                  f\"secret-like material in {path}: {pat.pattern}\")\n\n    def test_spec_artifacts_exist(self):\n        self.assertTrue((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/spec.md\").is_file())\n        trace = json.loads((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/trace.json\").read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(trace[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(\n            (ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/contracts/trace.schema.json\").is_file())\n\n    def test_evidence_dossiers_present(self):\n        dossiers = list((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/evidence\").glob(\"*.md\"))\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(len(dossiers), 7)\n\n    def test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene(self):\n        wl_path = ROOT / \"silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"\n        wl = json.loads(wl_path.read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(wl[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(wl[\"tasks\"])\n        raw = wl_path.read_text()\n        # Runtime hub downloads are forbidden estate-wide.\n        self.assertNotIn(\"-hf \", raw)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"hf download\", raw)\n        # Model-family names never enter worklist bytes (lint L16 mirror).\n        for banned in (\"qwen\", \"llama\", \"deepseek\", \"claude\", \"opus\"):\n            self.assertNotIn(banned, raw.lower(),\n                             f\"banned token '{banned}' in worklist bytes\")\n        # Every implementation task carries executable acceptance.\n        for task in wl[\"tasks\"]:\n            if task.get(\"kind\") == \"implementation\":\n                self.assertTrue(task.get(\"acceptanceCriteria\"),\n                                f\"{task['id']} has no acceptance criteria\")\n\n    def test_scripts_are_syntax_clean(self):\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"host\").rglob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for py in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.py\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"python3\", \"-m\", \"py_compile\", str(py)],\n                           check=True)\n\n    def test_receipts_verify_runs_clean(self):\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [\"python3\", str(ROOT / \"scripts/receipts-verify.py\")],\n            capture_output=True, text=True)\n        self.assertIn(proc.returncode, (0,),\n                      f\"receipts-verify failed:\\n{proc.stdout}{proc.stderr}\")\n\n    def test_notices_cover_the_import_manifest(self):\n        notices = (ROOT / \"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\").read_text()\n        for needle in (\"vllm-project/vllm\", \"AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm\",\n                       \"amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes\", \"nix-strix-halo\"):\n            self.assertIn(needle, notices)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988709335},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Note: test_repo_invariants.py's test_scripts_are_syntax_clean runs bash -n against host/**/*.sh — meaning my new scripts must pass bash -n. Also, test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene forbids banned tokens within worklist bytes — not my concern. Note: test_worklist forbids banned tokens in the worklist itself, but my files are not the worklist. However, note: \"model family names must not be included in the spec or worklist bytes\" — my host scripts could reference the model directory /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, but this is fine (it's the artifact name, not the family name).\n\nNext, I'll read host/rdma/ab-protocol.md (referenced from fn-env comments), check the worklist, and read handoff/OVERSEER-LAUNCH.md for context. I might also check the container script (fn-cluster-up probably launches podman containers — \"podman on both nodes (workers via ssh over the wire), distributed head on coordinator and worker join\"). Let me check the container image name and the build method — container/build.sh and scripts/build-engine.sh. fn-cluster-up must launch containers. Let me check what the existing scripts do with podman. I'll search for podman references in the repository.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0974542dc82f45e997a1f221","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && grep -rn \"podman\\|IMAGE\\|flashnext:dev\" scripts/ container/build.sh container/Containerfile | head -40; echo ====; ls container/rootfs/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_98e8a8a3d92a4376882df60f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/rdma/ab-protocol.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5671,"output":436,"cacheRead":29568,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":269,"totalTokens":35675,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988709340,"responseId":"chatcmpl-83076d68-dc37-41f7-819b-5c4dac7442f5","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0974542dc82f45e997a1f221","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"scripts/run-smoke.sh:7:IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nscripts/run-smoke.sh:10:podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\nscripts/run-smoke.sh:12:  -v \"$REPO_ROOT/results:/results\" \"$IMAGE\" python3 - <<'PY'\ncontainer/build.sh:2:# Container engine build — flashnext:dev (the ruling P4 container lane).\ncontainer/build.sh:21:#   FN_IMAGE=name:tag                    image tag (default flashnext:dev).\ncontainer/build.sh:24:#   podman run --rm -it --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\ncontainer/build.sh:26:#     -v \"$FN_VLLM_SRC:/opt/vllm\" flashnext:dev bash\ncontainer/build.sh:33:IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\ncontainer/build.sh:43:TORCH=\"\" TRITON=\"\" FORK_COMMIT=\"\" IMAGE_DIGEST=\"\"\ncontainer/build.sh:52:    FN_DIGEST=\"$IMAGE_DIGEST\" FN_IMAGE_NAME=\"$IMAGE\" python3 - \"$OUT\" <<'PY'\ncontainer/build.sh:60:        \"image\": os.environ[\"FN_IMAGE_NAME\"],\ncontainer/build.sh:91:BUILD_ARGS=(--tag \"$IMAGE\" --file container/Containerfile\ncontainer/build.sh:99:echo \"==> podman build $IMAGE\"\ncontainer/build.sh:100:podman build \"${BUILD_ARGS[@]}\" \"$REPO_ROOT\"\ncontainer/build.sh:103:BUILD_INFO=\"$(podman run --rm --entrypoint /usr/bin/env \"$IMAGE\" \\\ncontainer/build.sh:110:IMAGE_DIGEST=\"$(podman inspect --format '{{.Digest}}' \"$IMAGE\" 2>/dev/null || true)\"\ncontainer/build.sh:111:if [ -z \"$IMAGE_DIGEST\" ]; then\ncontainer/build.sh:113:    IMAGE_DIGEST=\"$(podman inspect --format '{{.Id}}' \"$IMAGE\")\"\ncontainer/build.sh:118:echo \"==> flashnext:dev built; receipt: $OUT\"\ncontainer/Containerfile:1:# flashnext:dev — the engine fork built from source at the exactly pinned wheel set\ncontainer/Containerfile:130:    \"image\": \"flashnext:dev\",\n====\nfn_expert_union.py\nfn_offload_batch.py\nfn_synctrace.py\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988719816},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_98e8a8a3d92a4376882df60f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# RDMA adoption gate -- counterbalanced socket-vs-verbs A/B\n\nRDMA does not get adopted because it built and passed the verify gate in\n`attended-bringup.md`. It gets adopted only if it wins a counterbalanced\ncomparison against the socket transport, with byte-identical outputs, run\nafter the verify gate passes. Until this protocol produces that result,\nsockets on both rails remain the transport of record on both ranks\n(spec.md P7). This file is that protocol, and where its result gets recorded.\n\nThis A/B is downstream of `attended-bringup.md` Gate 0: it presupposes a\ncommitted TP=2-over-TCP benchmark already exists under `results/` (the\ncomparison needs a baseline to compare against regardless), and it only runs\nafter that document's steps 1-10 have gotten both nodes to a passing verify\ngate.\n\n## Why counterbalanced, and why now specifically\n\nThe one existing data point for this pair's transport delta -- wkljohn's A/B\n-- gives RDMA **+3.4% decode** over TCP. That number is real, but two things\nabout this pair's own measured record narrow how much of it to expect here:\n\n1. **A meaningful share of the wider community's published RDMA-vs-TCP\n   deltas were measured against TCP running with unheld C-states** -- a\n   577us-RTT socket path being compared against a tuned RDMA path, which\n   overstates RDMA's advantage because the comparison's TCP arm was crippled,\n   not because RDMA itself is that much faster. This pair's own measured\n   record found exactly that defect locally (577us RTT with C3 idle enabled,\n   63-90us with `cpu_dma_latency=0` held) before it was fixed.\n2. **This pair's TCP arm, in this A/B, is the held one.** `attended-bringup.md`\n   step 2 item 3 makes re-confirming the C-state/MTU hold post-reboot a hard\n   precondition specifically so this comparison is not the crippled-TCP\n   version of itself. Our TCP is held at **~77us on both ends** (the measured\n   range is 63-90us average RTT depending on direction; ~77us is the figure\n   to plan around), not 577us. Expect the **honest** delta against that held\n   baseline -- which may be smaller than +3.4%, and\n   per this pair's own report, might be close to zero: the same record notes\n   `strix-rdma`'s own author reports the NHI path \"effectively identical to\n   TCP v3\" and ships TCP in production on his own rig, and that on this\n   specific pair the 5GbE control wire already beats the 40Gb/s Thunderbolt\n   rail on average latency. Go in expecting a real but small number, not a\n   repeat of +3.4%.\n\n## XDomain wedge discipline -- hard rules before any verbs traffic\n\nAdopted 2026-08-29 from the strix-rdma recon (post-reboot chapter record,\n`~/post-reboot-latest-2.md` §4): RDMA DMA TX toward a peer whose RX ring is\nnot open does not error out -- it stalls on zero end-to-end credits and can\nwedge the entire XDomain, **including TCP on the same cable**. Recovery is\nreboot-only. Therefore, whenever the verbs arm is live:\n\n1. **Out-of-band TCP barrier before first RDMA transmit.** Both sides\n   confirm, over the ethernet wire (`enp191s0`, never the rail itself), that\n   the peer's RX ring is open before either side sends its first RDMA\n   packet. No barrier, no transmit.\n2. **Never take both sides' rings down simultaneously.** One side stays up\n   until the other is confirmed down and quiescent.\n3. **Worker-first teardown.** The worker's rings come down first; the\n   coordinator follows only after confirming the worker is quiesced -- the\n   mirror image of the worker-first reboot rule in `attended-bringup.md`.\n\n**Exact-name hazard (verified in-source, `kernel/ibdev.c:2277-2290`):** both\nibverbs devices advertise rail 0's GID -- the driver registers one global\n`roce_netdev` for every rail, so `usb4_rdma5`'s GID table looks identical to\n`usb4_rdma0`'s. `NCCL_IB_HCA` must therefore be the **exact string**\n`usb4_rdma0` -- a prefix match (`usb4_rdma`) or selecting `usb4_rdma5`\nsilently routes RDMA onto the wrong wire with no error. Both devices existing\non both nodes is by design (fixed-stride naming, one lane per TB domain);\ntheir presence is not the hazard -- ambiguous selection is.\n\n## Protocol\n\n- **Depths:** reuse the main bench's depth series -- `0`, `10240`, `102400`\n  -- rather than inventing a separate one. A transport delta that only shows\n  up at one depth and not the others is itself a finding, not noise to\n  average away.\n- **Loads per arm:** 3.\n- **Order:** counterbalanced, A-B-B-A per depth (A = socket, B = verbs) --\n  not A-A-A-B-B-B. Thermal drift, cache warmth, and background load on a\n  shared pair all trend across a session; A-B-B-A cancels a linear trend in\n  a way a blocked order does not.\n- **What's held constant across both arms:** everything except the transport\n  env delta from `attended-bringup.md` step 11. Same weights, same context\n  depth, same speculative-decode setting, same batch shape, same node roles.\n  Swap only `NCCL_IB_DISABLE` / `NCCL_IB_HCA` / `NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX` between\n  arms; nothing else in the shared cluster env changes.\n- **Correctness check, every run:** capture a token fingerprint per load, both\n  arms. A transport swap that changes output tokens is not a performance\n  question anymore -- treat any fingerprint mismatch as a hard stop, file it,\n  and do not report a performance number until it's resolved. RDMA winning on\n  latency with a fingerprint mismatch is not a win.\n- **Metric:** median decode tokens/s per arm per depth (3 loads -> take the\n  median, not the mean, to blunt one bad load).\n\n## Adoption rule\n\nAdopt RDMA as the transport of record **only if**:\n1. Verbs beats sockets on decode at a majority of the three depths, and\n2. every fingerprint matches across both arms at every depth.\n\nOtherwise, sockets stay the transport of record on both rails, exactly as\nbefore this session -- a small or negative delta is a valid, useful result,\nnot a failed bring-up. The RDMA package stays staged and rebuildable for the\nnext attended session either way; nothing about a \"no\" result here requires\ntearing anything down beyond the normal rollback in `attended-bringup.md`.\n\n## Recording the result\n\nLand the three-depth, two-arm result under `results/`, in the same receipt\nshape the rest of the campaign's bench matrix uses (medians, fingerprints,\ndepth series -- see spec.md's bench-matrix ruling). Whichever way the\nadoption rule falls, write the receipt: a documented \"RDMA measured, not\nadopted, delta was +N%\" is exactly the kind of result the morning ledger is\nfor, and saves the next person from re-running this A/B from scratch.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988719816},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright. Next, I'll check the worklist for details of the host-tooling task, the OVERSEER-LAUNCH.md handoff for orchestration context, and check container/build.sh and the Containerfile to understand how to run the containers (mounts, devices) so that fn-cluster-up correctly starts the containers. I'll also check the existing files under host/ (rdma-related). Furthermore, I'll check the contents of tests/test_instruments.py to match the test style. Let me check the host-tooling entry in the worklist and its gate wiring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c3f79eb219b543bea0574d27","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwl = json.load(open(\"silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"))\nfor t in wl[\"tasks\"]:\n    print(t[\"id\"], \"|\", t.get(\"kind\"), \"|\", t.get(\"title\"))\nprint(\"---gates---\")\nfor g in wl.get(\"gates\", []):\n    print(g.get(\"id\"), g.get(\"run\") or g.get(\"argv\") or \"\")\nPY"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0ed6a538ddcb4be1acbd445f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/handoff/OVERSEER-LAUNCH.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3104,"output":344,"cacheRead":34816,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":117,"totalTokens":38264,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988719820,"responseId":"chatcmpl-d0df294b-bbf3-46c0-9455-e6c7eb214fea","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_c3f79eb219b543bea0574d27","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"container-recipe | implementation | Container recipe: pinned wheel set + engine fork built from source\ninstruments | implementation | Engagement-proof instruments adapted into the container overlay\nhost-tooling | implementation | Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\nbench-harness | implementation | Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix\nproxy-tooling | implementation | Proxy checkpoint builder and single-node first-light runner\nmorning-ledger | implementation | Render the morning ledger and the announcement draft\ncatalog-handoff | implementation | Prepare the fleet catalog patch for the morning operator\nrdma-package | implementation | Integrate the operator-attended RDMA package under host/rdma\ncp-weights | checkpoint | Checkpoint: stage the workload checkpoint onto both nodes\ncp-build | checkpoint | Checkpoint: the container engine build\ncp-smoke | checkpoint | Checkpoint: in-container smoke — identity, fp8 cast, admission\ncp-proxy | checkpoint | Checkpoint: proxy checkpoint first light, single node\ncp-tp2 | checkpoint | Checkpoint: pair first light, residency, fidelity, full context\ncp-bench | checkpoint | Checkpoint: the counterbalanced bench matrix\ncp-close | checkpoint | Checkpoint: full verification pass over everything the night produced\n---gates---\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988728868},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0ed6a538ddcb4be1acbd445f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Overseer launch prompt — paste this to the fresh Fable session\n\n*Durable copy of the launch text (so it survives outside any chat). The\noverseer's full operating manual is `handoff/OVERSEER.md`; this is the\none-paragraph mission to open the session with.*\n\n---\n\nYou are the overnight overseer for **flashnext** — the campaign that puts\nQwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8 at TP=2 on the coordinator/worker Strix Halo pair by\nmorning. You run in `~/mecattaf/flashnext` on the coordinator. **Read\n`handoff/OVERSEER.md` first and follow it — it is your operating manual**\n(state-at-handoff, the dependency spine, engine subtleties, red lines,\ntimeline). Then read `handoff/ROUTING.md` — the compute-routing doctrine and\nescalation ladder you enforce — then the **\"Final outcome\" section of\n`handoff/PREARM-REBOOT.md`** (what the pre-arm bake actually landed),\n`docs/GATE0.md`, and the first-hardware checklist at the bottom of\n`patches/MANIFEST.md`.\n\nYour mission ends in exactly one of two states: (a) the model serving at TP=2\nwith counterbalanced benchmarks committed under `results/`, or (b) a typed\nblocker in `docs/MORNING.md` naming the literally-unresolvable cause, with\ndrafted upstream issue text under `handoff/upstream-issues/`. \"Couldn't\nachieve X so skipped it\" and \"waiting for Y\" are forbidden outcomes —\ndiagnose, steer, fix, escalate, in that order. The spec\n(`specs/flashnext/spec.md`, ratified) and worklist govern; expect minimal\nsteering to be needed, but verify rather than assume.\n\n**Your first act, after preflight sanity, is to ARM the campaign yourself:**\n\n```\ncd /home/tom/mecattaf/flashnext\ngit pull\ntally campaign arm mecattaf/flashnext silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\n```\n\nThen immediately post the host-tooling pre-dispatch steer from\n`handoff/ROUTING.md` (the pre-dispatch re-read folds it into the lane's first\nbrief) and start the container base pull + wheel prefetch.\n\nState you inherit (2026-08-29 pre-arm session; the run now launches in the\nDAY, unsupervised by the operator — same rules, no reboots, no exceptions):\nall five worklist gates green (9/9 unit tests, spec-lint, flake check, 12/12\nfork verify, receipts verify); spec ratified at `a066074`; pi confirmed on\nqwencloud qwen3.8-max (`pi auth check qwen-token-plan` exit 0); rails\nverified (rail 0 loss-free, RTT band 96–112 µs — flagged not blocking).\n**Weights staging is COMPLETE on both nodes**: 131 shards / 185,563,801,379\nbytes each, verified pass, receipts committed at\n`results/receipts/weights-{coordinator,worker}.json`. The `cp-weights`\ncheckpoint will fast-path over the staged copies (the script is idempotent)\nand re-verify — expect it to be quick.\n\nNCCL discipline, doubly load-bearing since the pre-arm bake: ibverbs devices\nnow EXIST on both nodes (`usb4_rdma0` + `usb4_rdma5`, both by design), so\n**`NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` is unconditional** — verify `host/fn-env.sh` carries it\nafter the host-tooling lane runs, and steer if not. Morning hazard for\nwhoever runs the attended RDMA A/B (not you): both verbs devices advertise\nrail 0's GID, so `NCCL_IB_HCA` must be the EXACT string `usb4_rdma0`, and the\nXDomain wedge discipline in `host/rdma/ab-protocol.md` applies before the\nfirst verbs transmit.\n\nAuthorities you hold: `tally campaign status`/`steer`/`resume` on the armed\ncampaign; ultracode workflows for any engineering the lanes can't carry (the\nfork at `mecattaf/vllm@flashnext` is yours to extend, with `patches/` mirror\ndiscipline enforced by `scripts/verify-fork.sh`); **tally.nix problems become\nGitHub issues on `mecattaf/tally.nix`** (index them in\n`handoff/TALLY-FINDINGS.md`), and if a tally defect is fatal to tonight's\nbuild you may patch tally.nix itself, minimally, issue-referenced. Never:\nreboot either node, touch RDMA transport (sockets are the transport of\nrecord), write under `specs/flashnext/`, run garbage collection, `nix flake\nupdate nix-strix-halo`, or bump/deploy the tally pin while the campaign is\narmed.\n\nMinute zero: the AMD ROCm skills are already vendored in the fleet\n(`rocm-doctor`, `magpie-kernel-evaluator`, `tracelens-analysis-orchestrator`\nin `~/.claude/skills` via dotfiles — no `npx` install needed; use them for\nbring-up diagnosis and the benchmark deliverable). Start the container base\npull + wheel prefetch, and watch the weights staging checkpoint. The operator\nwakes to `docs/MORNING.md`.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988728868},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright. Next, I'll check the worklist's acceptance criteria for host-tooling (to make sure they match what's written in the brief). Also, I'll check container/build.sh and Containerfile to grasp the container's runtime usage. Furthermore, I'll check the gates inside the worklist — in the output, the gate's id was displayed as empty. Let me check the worklist's gate structure and the host-tooling task details.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a63843fda63d45718f6135a2","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwl = json.load(open(\"silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"))\nprint(json.dumps({k: v for k, v in wl.items() if k != \"tasks\"}, indent=1)[:2000])\nfor t in wl[\"tasks\"]:\n    if t[\"id\"] == \"host-tooling\":\n        print(json.dumps(t, indent=1)[:4000])\nPY"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1869,"output":252,"cacheRead":37888,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":91,"totalTokens":40009,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988728873,"responseId":"chatcmpl-47eafccb-d097-488c-a38e-fea2cf56b732","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a63843fda63d45718f6135a2","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n \"campaign\": {\n  \"name\": \"flashnext\",\n  \"maxTasks\": 16,\n  \"maxParallel\": 1,\n  \"steward\": \"narrator\",\n  \"agent\": {\n   \"adapter\": \"pi\",\n   \"approvalPolicy\": null,\n   \"sandboxPolicy\": null,\n   \"diagnosisSandboxPolicy\": null,\n   \"runtimeMaxSec\": 14400\n  },\n  \"gates\": [\n   {\n    \"kind\": \"command\",\n    \"id\": \"repo-tests\",\n    \"preflightArgv\": [\n     \"sh\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"command -v python3 >/dev/null\"\n    ],\n    \"argv\": [\n     \"python3\",\n     \"-m\",\n     \"unittest\",\n     \"discover\",\n     \"-s\",\n     \"tests\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"kind\": \"command\",\n    \"id\": \"spec-lint\",\n    \"preflightArgv\": [\n     \"sh\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"test -d /home/tom/mecattaf/tally.nix\"\n    ],\n    \"argv\": [\n     \"bash\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"nix develop /home/tom/mecattaf/tally.nix --command cargo run -q -p spec-lint -- specs/flashnext --worklist silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json --root . || [ $? -eq 1 ]\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"kind\": \"command\",\n    \"id\": \"flake-eval\",\n    \"preflightArgv\": [\n     \"sh\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"command -v nix >/dev/null\"\n    ],\n    \"argv\": [\n     \"nix\",\n     \"flake\",\n     \"check\",\n     \"--no-build\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"kind\": \"command\",\n    \"id\": \"fork-verify\",\n    \"preflightArgv\": [\n     \"sh\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"command -v git >/dev/null\"\n    ],\n    \"argv\": [\n     \"bash\",\n     \"scripts/verify-fork.sh\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"kind\": \"command\",\n    \"id\": \"receipts-verify\",\n    \"preflightArgv\": [\n     \"sh\",\n     \"-euc\",\n     \"command -v python3 >/dev/null\"\n    ],\n    \"argv\": [\n     \"python3\",\n     \"scripts/receipts-verify.py\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n}\n{\n \"id\": \"host-tooling\",\n \"kind\": \"implementation\",\n \"title\": \"Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\",\n \"goal\": \"Write the host/ tooling and scripts/run-tp2.sh per spec claims 4.2, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5 and 6.1 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4, #r6), composing the disciplines in specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md section 5 and specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md section 5. host/fn-env.sh: PYTHONHASHSEED=0; PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85; HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1; inductor and triton cache dirs under the state directory, never tmpfs; NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME set ONLY to the Thunderbolt rails that carry a routable /30 IP (rail 0 thunderbolt0/10.99.0.x is guaranteed; rail 1 thunderbolt1 is trained-but-IP-unconfigured today per dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix and MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP \\u2014 a peerless link-local rail in the list hangs RCCL bootstrap; the env file computes this from `ip -br addr` at write time and logs which rails it chose, rather than hardcoding both); NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY (an ibverbs device now exists on the rails from the pre-arm bake; without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA \\u2014 sockets are the transport of record until the attended morning A/B, evidence host/rdma/ab-protocol.md); VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_; VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1; a loud comment forbidding exports of engine defaults read through is-set probes. host/fn-cluster-up.sh: podman on both nodes (worker over ssh on the wire), distributed head on the coordinator and worker join, worker python pool capped small, a hard two-GPU gate before serve, then the serve process at tensor-parallel size 2 with eager execution, model directory /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, served name flashnext; reap stranded serve processes before start and gate on zero residue. host/fn-cluster-down.sh: idempotent teardown both nodes. host/fn-preflight.sh: byte-diff of both ranks' FN_ env, latency-hold device read on both ends, per-rail link-speed record, round-trip probe on both rails. host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service: oneshot, RemainAfterExit, ExecStopPost teardown, no install section. scripts/run-tp2.sh: preflight, up, two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared, residency read after 50 warmed decode tokens (GTT per rank from sysfs plus process RSS plus table page-cache residency), fidelity capture (fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits under results/), then a 262144-context probe, writing receipts tp2.json, residency.json, fidelity.json, context.json per the receipts-verify.py schema. Extend tests/test_repo_invariants.py-adjacent coverage with tests/test_host_tooling.py asserting the env file carries the doctrine lines and unit files carry ExecStopPost and no WantedBy install. Boundaries: touch only host/, scripts/run-tp2.sh, and tests/.\",\n \"deliveredBehaviors\": [\n  \"One command stands the pair service; one command tears it down; failure cannot strand ranks\",\n  \"First light produces the four receipts the ladder grades\"\n ],\n \"readFirst\": {\n  \"specSections\": [\n   \"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r4\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md\"\n  ],\n  \"styleReferences\": [\n   \"scripts/stage-weights.sh\",\n   \"scripts/run-smoke.sh\"\n  ]\n },\n \"acceptanceCriteria\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"host-doctrine\",\n   \"description\": \"Env doctrine present, units teardown-safe, all scripts syntax-clean, host tests pass.\",\n   \"argv\": [\n    \"bash\",\n    \"-euc\",\n    \"for f in host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh; do bash -n \\\"$f\\\"; done && grep -q 'PYTHONHASHSEED=0' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'thunderbolt0' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'ExecStopPost' host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service && ! grep\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988735813},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All inputs are in place. Note: the worklist itself contains the model family name \"Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8\" in OVERSEER-LAUNCH.md — this is fine, it's a handoff document, and the forbidden-name rule applies only to spec/worklist byte sequences. My scripts should use the artifact name flashnext-fp8 and the served name flashnext, and must never use the family name. Also, test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene only scans the worklist byte sequences.\n\nWait — an important detail: `test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene` scans the raw worklist byte sequences for banned tokens. My files are not the worklist. But note: does the worklist goal byte sequence contain \"qwen\"? The worklist says \"full naming lives in README.md and the evidence\" — no problem.\n\nNow, about the repo-invariants test `test_scripts_are_syntax_clean` — this runs bash -n against all of host/**/*.sh, including host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh (existing). My new files must pass bash -n.\n\nAlso note: run-smoke.sh's model registry check references \"Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration\" — hmm, that's in scripts/run-smoke.sh, which is not a worklist. Fine.\n\nNow let me plan the deliverables:\n\n1. **host/fn-env.sh** — the canonical env file, sourced from the ray head, ray worker, and vllm serve. What's needed:\n   - `export PYTHONHASHSEED=0`\n   - `export PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85`\n   - `export HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1`\n   - Inductor + triton cache dirs under the state directory, never under tmpfs. Something like FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"; TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor; TRITON_CACHE_DIR=$FN_STATE_DIR/triton. mkdir -p.\n   - NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME computed from `ip -br addr`: only thunderbolt rails with a routable /30 IP (neither link-local 169.254.x nor absent). Rail 0 thunderbolt0 10.99.0.x is guaranteed; thunderbolt1 must not be listed unless it has a peer IP. At write time compute and log which rails were chosen.\n   \n   \"At write time compute\" — hmm, fn-env.sh is sourced at bringup. The phrasing \"the env file computes this from `ip -br addr` at write time and logs which rails it chose rather than hardcoding both\" — I'll interpret it this way: at env load time (bringup, i.e. the moment just before the file's content is written to RCCL — at ray start / serve start), the file computes the rail list. \"Write time\" = the moment the env var is written/exported (at source time). Grepping for 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' and 'thunderbolt0' in fn-env.sh must succeed — thunderbolt0 must appear in the file (as the guaranteed rail / candidate). So fn-env.sh has a function that walks candidate rails (thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1), checks that `ip -br addr show dev $rail` has a global IPv4 address that is not 169.254, and joins them with comma. Log chosen rails to stderr/journal. If zero rails chosen → hard fail (loud refusal). thunderbolt0 is guaranteed — but still computed; can be asserted as a fallback.\n\n   - `export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` unconditional, with a loud comment referring to host/rdma/ab-protocol.md.\n   - `export VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_`\n   - `export VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1`\n   - Loud comment forbidding exports of engine defaults that are read via the is-set probe (F.9): VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER*.\n   - FN_-prefixed variables for ray propagation. But note: VLLM_* variables also need to propagate to the ray worker... Actually, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_ means ray will propagate FN_* variables to workers. But vLLM's own ray env copy list includes by default a set of prefixes (VLLM, RAY, etc.). Does the ds4 donor use DS4_ prefix because vLLM doesn't propagate custom prefixes? Actually, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY is precisely the mechanism to add extra prefixes. vLLM's defaults already include \"VLLM\", \"RAY\", \"CUDA\", \"ROCM\", \"NCCL\", \"PYTORCH\", etc. So the VLLM_* and NCCL_* exports propagate by default, and FN_* needs the extra prefix. Good — so the FN_ prefix propagation is a correctness requirement.\n   \n   Also from ds4 donor: worker python pool cap: RAY_NUM_CPUS → but this is passed at ray start (--num-cpus). The ds4 env exports `RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}`. Let's do the same: export RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} with comment, used by fn-cluster-up.\n   \n   Maybe also TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC etc. The brief doesn't require them; but \"compose the discipline\" — a modest NCCL socket timeout block is reasonable: TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS? Keep focused; add a few with justification (2400 sec heartbeat for cold kernel cache). Hmm, caution: don't over-engineer. What the brief enumerates is required; additions must be within the write boundary and must not violate F.9. Since model loading takes tens of minutes on cold kernel cache, I'll include a small NCCL timeout block from the donor (TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2400, TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=0, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=2400000).\n\n   Also RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1? On Strix Halo with 1 GPU per node, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0. ray status needs to report 2.0 GPU — 1 GPU per node. Should I export HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0? Donor had it. Hmm, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 with a single device is harmless. But might interfere with KFD device detection... The donor pinned it for multi-GPU selection. On this fleet each node has exactly one GPU (Strix Halo desktop). I'll skip HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES and instead keep the RAY block minimal: RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1 (donor doctrine for ray + rocm visible devices interaction), RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=0 (donor: avoids ray OOM killer killing the worker; on a 128GB UMA box vLLM legitimately uses most of memory). These are documented in the donor manifest and safe.\n\n   FN_-prefixed variables: the FN_ prefix is used in VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_ — so configuration variables should be FN_-prefixed so they propagate. E.g. FN_MODEL_DIR, FN_SERVED_NAME, FN_PORT, FN_MAX_CTX. Define FN_ configuration defaults in fn-env.sh.\n\n2. **host/fn-cluster-up.sh** — full bringup:\n   - Load fn-env.sh.\n   - Worker over ssh on the wire: WORKER_SSH=\"worker\" or fleet identity 10.99.9.2? Steering says worker-side actions use the ethernet wire (10.99.9.2), never ride the 10.99.0.x rails. The dotfiles SSH nickname exists; per §2.1 the fleet IP is 10.99.9.x on lo — stable identity. So FN_WORKER=${FN_WORKER:-10.99.9.2}.\n   - Reap first, gate later: reap stale serve processes on both nodes (bracket grep trick), SIGTERM→SIGKILL, hard-gate on zero residue.\n   - podman on both nodes: start container (name fn-pair? image flashnext:dev) — \"podman on both nodes (worker over ssh on the wire), distributed head on coordinator, worker join\". So: on both nodes `podman run` a long-lived container with /dev/kfd /dev/dri, --ipc=host, network=host, model directory mount, results mount, cache mounts. Name: flashnext-pair (or follow donor: same name on both boxes, e.g. \"flashnext\").\n   - ray start --head on coordinator inside container; ray start --address=... on worker inside container over ssh. Note: ray head address must be reachable from worker over... Ray control traffic rides on what? NCCL sockets ride on rails; ray GCS connects over... The distributed head listens on node IP. Since the container uses network=host, ray head binds to coordinator IP. Worker should join using coordinator's address — over which interface? The tensor rails carry tensors only; wire carries control (steering 5). So ray address should be the coordinator's wire address 10.99.9.1? Or 10.99.1.1 (eth-fleet /30)? Fleet IPs 10.99.9.x are on lo and are the stable identity. Hmm: \"worker-side actions use the ethernet wire (10.99.9.2)\" — so ssh to 10.99.9.2. Ray join: worker must dial coordinator. Use fleet IP: FN_HEAD_IP default 10.99.9.1, worker dials $FN_HEAD_IP:6379. That keeps control on wire/lo identity. NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME on the rails handles the tensor plane. Good.\n   - Worker python pool cap small: ray start --num-cpus=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} on both (head + worker) with --include-dashboard=false on head only (donor: ray panics if passed to worker).\n   - Hard 2-GPU gate before serve: poll `ray status` for \"GPU: 2.0\" (or parse resources), with timeout, abort loudly.\n   - Then serve process: tensor-parallel 2, --enforce-eager, model /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, served name flashnext, --distributed-executor-backend ray. P10 ruling: \"first light runs in that sanctioned mode\" — piecewise capture with mmap op as a split boundary — but the task brief says \"serve process at tensor-parallel size 2 with eager execution\". The claim 4.2 says \"first light eager on both rails\". Serve in eager (--enforce-eager) as the task says; P10's piecewise mode is for the table path's guard... Actually the task goal explicitly says \"eager execution\" and acceptance only checks the tooling. Going with --enforce-eager.\n   - Serve launched under systemd-run --user transient unit? Donor used systemd-run --user --unit. For flashnext-pair.service (oneshot, RemainAfterExit, ExecStart=fn-cluster-up.sh), if cluster-up execs the serve process directly, then the oneshot unit stays \"activating\" until serve returns... Donor pattern: systemd-run --user transient unit supervises podman exec wrapper, main unit returns success. But RemainAfterExit=yes + Type=oneshot means ExecStart must return. Model load takes tens of minutes — TimeoutStartSec=45min like donor, and serve runs in background via systemd-run transient unit (unit name flashnext-serve-manual), then cluster-up polls API readiness (donor: 105×20s ≈ 35 min) and verifies. This matches \"failure cannot leave ranks stranded\" (ExecStopPost teardown) and the donor's structure.\n   - Also gate on API health before declaring success (wait-for-reality pattern §5.1 library-reachable: poll /v1/models or /health via curl on localhost port).\n   - FN_PORT default 8427? Donor used 1234. Pick FN_API_PORT=${FN_API_PORT:-8000}? vllm default is 8000. Let's go with FN_PORT default 8000. Hmm, llama-swap might occupy something. Use 8000, overridable.\n\n3. **host/fn-cluster-down.sh** — idempotent teardown on both nodes: tolerate broken config, reap serve (SIGTERM→SIGKILL), ray stop --force local + ssh, stop/remove podman containers both nodes, exit 0 unconditionally.\n\n4. **host/fn-preflight.sh** —\n   - byte diff of FN_ env across both ranks: compute env on both nodes (source fn-env.sh inside container? or on host?) and diff sorted `env | grep '^FN_'`... but fn-env.sh only exports vars when sourced. Byte-diff the FN_ env across both ranks — means compare env that each rank sees. Simplest robust approach: on each node run a bash that sources fn-env.sh and prints `env | grep '^FN_' | sort`, then diff. But env contains computed values like NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME — those aren't FN_ prefixed. The spec says \"byte diff of FN_ env across both ranks\" — i.e. the FN_ variables. But the critical divergence risk is the full doctrine env (NCCL etc.). The ds4 donor's concern: \"without it, the two TP ranks diverge\". Let's byte-diff the full canonical env block: source fn-env.sh with a clean baseline and dump all exported vars (`bash -c 'set -a; source fn-env.sh; env' | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|VLLM_|RAY_|TORCH|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_)' | sort`), compare. That's closer to intent (rank env agreement). Write both dumps to temp files, diff, record in receipt data. Hmm, preflight writes its own receipt? The brief lists receipts tp2/residency/fidelity/context for run-tp2. Preflight result can be part of the tp2 receipt data (rails_chosen, preflight pass). Have fn-preflight.sh exit non-zero on mismatch, print a summary, and optionally write results/receipts/preflight.json? receipts-verify checks every *.json under results/receipts — the \"preflight\" step has no bounds → any well-formed receipt is fine. Write preflight.json with status pass/fail; on failure run-tp2 stops. Actually if preflight fails, don't write a fail receipt (which would fail receipts-verify later)? The ladder wants failures to be visible... receipts-verify counts status:fail as a violation. For an overnight runner, a failed step writing a fail receipt is the \"typed blocker\" story, but it makes receipts-verify red. Better: on failure preflight exits non-zero without writing a receipt; run-tp2.sh reports and stops. Hmm, but F.12: \"never record an overnight step skipped without a typed blocker\" — that's about recording a step skipped. Simplest honest behavior: preflight always writes a receipt (pass or fail), and exits non-zero on failure. If it fails, the night produces a failing receipt = graded failure, typed by the data. But then receipts-verify exits 2 even if everything else is fixed... Acceptance criteria only require syntax + doctrine + tests pass, and run-tp2 is not executed at gate time. Writing a fail receipt on actual failure is honest and the ladder grades it — consistent with \"receipts are graded, not told\". Going with that.\n   - Latency-hold device reads at both ends: on both nodes read `od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` (needs root; try sudo -n) — expected value 0 means held. Also `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster`. Record both; if not held (value != 0) warn/fail. U.2 says latency hold must be armed; the tp2 receipt records held round-trips within budget at both ends. Preflight records device reads, round-trip probe measures RTT vs 200µs budget.\n   - Per-rail link speed record: read /sys/class/net/thunderboltN/speed (Mb/s) on both nodes.\n   - Round-trip probe on both rails: ping peer over each rail that has IP. Rail 0 peer is 10.99.0.2/10.99.0.1; rail 1 thunderbolt1 has no IP today, so probe is \"skipped — no peer IP\" (recorded). Round-trip via wire too? Task says \"round-trip probe on both rails\" — rails only. Rail with routable IP: ping -c N -W ... parse avg RTT µs; assert within budget 200µs? U.2 says tp2 receipt records held round-trips within budget at both ends — \"both ends\" means both nodes' latency holds, read at both ends. RTT budget: latencyBudgetMicros=200. Record rtt_us and budget; only fail on rail 0's RTT if above budget? Measured band is 96–112 µs, flagged as not blocking. Set threshold to 200 µs per lowlat doctrine. Rail 1 without peer IP: recorded as unprobed with reason.\n\n5. **host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service** — oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, ExecStart=%h/.../host/fn-cluster-up.sh? The unit is a repo artifact; per donor the installed path is %h/ds4-cluster-restart.sh. For flashnext: ExecStart=%h/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh? Donor assumed the scripts lived at $HOME. Our repo is at /home/tom/mecattaf/flashnext. Use a repo-root-relative %h path? systemd specifiers: %h = user home. The donor unit used %h/ds4-cluster-restart.sh (scripts at home). On flashnext the repo is at ~/mecattaf/flashnext. Should I write ExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh? Hmm, that hardcodes the checkout location. Alternative: document that the unit is linked/copied with adjustment. The test asserts only ExecStopPost and no WantedBy. Acceptance greps 'ExecStopPost' and absence of 'WantedBy'. Follow donor: ExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh, ExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh, ExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh, with a comment explaining why StopPost (ExecStart failures don't run ExecStop). No [Install] section, with comment. Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, TimeoutStartSec=45min, TimeoutStopSec=5min. Wait — user unit or system unit? Donor's was user? `systemd.services.ds4-vllm.service` and %h... %h works in user units (user home). Actually in system units %h is /root. Donor's ds4-vllm.service was presumably a system unit running as the user via User=? The manifest doesn't say; ssh/podman as user tom suggests user-level. I'll write it to work with `systemctl --user link`, and document that. Use %h specifier.\n\n6. **scripts/run-tp2.sh** — first-light runner:\n   - preflight (host/fn-preflight.sh) → abort on failure.\n   - up (host/fn-cluster-up.sh) → wait for serve ready.\n   - Two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared: fixed prompt, temperature 0 (greedy), max_tokens=300, run twice against /v1/completions with served model name flashnext; byte-compare the completion text; record in tp2.json with byte_identical_repeat=true (bound in receipts-verify _tp2!), plus per-rail link speeds (from preflight), rails chosen, latency held at both ends (U.2), and preflight env diff pass.\n   - Residency after 50 warmed decode tokens: issue a request that generates ≥50 tokens (after a warmup request), then read per-rank GTT from sysfs + process RSS + table page-cache residency. GTT from sysfs: amdgpu GTT usage... sysfs per rank: each rank is a process on each node. GTT from sysfs — hmm, per-node /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used? Strix Halo is an APU; patches mention VRAM reporting / unified memory. \"Per-rank GTT from sysfs\" — probably read /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_vram_used? The claim says GTT. amdgpu sysfs has mem_info_gtt_used? Actually amdgpu doesn't expose GTT directly... It does: `mem_info_gtt_total`/`mem_info_gtt_used` exists on newer kernels? There are mem_info_vram_used/total, mem_info_vis_vram, mem_info_gtt_total/used (added around 6.x?). On Strix Halo with amdgpu... fork patches 0004/0011 mention VRAM reporting on unified memory. Implement: for each node, read /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used if present, else compute from kfd? Keep it generic: try mem_info_gtt_used; record raw number; convert to GiB. Process RSS: sum RSS of vllm serve + ray workers? Per-rank = per-node serve process tree. Walk /proc via pgrep or ps for RSS total. Table page-cache residency: fincore of model files? Table = engram table files in the model dir (which file? ple/engram table...). Without knowing exact file name... The checkpoint directory /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8 contains the table as some file. Page-cache residency = count cached pages of the table file(s) × page size. Use `mincore` via python (fn can run python3 on host, or inside container). Since this is a scripted runner to be executed overnight with real hardware, I need to be practical: implement best-effort measurement:\n     - GTT per rank from sysfs (mem_info_gtt_used, fallback 0 with flag)\n     - Per-rank RSS (ps)\n     - Table page-cache residency: linux `fincore` util (from util-linux) against files matching the table pattern (e.g. *ple* / *engram* / *.table*?), or via cgroup? Simplest: fincore every file in model dir and sum pages of files that look like the table? But which file is the table? The ds4 story: 51.2B-param hash-lookup embedding... In ds4-vllm it was a big mmap file. Table files — in Qwen3.8-Flash-Next... no name available. The container rootfs has fn_offload_batch.py etc. Hmm. For the mmap path: \"table bytes GPU-resident is zero\" is the key bound: data.table_gpu_resident_bytes == 0. How to measure? If the mmap path is engaged, no table bytes GPU-resident — assert from absence of table allocation on GPU: read GTT/VRAM usage and compare to expected? Practical approach: compute table_gpu_resident_bytes as 0 if the mmap guard is engaged — but that's telling, not measuring.\n     \n     Let me think about what's measurable: the engine's mmap path page-cache-faults the table from disk. GPU residency of table bytes would show up in... no direct probe. Honest proxy measurement: page-cache residency of table file (pages cached × 4096) shows table is served from page cache; GPU-resident table bytes: assert 0 by fincore + accounting of weights loaded to GPU: total model bytes minus table bytes ≈ expected GPU allocation. Too speculative.\n     \n     Practical script design: python helper embedded in run-tp2.sh measures per node:\n       - gtt_bytes: /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used (or gttd), fallback None → record null and don't fail on GTT bound? receipts-verify bound: gtt_gib_per_rank per rank > 80 fails; if key missing, loop over {} — passes. But honesty: record what we can.\n       - rss: sum RSS of serve process tree.\n       - table page-cache residency: fincore against files matching the table glob (FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*.ple *.engram *table*\"?) — hmm. Actually let me reconsider: fork patches: 0009 \"Model-Relocate-ple_emmap_to_the_platform-neutral-comm...\" and 0010 \"ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t...\". PLE = the table. Let me look at those patches to find table file naming and how residency can be probed. Also fn_offload_batch.py / fn_expert_union.py / fn_synctrace.py — the instruments — might reveal hooks.\n\n   Let me read the patches and instruments before writing residency measurement.\n\n   - Fidelity capture: fixed-prompt loss and frontier logits under results/ — need engine support for loss/logprobs: /v1/completions with logprobs, and \"loss\" requires a scoring endpoint (vllm's score?). Frontier logits = logits of final token? Capture: for a fixed prompt set, request completion with logprobs=5, save JSON to results/fidelity-*.json, record sha256 fingerprint. Fidelity receipt data: file list + hashes.\n   - 262144-context probe: build a long prompt (~262144 tokens?) — that's enormous; a probe that sends a prompt and requires --max-model-len >= 262144. Serve must set --max-model-len 262144 (or bigger). Donor used max ctx 524288. FN_MAX_CTX default 262144? The context receipt records decode within 10% of short-context figure: measure decode tok/s on short request and long-context request, ratio ≥ 0.9. Probe: fill prompt to ~262144 tokens via repeated fixed text, then decode a small number of tokens, measure decode rate; compare with short-context decode rate.\n   - Receipts: tp2.json, residency.json, fidelity.json, context.json with schema step/status/ts/data.\n\n7. **tests/test_host_tooling.py** — assert that fn-env.sh contains doctrine lines (PYTHONHASHSEED=0, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF line, HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1, NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME computed not hardcoded — assert absence of `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1` hardcode? Task says \"assert that env file carries doctrine lines and unit files carry ExecStopPost and no WantedBy install\"). Additional: bash -n against every host/*.sh and scripts/run-tp2.sh (duplication with repo-invariants, fine); assert fn-env.sh forbids probe-default exports (no VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM export, etc.); assert cluster-up contains reap-before-gate ordering? Keep to spec: doctrine lines in env + ExecStopPost present + no WantedBy + no [Install].\n\nNow, about the computed NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME: fn-env.sh computes at source time. But note: it should be computed per node — each node sources fn-env.sh locally on that node (ray worker sources on worker). Both nodes currently have only thunderbolt0 with IP, so both compute thunderbolt0. Log the choice: echo to stderr.\n\nAlso, fn-env.sh must be idempotent and overridable via FN_ variables (donor uses ${DS4_X:-default} pattern).\n\nLet me now check patches 0009/0010 and instruments for table naming and residency probes.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me examine the PLE/mmap patches and the instruments to understand table naming and residency probes:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dc9a87745359490f8bcff141","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && head -80 patches/0009-Model-Relocate-ple_emmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch 2>/dev/null || ls patches/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_39d074d483544e60b013a530","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && grep -n \"ple\\|PLE\\|engram\\|table\" patches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3743,"output":6412,"cacheRead":38016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6183,"totalTokens":48171,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988735818,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a3910c46-49e2-4073-a568-c23974e4d993","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_dc9a87745359490f8bcff141","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0001-flashnext-pin-build-system-torch-to-2.11.0-for-the-g.patch\n0002-flashnext-add-tools-check_nix_substrate.py-packaging.patch\n0003-ROCm-detection-via-KFD-topology-when-amdsmi-fails.patch\n0004-Detect-AMD-APU-fix-VRAM-reporting-for-unified-memory.patch\n0005-Disable-skinny-GEMM-on-gfx1151.patch\n0006-Fix-Wave32-LDS-overflow-in-top-k-merge-launch.patch\n0007-fork-kill-switch-and-loud-logging-for-the-APU-memory.patch\n0008-ROCm-Enable-block-FP8-fused-MoE-on-gfx1151-via-in-ke.patch\n0009-Model-Relocate-ple_mmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch\n0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch\n0011-fork-route-AMD-APU-memory-into-MemorySnapshot-on-the.patch\n0012-ROCm-Apply-the-52970-fp8-dot-upcast-to-the-block-sca.patch\nMANIFEST.md\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988883070},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_39d074d483544e60b013a530","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2:From: work-ple <work-ple@flashnext.local>\n4:Subject: [PATCH 10/12] [ROCm] Wire VLLM_PLE_MMAP and the FP8 PLE stack into\n7:Before this, on a ROCm build VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 was a silent no-op: the amd/\n8:tree never imported ple_mmap, the custom op was never registered, and the\n13:amd/ple_layer.py\n15:  W2  'from ..common import ple_mmap' (module-scope, so the op registers\n19:      ple_mmap.enabled() -> check_cudagraph_safety + validate_shards_for +\n23:      VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 (the default) is the kill switch.\n30:      load-bearing: ple_mmap's op body probes for it to decide whether\n31:      layer_name resolves to a PLE layer.\n33:      ple_mmap.set_weight_scale (the placeholder has no such Parameter for\n38:Blocker A -- the FP8 PLE stack, ported at full parity from nvidia/:\n39:  Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod, _get_ple_embedding_quant_method,\n40:  Qwen4ExpPLELayer._get_embedding_weight_scale / _dequantize_embeddings,\n43:  _dequantize_embeddings call in the PLE forward before key_proj/value_proj.\n46:  copy_ple_embedding_shard_ bare-casts FP8 -> bf16, doubling the bytes and\n54:  which equals params_dtype for an unquantized table.\n61:amd/model.py: get_current_vllm_config import, the ple_mmap import, and the\n62:two 5-line build_tables blocks after load_weights. amd/model.py is now\n65:tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py: new, no GPU and no ROCm\n66:runtime required. Pins that both trees reach the same ple_mmap module\n71:raise when an FP8 table has no scale).\n78: tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py | 352 ++++++++++++++++++++\n80: vllm/models/qwen4_exp/amd/ple_layer.py      | 254 +++++++++++++-\n81: vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py    |  11 +-\n83: create mode 100644 tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py\n85:diff --git a/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py b/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py\n89:+++ b/tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_ple_mmap_amd.py\n93:+\"\"\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP and the FP8 PLE stack on the ROCm (amd/) model tree.\n96:+the ``amd/`` tree is imported instead, ``ple_mmap`` was never imported, the\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988883070},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The table is the \"PLE\" (Positional Lookup Embedding?) table — mmap the shard of the file. Let's search for the filename pattern of the table shard — search for \"shard\" in ple_mmap in patch 0009, e.g., the \".ple\" extension or a build_tables call, etc.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dc98901751624c9e94b73d91","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5