cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988890917,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f5041f46-c05a-460d-acc6-1bbd51084413","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_24813b1159da490ca82f9078","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"patches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:39:  Qwen4ExpPLEFp8EmbeddingMethod, _get_ple_embedding_quant_method,\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:46:  copy_ple_embedding_shard_ bare-casts FP8 -> bf16, doubling the bytes and\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:62:two 5-line build_tables blocks after load_weights. amd/model.py is now\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:194:+        \"model.layers.1.ple.ple_embedding\",\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:211:+# the custom op is registered exactly once and build_tables walks one\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:222:+def test_amd_ple_embedding_exposes_the_name_the_mmap_op_probes_for() -> None:\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:223:+    \"\"\"ple_mmap's op body resolves layer_name -> ple_embedding and refuses\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:246:+    # validate_shards_for tolerates (it logs and defers to build_tables) —\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:346:+    # placeholder now refuses to serve zeros until build_tables attaches one.\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:410:+    layer.ple_embedding = module\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:425:+    layer.ple_embedding = module\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:438:+    layer.ple_embedding = SimpleNamespace(ngram_embedding=SimpleNamespace())\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:471:+            ple_mmap.build_tables(\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:485:+            ple_mmap.build_tables(\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:542: from ..common.ple import copy_ple_embedding_shard_\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:595:+def _get_ple_embedding_quant_method(\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:672:+                quant_method=_get_ple_embedding_quant_method(\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:810:+                    # calls load_weights at all — build_tables must attach\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:822:                 copy_ple_embedding_shard_(\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:836:             f\"{prefix}.ple_embedding\",\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:842:             int(config.ple_embed_dim),\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:848:+        embedding = getattr(self.ple_embedding, \"ngram_embedding\", None)\npatches/0010-ROCm-Wire-VLLM_PLE_MMAP-and-the-FP8-PLE-stack-into-t.patch:873:         embeddings = self.ple_embedding(input_ids, query_start_loc, ngram_context)\npatches/0009-Model-Relocate-ple_mmap-to-the-platform-neutral-comm.patch:91: from ..common.ple import copy_ple_embedding_shard_\n===\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_expert_union.py:143:            topk_ids = res[1] if isinstance(res, tuple) and len(res) >= 2 else None\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_offload_batch.py:25:refcounting: ``TieringOffloadingManager.complete_store`` pins a block via\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_offload_batch.py:27:``primary.complete_read()`` as soon as the secondary-tier write lands. Blocks\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_offload_batch.py:89:    offloaded_block_sizes: \"list[int] | tuple[int, ...]\",\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_synctrace.py:14:`with_stack=True` on the torch profiler answers that in principle and is\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988898110},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The exact filename of the table shard can't be derived from the patch. The mmap table is provided from a checkpoint file, and the shard is probably part of a safetensors file or a dedicated .ple file. For measuring \"table page-cache residency,\" a practical and honest approach is to use `fincore` (or Linux's mincore) to measure the page-cache residency of the model files that the mmap table backs. Since we can't identify the exact file, we can measure the page-cache residency of *all* files within the model directory whose names match the PLE/table glob, falling back to a configurable FN_TABLE_GLOB. Actually—better idea: since the table is mmap'd from checkpoint shards, and residency questions are about *pages of the table that are resident in the page cache*, measure it for files matching a configurable pattern, defaulting to `*.ple` plus `*ple*`? Hmm, there's a risk of nonsense results.\n\nAn alternative honest approach: measure the total of cached pages for files matching FN_TABLE_GLOB (default \"*ple*\", case-insensitive) inside the artifact directory on each node. If nothing matches, record null/0 with a \"matched_no_files\" flag. Since the residency-verification upper bound only checks table_gpu_resident_bytes == 0, read_after_warmed_decode, and gtt_gib ≤ 80, the page-cache field is recorded for the morning operator, and the hard constraint lies elsewhere.\n\nRegarding table_gpu_resident_bytes: the mmap path means zero bytes of the table reside on the GPU. A measurable honest value: if VLLM_PLE_MMAP is enabled (which we enforce) and serve starts up (build_tables attached), then the table is provided via page-cache faults → GPU-resident table bytes is 0. But \"measured, not told\": a real probe would be to compare the GPU/GTT footprint against the table size. Too fragile. A middle-ground: compute table_gpu_resident_bytes = max(0, measured_gpu_alloc - expected_non_table_bytes)? No, don't know the expected value.\n\nHmm—what the donor's AGENTS.md/manifest says... in ds4 the check was probably via the log line \"ple_mmap engaged\". Honest probe: vLLM logs at startup whether the mmap path was selected (build_tables logs). We can grep the serve log for the ple_mmap engagement marker and record residency = 0 only if the marker is present and VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1. Otherwise record null → receipts-verify fails (data.get(...,0) != 0 → null is... `d.get(\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\", 0) != 0` — null != 0 → fail. Good, honest: if we can't prove it, the receipt fails).\n\nPlan: table_gpu_resident_bytes = 0 if (env VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1) and (the serve log contains ple_mmap's engagement marker, e.g., a \"ple_mmap\" line or \"build_tables\"). Otherwise null (fail the gate loudly). Grep the log for case-insensitive \"ple_mmap\". This is a real probe of the execution's own log, not a story.\n\nNow the other measurements:\n\n- GTT per rank from sysfs: /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used — does it exist? Not sure. Try candidates: mem_info_gtt_used, and as a fallback read gtt size via /sys/module/amdgpu/parameters? For APUs, patches 0004/0011 route APU memory into MemorySnapshot. Read what's available, record raw values, and set the flag accordingly. Compute GiB. If unavailable, record null—but then the upper bound for gtt_gib_per_rank is skipped (empty / missing). Hmm, receipts-verify loops d.get(\"gtt_gib_per_rank\") or {} — if rank keys are missing, no check. Acceptable but weak. Alternative: measure the GTT approximation as process RSS? No. Let's be practical: try sysfs files in order: mem_info_gtt_used, mem_info_vram_used (on APUs VRAM==GTT-like unified memory). Patch 0004 \"Fix VRAM reporting for unified memory\" suggests mem_info_vram_* is meaningful. Try gtt first, fall back to vram, and record which one was used.\n\n- Process RSS per rank: sum RSS from ps for the serve process tree (vllm serve + ray workers) on each node.\n\nNow the big question: how much of run-tp2.sh is executed inside the container vs. outside? vllm serve runs inside a container on the coordinator, exposed via network=host to FN_PORT. The client request just curls localhost:FN_PORT—easy with python3's urllib (no external deps). Per-node measurements are done via ssh to the worker + locally.\n\nNow let's look at what serve requires: the model directory is mounted into the container. Container runtime: podman run with name, -d, network=host, --ipc=host, devices, volumes: model directory (ro), state directory for cache (rw), results directory. Then run `podman exec` for ray/vllm.\n\nStructure of fn-cluster-up.sh (adapted from ds4-cluster-restart.sh):\n\n```\nset -euo pipefail\nROOT=...\nsource host/fn-env.sh   # exports FN_* + doctrine\nFN_WORKER_HOST=${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}  # wire, never rail\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n```\n\nReap function (local + remote): pkill -f \"vllm serve\" inside container? Serve runs inside container via podman exec. The stranded husks that the donor reaped were *host* processes (podman exec wrapper + conmon leftovers?). Actually the donor reaped host processes matching `bin/[v]llm serve deepseek`—podman exec's process tree is visible from host /proc. Same applies here: reap host processes matching `[v]llm serve` (bracket trick), SIGTERM, wait 3s, SIGKILL, gate residual==0.\n\nOn both nodes: first reap (worker via ssh), gate zero residue, then containers, then ray, then serve.\n\nContainers: name FN_CONTAINER=${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}. For idempotency: podman rm -f existing, then podman run -d ... sleep infinity (donor used long-lived containers via restart scripts). Image FN_IMAGE default flashnext:dev. Mounts:\n- -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro\n- -v $FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR (cache)\n- Mount results? The serve log needs to be visible from host for ple_mmap grep. Log to $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log inside a mounted directory.\nDevices: /dev/kfd, /dev/dri, seccomp unconfined, ipc host, network host, --pull=never? Use defaults.\n\nEnv injection into container: pass required vars via -e, or via `--env-file` generated from fn-env.sh's exports. Simplest: at container startup source fn-env.sh and `podman run --env-file <(env | grep ...)` — but the ray worker reads env at ray start (propagated from head via VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY + default prefixes). NCCL/VLLM/PYTORCH vars must exist in the serve process's environment. Also start ray inside the container with fn-env.sh sourced: `podman exec $C bash -lc 'source /host/host/fn-env.sh && ray start ...'`. Mount the repo's host/ directory? Mount the whole repo read-only into the container at /opt/flashnext? That couples the container to the host checkout. But worker also needs scripts — worker's repo copy? Donor ran worker via ssh podman exec without a repo, using only env vars passed at ray start.\n\nA cleaner donor-derived approach: pass env explicitly at ray start and serve launch (not relying on files inside the container):\n- Generate env-file on coordinator: bash -c 'source host/fn-env.sh; env' | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|VLLM_|TORCH|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_|TRITON_)' > /tmp/fn-env.list (tmpfs is fine for transport. Cache directory must not live on tmpfs—the env-file transport is not a cache).\n- podman run --env-file that-file -d ...\n- podman exec inside: env is already in the container's base env. ray start reads it. Serve picks it up. This is simple and correct: doctrine env baked into container env at `run`. But NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is computed on coordinator at file generation time. Worker needs its own computed value... the rail is symmetric on both nodes (rail 0 has IP on both). Still, safer to generate an env file on the worker node (ssh worker 'bash -lc \"...compute...\"' > list) and run the container with --env-file there. fn-env.sh is needed on the worker—via ssh stdin: `ssh worker 'cat > /tmp/fn-env.sh' < host/fn-env.sh` and then ssh worker bash /tmp/fn-env.sh ... env. Good: the script ships env to the wire. That satisfies \"env doctrine computed per node\".\n\nRay head: podman exec -d coordinator-container ray start --head --port 6379 --num-cpus=$RAY_NUM_CPUS --include-dashboard=false. Worker: podman exec worker-container ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:6379 --num-cpus=$RAY_NUM_CPUS. Wait—ray's address: worker connects to coordinator's ray GCS. Which IP does ray head advertise/bind? `ray start --head` binds to the node's IP (auto-detected, probably the default route—the wire). Worker dials $FN_HEAD_IP (fleet lo 10.99.9.1). Route worker → 10.99.9.1 goes via the wire (eth), because the fleet IP is reachable via the ethernet /30 route? §2.1: 10.99.9.x/32 on lo is a stable identity. 10.99.1.0/30 eth-fleet is always-on. Routing between fleet IPs presumably goes via eth-fleet (#240 made eth the control plane). Good—ray control goes on the wire, and the rail stays tensor-only via NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME pin.\n\n2-GPU gate: ray status on the head container: poll `podman exec $C ray status` for \"GPU\" total 2.0, timeout ~120s, otherwise abort loudly (dump ray status).\n\nServe: on coordinator, transient via systemd-run? Simpler and more robust: `podman exec -d` to run vllm serve, writing the log to a mounted state dir. But then the unit's teardown relies on fn-cluster-down's reap. Donor used a systemd-run user unit to monitor the podman exec wrapper, making it easy to stop + track via journal. For our service unit: flashnext-pair.service ExecStart=fn-cluster-up.sh returns after serve becomes ready. Serve itself runs detached (podman exec -d). RemainAfterExit keeps the unit active. ExecStop/StopPost = fn-cluster-down.sh reaps everything. Good—\"failure cannot strand ranks\": if cluster-up fails midway, ExecStopPost runs cluster-down.\n\nserve flags: vllm serve /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8 --served-model-name flashnext --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --enforce-eager --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX --max-num-batched-tokens 512? Donor pinned kv-cache bytes. For flashnext, keep minimal-but-doctrinal: --tensor-parallel-size 2, --distributed-executor-backend ray, --enforce-eager, --max-model-len ${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}. Donor warns \"no comments inside line-continuation commands\"—carry that warning over. Add --gpu-memory-utilization? Donor said to pin kv bytes instead. On unified-memory Strix Halo, default gpu-mem-util 0.9 may grab too much. Donor used 0.83. Add --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}. No kv pin (no validated number on this workload), with a comment that the pin overrides util once validated.\n\nAPI readiness poll: curl /v1/models until flashnext appears, up to FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT (default 2700s = 45min), 20s interval.\n\nThen run-tp2.sh continues: completion.\n\nrun-tp2.sh flow:\n1. bash host/fn-preflight.sh (writes results/receipts/preflight.json. Aborts if non-zero).\n2. bash host/fn-cluster-up.sh (abort on failure).\n3. Two greedy completions: POST /v1/completions {\"model\":\"flashnext\",\"prompt\":FIXED,\"max_tokens\":300,\"temperature\":0}. Compare .choices[0].text bytes. byte_identical_repeat.\n4. Write tp2.json: data {byte_identical_repeat, rails: preflight's rail speed, rails_chosen, rtt_us per rail, latency_hold: {coordinator:0, worker:0}, budget_us:200}.\n5. Warmed decode: warmup request (12 tokens), then a request with ≥50 decode tokens (max_tokens=64). Then residency snapshot: python3 helper does locally + ssh-worker: GTT from sysfs, process RSS (pgrep -f \"vllm serve\" → ps o rss), table page-cache residency via fincore over glob-matched files, ple_mmap engagement from serve log grep → table_gpu_resident_bytes. Write residency.json {gtt_gib_per_rank:{coordinator:x,worker:y}, rss_gib_per_rank, table_page_cache_gib, table_gpu_resident_bytes:0, read_after_warmed_decode:true}.\n6. Fidelity: a few fixed prompts. Capture completions with logprobs (frontier logits = logprobs of final generated token), plus loss via the score endpoint? Does vLLM's OpenAI server have /v1/score? For loss: use completions with prompt and echo + logprobs=1 and compute NLL from returned logprobs—feasible: request with max_tokens=1, logprobs=5, echo=true? Simpler: capture token logprobs on a fixed continuation and compute average NLL = loss. Save JSON files results/fidelity/prompt-N.json, hash them, and receipt fidelity.json {files:[...], hashes, prompt count}.\n7. Context probe: build a long prompt of ~262144 tokens (repeat a fixed sentence. Estimate token count ≈ chars/4. Target 262144*~4 chars ≈ 1MB of text—fine). Measure short-context decode speed and long-context decode speed. ratio = long_decode_tps / short_decode_tps. Write context.json {target_context:262144, short_decode_tps, long_decode_tps, decode_ratio_vs_short_context: ratio}. Receipts-verify constraint: ratio < 0.9 → fail. If probe errors (e.g., prompt too long for server), record fail status honestly.\n\nAll receipts: {\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"}.\n\nClient: python3 with urllib only. Write helper python inline in run-tp2.sh, taking JSON paths etc. The script may run overnight without network, so keep it stdlib-only.\n\nNow tests/test_host_tooling.py:\n\n```python\nclass HostTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    test_fn_env_doctrine_lines: read host/fn-env.sh. assertIn PYTHONHASHSEED=0, PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85, HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1, VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME, thunderbolt0. Assert computed not hardcoded: assertNotIn \"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1\", presence of `ip -br addr` too.\n    test_fn_env_no_probe_default_exports: F.9—assert no lines exporting VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM / VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER.\n    test_unit_stoppost_no_install: flashnext-pair.service contains ExecStopPost, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes. Does not contain WantedBy or [Install].\n    test_host_scripts_syntax_clean: bash -n over host/*.sh + scripts/run-tp2.sh.\n    test_preflight_and_runner_exist + exec bit.\n```\n\nNote: acceptance also requires the glob `host/*.sh` to cover fn-env.sh, fn-cluster-up.sh, fn-cluster-down.sh, fn-preflight.sh. host/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh already exists (syntax clean? repo-invariants tests probably already pass at baseRev, so yes).\n\nWrite boundaries: host/, scripts/run-tp2.sh, tests/ only. All files fit.\n\nCommit message style: let me check git log message style: \"instruments: ...\", \"container-recipe: ...\", \"receipts: weights staged...\". So prefix with task ID: \"host-tooling: Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\". Commit on branch tally-work/flashnext-57f87640bc5b/host-tooling. Don't push.\n\nAnother check: fn-env.sh's NCCL rail computation must mention thunderbolt0 (grep requirement)—candidate list includes thunderbolt0, thunderbolt1. Log which one was chosen.\n\nEnv directory: FN_STATE_DIR default: /var/lib/flashnext? A user-writable directory needed for the cache under the state directory. Use ${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}. Inside the container, HOME is root's home (podman default runs as root in image?). Pass via env file. Cache directory must be on-disk (not tmpfs)—$HOME/.local/state is on-disk on both nodes. Inside the container mount the host directory: -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\". mkdir -p on host first.\n\nAlso HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 default via ${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}? The brief says HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1. Keep plain export with override default.\n\nWorker pool cap: export RAY_NUM_CPUS=${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4} in fn-env.sh, consumed by cluster-up (ray start --num-cpus on both nodes).\n\nNow—does the repo have a container/rootfs/.keep etc? Not relevant.\n\nCheck `tests/test_instruments.py` for the test style (and to confirm tests/ is a package: unittest tests.test_host_tooling — need tests/__init__.py? python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling requires tests to be a package, or at least importable. If tests/ has no __init__.py, python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling — unittest treats \"tests.test_host_tooling\" as a module path. Import via file-path discovery needs the package. Actually `python -m unittest tests.test_foo` works without __init__.py in py3? unittest imports module tests.test_foo—importlib needs tests to be importable as a namespace package—in Python 3, namespace packages work without __init__.py, so with cwd=repo root `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling` works. The repo-tests gate uses discover -s tests, which works either way. Let me check whether __init__.py exists.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me check the tests dir structure and the git log style, then look at the instruments test for conventions:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_e70603e5660546bfad78050c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && ls -la tests/ && head -30 tests/test_instruments.py && git log --format='%s' -8"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1865,"output":4425,"cacheRead":49152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4314,"totalTokens":55442,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988898114,"responseId":"chatcmpl-216e4819-7d48-4f91-a7d1-5809e6e9c407","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e70603e5660546bfad78050c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 24\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:30 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:30 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 7047 Aug 29 09:30 test_instruments.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 4637 Aug 29 09:30 test_repo_invariants.py\n\"\"\"Instrument overlay tests — compile, notices, and budget invariants.\n\nThe offline half for container/rootfs/fn_*.py: py_compile each module (cfile\ninto a tempdir, so no bytecode lands in the overlay), check every adapted\nmodule carries its upstream notice header, and check fn_offload_batch's\nbudget arithmetic against the reference invariants (floor at the max group\nsize; promote counted in blocks). fn_offload_batch is torch-free by design,\nso that module alone is imported and exercised directly; the other two are\nchecked at the text/compile level so this suite runs on a host without\ntorch or vllm.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport importlib.util\nimport os\nimport pathlib\nimport py_compile\nimport subprocess\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nROOTFS = ROOT / \"container\" / \"rootfs\"\n\n# name -> (upstream file the notice must cite, env vars the module must expose)\nMODULES = {\n    \"fn_synctrace.py\": (\"ds4_synctrace.py\", (\"FN_PROFILE\",)),\n    \"fn_offload_batch.py\": (\n        \"ds4_offload_batch.py\",\n        (\"FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\", \"FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\"),\ninstruments: Engagement-proof instruments adapted into the container overlay\ncontainer-recipe: Container recipe: pinned wheel set + engine fork built from source\nhandoff: staging complete, campaign launches attended-by-daylight not overnight\nreceipts: weights staged on both nodes (131 shards, 185563801379 bytes each)\nhandoff: OVERSEER-LAUNCH refresh — routing read order, overseer owns the arm, weights pre-staging state, NCCL discipline\nREADME truth-up: pre-arm bake done, spec ratified, four host-bake findings, transport story, overnight compute routing\nhandoff: compute-routing doctrine (ROUTING.md) — tiers, per-task table, escalation ladder\nspec ratification: flashnext spec.md status -> ratified 2026-08-28\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787988999031},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright. No __init__.py; namespace packages will work. Commit style: \"taskid: Title\".\n\nLet me double-check a few remaining details too:\n\n1. How does the worker get the repo? fn-env.sh gets shipped via ssh stdin — no problem.\n2. `fn-cluster-down.sh` should also stop the systemd-run unit if donor was using one — here serve runs via podman exec -d, so teardown is: kill the serve process on both nodes, ray stop, podman rm -f the containers. Idempotent, always exit 0.\n\n3. For fn-preflight's \"byte-level diff of FN_ env vars on both ranks\": compute per-node env dumps by sourcing fn-env.sh via ssh. But fn-env.sh sources from a repo path — on the worker the repo might not exist at the same path. Ship the file via stdin to both nodes: `bash -s <<EOF`? Cleaner: `ssh worker 'cat > \"$t/fn-env.sh\"' < host/fn-env.sh`. Wire (ssh) delivers it. Then dump: `bash -c 'set -a; source $t/fn-env.sh; env'` filtered on FN_ and doctrine keys, sorted → byte diff. NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is computed identically on both nodes (both have the same rails), so the diff should match. PYTHONHASHSEED=0 is static. Good.\n\nBut wait: fn-env.sh logs the chosen rails on source (to stderr) — capture stdout only for the diff. Log to stderr so the diff stays clean.\n\n4. Latency-hold device read: `sudo -n od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` — requires root access to read /dev/cpu_dma_latency. Use sudo -n and tolerate failure (record \"unreadable\"). Check `systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster` too. Expected value is 0 (held).\n\n5. Per-rail link speed: /sys/class/net/thunderbolt0/speed (Mb/s) on both nodes. Record.\n\n6. Round-trip probe: for each rail with a routable IP: ping peer's rail IP. Rail 0 peer: coordinator→10.99.0.2, worker→10.99.0.1. Determining peer depends on hostname. Run preflight on coordinator (driver) and ssh to worker to run the probe there too (\"on both ends\"). Each node pings its peer on each rail. Record avg rtt µs, budget 200.\n\n7. tp2.json needs U.2 \"record round-trips within budget, held, on both ends\": include the held device value and rtt vs. budget.\n\nNext receipts-verify: preflight step name \"preflight\" — no bound checker, fine.\n\nNow writing files. Attention to detail:\n\nfn-env.sh:\n- `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, set -u? It's sourced — avoid set -e in a sourced file? Donor's env files generally don't set -e. Defensive but source-safe: no set -e (caller owns it). Use set -u? If caller hasn't set -u, referencing unset ${FN_X:-} is fine. Skip set entirely, document.\n- Rail computation:\n\n```bash\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    [ -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ] || { echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent; not listed\" >&2; continue; }\n    # routable /30 = a global IPv4 that is not link-local 169.254/16\n    local addr\n    addr=$(ip -br addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | awk '{for(i=3;i<=NF;i++) print $i}' | cut -d/ -f1 \\\n      | grep -Ev '^$|^169\\.254\\.' | head -n1 || true)\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED ($addr routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  ...\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"$(fn_choose_rails)\"\n[ -n \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ] || { echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no routable Thunderbolt rail; refusing\" >&2; return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1; }\n```\n\ngrep 'thunderbolt0' must hit — it's in the candidate list. Good.\n\nNote: `ip -br addr show dev thunderbolt1` line format: \"thunderbolt1  UP  169.254.x.x/16 ...\" — fields 3+. If no address, fields are just name + flags. Also filter fd00:? IPv4 only: match ^[0-9.]+$. Refine: `grep -E '^[0-9.]+$' | grep -Ev '^169\\.254\\.'`.\n\n- return 1 vs. exit 1 on source failure: use `return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1` trick to work both sourced and executed.\n\nfn-env.sh content plan (mirroring donor structure, commented):\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# flashnext pair-service env doctrine (sourced by ray head, ray worker, vllm serve...)\n# ... header explaining what this is\n# DO-NOT-EXPORT probe defaults comment (F.9) — big and loud.\n```\n\nVariables:\n- FN_STATE_DIR, FN_MODEL_DIR=/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8, FN_SERVED_NAME=flashnext, FN_PORT=8000? Donor used 1234. Let's default FN_PORT=8000. FN_MAX_CTX=262144 (claim 4.5), FN_WORKER_HOST=10.99.9.2, FN_HEAD_IP=10.99.9.1, FN_CONTAINER=flashnext-pair, FN_IMAGE=flashnext:dev, FN_RAY_PORT=6379.\n- Determinism: PYTHONHASHSEED=0 (with donor rationale comment).\n- PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF.\n- HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=1 with comment.\n- Cache dirs: TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR/TRITON_CACHE_DIR under $FN_STATE_DIR, mkdir -p, never on tmpfs.\n- NCCL block: computed NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME + NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 unconditional + timeout block + NCCL_DEBUG=WARN? Keep minimal: TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=2400, TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=0, NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=2400000 (cold kernel cache ~25min rationale).\n- Ray block: VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_, RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1, RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=0, RAY_NUM_CPUS=4 default.\n- VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 with mmap-path comment.\n- VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1, HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1? Runtime hub downloads forbidden (F.6) — HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 enforces. VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1. Good doctrine, from dotfiles vllm renderer's offlineModelEnv. Include.\n\nfn-cluster-up.sh plan:\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Bring the flashnext pair service up: containers on both nodes (wire ssh to\n# worker), ray head+join, 2-GPU gate, then TP=2 eager serve. REAP FIRST, GATE AFTER.\nset -euo pipefail\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n\nlog() { echo \"fn-cluster-up: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# Ship fn-env.sh to worker via wire; write per-node env-file for containers\nship_env() { local dest=\"$1\"; ... }\n\nmake_env_file(): source fn-env.sh in a subshell, filter env, write file\n```\n\nEnv-file generation: `( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"; env ) | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED|HSA_|PYTORCH_|TORCHINDUCTOR|TRITON_|HF_)' | sort > \"$f\"`. On worker: pipe the script via ssh.\n\nReap (host-side, covers podman exec'd processes):\n\n```\nreap_serve() {  # $1 = \"local\" | ssh-prefix...\n  pattern='[v]llm serve'\n  pids=$(pgrep -f \"$pattern\" || true)\n  [ -n \"$pids\" ] && kill -TERM $pids; sleep 3\n  pids=$(pgrep -f \"$pattern\" || true); [ -n ] && kill -KILL\n  residue=$(pgrep -cf \"$pattern\" || true)\n  [ \"$residue\" -eq 0 ] || { log FATAL; exit 1; }\n}\n```\nWait: pgrep -cf prints count; pgrep returns 1 on no match; `|| true`. Residue check: use `pgrep -f \"$pattern\" | wc -l`.\n\nRemote: `worker 'pids=$(pgrep -f \"[v]llm serve\" || true); ...'` — encapsulate as reap_node with local flag.\n\nContainers:\n```\nstart_container() { # node, envfile\n  podman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  podman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --ulimit memlock=-1 --ulimit memlock=-1 ... \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n    --env-file \"$envfile\" \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity\n}\n```\nWorker: `worker \"podman rm -f ... && podman run ... --env-file $envfile ...\"` — envfile is on worker side (made there via ssh). Model dir staged on both nodes (weights receipt).\n\nRay:\n```\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" --include-dashboard=false\nworker \"podman exec $FN_CONTAINER ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT --num-cpus $RAY_NUM_CPUS\"\n```\n\nTwo-GPU gate:\n```\nfor i in $(seq 1 60); do\n  gpus=$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^\\s*[0-9.]+/([0-9.]+) GPU' ...)\n```\nParse: ray status outputs \" 0/2 GPU\". grep -oE '[0-9.]+/[0-9.]+ +GPU' and take total after '/'. Simpler: `podman exec ... python3 -c \"import ray; ray.init(address='auto'); print(ray.cluster_resources().get('GPU',0))\"` — heavy but deterministic. Donor grepped `ray status` for \"2.0 GPU\". Use grep: `ray status | grep -qE '(^|[^0-9.])2\\.0/?[0-9.]* *GPU'`... hmm, format of ray status resource lines is: ` 0/2 GPU`. So grep -E '^[[:space:]]*[0-9.]+/2(\\.0)?[[:space:]]+GPU'. To be robust: extract with sed then compare via awk:\n```\ntotal=$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null | awk '/ GPU$/ {print $2}' | cut -d/ -f2 | head -n1)\n[ \"${total:-0}\" = \"2\" ] || [ \"${total:-0}\" = \"2.0\" ] && break\n```\nawk on \"0/2 GPU\": $2 = \"0/2\"? Line looks like \" 0/2 GPU\" → $1=\"0/2\", $2=\"GPU\". So awk '$2==\"GPU\" {split($1,a,\"/\"); print a[2]}'. Good.\n\nTimeout 120 iterations × 2s = 4 min; on fail: dump ray status, exit 1.\n\nServe: via podman exec -d with log:\n```\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83} \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log 2>&1\"\n```\nWarning about comments inside backslash continuation (donor's minefield) — do not put comments in, carry the warning over.\n\nWait — vllm serve with positional arg model dir: `vllm serve <model>`. Yes.\n\nReadiness poll: `curl -fsS localhost:$FN_PORT/v1/models | grep -q flashnext` for FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT=2700, step 20s.\n\nPrint summary + exit 0.\n\nfn-cluster-down.sh:\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Idempotent teardown...\nset -uo pipefail   # no -e: must not die mid-teardown\nsource fn-env.sh tolerantly: `source ... 2>/dev/null || true`, then fallbacks: FN_WORKER_HOST=${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2} etc.\nReap serve local+remote, ray stop --force local+remote (inside containers? ray stop needs to run where ray is running: inside container podman exec ray stop --force; but if the container is gone, then host pgrep reap covers it. Order: reap serve (host pgrep, kills processes in container via host pidns — podman shares pidns with host by default, so processes are visible), then podman exec ray stop --force || true on both nodes, then podman rm -f container on both nodes.\nexit 0\n```\nNote: does rootless podman on the host have container processes visible from host /proc? Rootless podman: container processes run inside user namespace but are visible from host /proc (with mapped uids). pgrep -f \"vllm serve\" on the host can see them. Donor relied on exactly this. Good.\n\nfn-preflight.sh:\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Readiness: byte diff of env across both ranks, latency-hold reads on both ends,\n# per-rail link speed, round-trip probe on both rails.\nset -euo pipefail\nsource fn-env.sh\nRECEIPT=${REPO_ROOT}/results/receipts/preflight.json\n```\nSteps:\n1. Per-node env dump: local via bash; remote via ssh. Filter doctrine + FN keys, sort. cmp. Record pass/fail.\n2. Latency hold: local `od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency` via sudo -n (may need root); fallback: cat? /dev/cpu_dma_latency is root rw. Try `sudo -n od ...`, on failure → \"unreadable\". Also systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster. Remote same via ssh. \"held\" = value 0.\n3. Link speed: /sys/class/net/$rail/speed on both nodes for thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1 (skip if absent).\n4. RTT probe: per node, per rail with routable IP: peer IP: coordinator rail0 peer 10.99.0.2; worker rail0 peer 10.99.0.1. How to compute? Derive: read own IP a.b.c.1/.2 → peer = flip last octet, keep /30. General: for /30, peer of x.1 is x.2 and vice versa. Implement in awk: last octet 1→2, 2→1, else skip. ping -c 20 -i 0.01 -W 2 peer → parse avg from rtt line, µs = avg*1000. Budget FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=200.\nAggregate status: pass if env diff matches and rail0 rtt on both ends within budget and latency-hold held (value 0) on both ends (fail with reason on unreadable? U.2 requires hold armed — if unreadable, mark status fail? That risks failing the night for a mere sudo config. The manifest says device reads 0 when held; sudo -n should work for tom (fleet sudo is configured). Soft-fail: record as \"unknown\", overall status fail only if env diff or rtt budget violated; hold unknown → warn but pass? Donor's doctrine is strict: preflight is a gate. But a permission issue is not hardware truth. Compromise: if is-active lowlat-cluster is active AND device value is readable → record held:true. If unit is active but device is unreadable → held:\"unreadable\" (don't fail). If unit inactive → held:false → fail. That's principled: the unit's state is authoritative evidence of arming; the device read corroborates.)\n\nWrite preflight.json {\"step\":\"preflight\",\"status\":...,\"ts\":...,\"data\":{...}}. exit 0/1.\n\nrun-tp2.sh — orchestrates everything as designed. Python client via heredoc. Fixed prompt: a constant string, no family name. Use `FN_FIXED_PROMPT` default: \"The fleet boots before the operator arrives.\" Fine.\n\nTwo greedy completions: temperature 0 → greedy. Compare .choices[0].text.\n\nFidelity: 3 fixed prompts; for each: completion with max_tokens=64, logprobs=5, echo=true? /v1/completions supports echo + logprobs. Compute loss = -mean(token logprob of continuation). Frontier logits = final token's top-5 logprobs. Save to results/fidelity/prompt-<i>.json. sha256 of each file → receipt.\n\nContext probe: short: prompt ~32 tokens, max_tokens 64 → decode tps via usage + timing (measure walltime for generation; tps = completion_tokens / (elapsed - prefill)? Can't measure prefill directly; use overall tps as proxy on both arms — the ratio cancels the bias if prefill is small on both... short-prompt long-decode, and long-prompt short-decode: short arm: prompt 32, decode 128. Long arm: prompt ~262144-128, decode 128. Decode-heavy ratio: measure (completion_tokens)/(elapsed_total - prefill_estimate)? Keep simple: measure tokens/sec over decode by streaming? Use stream=true with SSE timing: time from first chunk to last chunk ≈ decode time for both arms. That's the honest decode separation (bench harness task does the proper client; here the ratio probe is best-effort: use streaming, measure inter-chunk decode window). Compute decode_tps = (completion_tokens - 1)/decode_elapsed. Ratio = long/short.\n\n262144-token prompt: build by repeating text. Token count estimate: use a rough 3.5 chars/token for English; target 262144 tokens → ~917k chars. Fine in-memory. Set an FN_CONTEXT_TARGET=262144 constant; note exact tokenization is engine-side; verify server accepted full prompt (usage.prompt_tokens reported) and record the actual prompt_tokens. If the server truncates/refuses → fail receipt.\n\nResidency python (host-side): run on coordinator locally and via ssh on worker:\n```\nssh worker python3 - <<'PY' ... \n```\nCollect JSON per node: {gtt_bytes, gtt_source, rss_bytes, table_pages_cached, table_files_matched}. GTT candidates: /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_gtt_used; fallback mem_info_vram_used (with source name recorded). RSS: walk /proc, cmdline contains \"vllm serve\" or ray:: — actually rank processes = vllm serve main + ray workers. Sum VmRSS of processes whose cmdline matches \"vllm serve\" or \"ray::\" ... ray worker processes' cmdline is like \"ray::IDLE\"? On this pair, one rank per node: the TP rank on that node = the vllm/ray python worker running the model. Simpler: sum RSS of processes matching 'vllm serve' or 'ray::Worker'? Use pgrep -f 'vllm serve|ray::' — caveat. Use regex 'vllm serve' and 'ray::' to sum RSS. OK.\n\nTable page cache: files under FN_MODEL_DIR matching os.environ.get FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*ple*\" (case-insensitive?) — the PLE table shards. fincore via linux-fincore binary may not exist; use python mincore: open file, mmap, mincore per page chunk — heavy for 185GB file! Use `fincore` if available; else fall back to /proc/meminfo? Alt honest and cheap probe: for matched table files, use `mincore` over the mapped region — mmap a 185GB file and touch pages in 4KB steps is a page-table walk only (mincore doesn't fault). Python mmap over huge file + mincore in 4KB steps = 48M syscalls — too slow. Stride by page checking 1 page per 2MB region? Loses resolution. Realistic: use `fincore` (util-linux) if available, else `pcstat` if available, else record null with reason. This pair's fleet probably has util-linux fincore. Implement fincore-first, graceful null fallback.\n\nWhich files: the table is 51.2B params FP8 ≈ 51GB. Detect: files ≥ 1GiB that are NOT *.safetensors? The shards are safetensors (131 of them) — the table might be in safetensors shards... For the mmap path, ds4 had a dedicated table file (mmap requires raw layout). ple_mmap.validate_shards_for suggests shards. Honestly: FN_TABLE_GLOB default \"*ple*,*ngram*,*.table\" — if zero matches → record files_matched 0 and page_cache null; the receipt still records table_gpu_resident_bytes based on serve log probe. Enough; morning operator can set FN_TABLE_GLOB.\n\ntable_gpu_resident_bytes: probe serve log: grep -i 'ple_mmap' $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log for engagement marker (e.g., \"ple_mmap\" + \"engaged\"/\"build_tables\"). Marker not precisely known; accept any line matching 'ple_mmap' AND VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1 → 0; else null (receipt will fail loudly → morning operator confirms). Also, log the matched lines into data.\n\nHmm wait — the fork's ple_mmap logs loudly (patch 0010 test says it logs and defers). Accept grep -ci 'ple_mmap' > 0.\n\nresidency.json data:\n```\n{\"gtt_gib_per_rank\": {\"coordinator\": x, \"worker\": y},\n \"gtt_source\": ..., \"rss_gib_per_rank\": {...},\n \"table_page_cache\": {...},\n \"table_gpu_resident_bytes\": 0,\n \"read_after_warmed_decode\": true}\n```\nUpper bound: gtt ≤ 80 GiB per rank.\n\nWarm decode requirement: before reading residency, issue a completion with max_tokens=64 (after warmup run). Set flag true only after that request succeeds.\n\nNow, unit file:\n\n```\n# flashnext pair service — one command up, one command down, no boot-time install.\n[Unit]\nDescription=flashnext TP=2 pair service (coordinator + worker)\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nRemainAfterExit=yes\nTimeoutStartSec=45min\nTimeoutStopSec=5min\n# The model takes tens of minutes to load and claims both GPUs...explicit start only.\nExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh\nExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n# ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails; StopPost does, so a failed\n# bring-up still tears the pair down instead of stranding ray daemons and\n# half-up containers behind a 'failed' wrapper.\nExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n\n# Deliberately NO [Install] section: ...\n```\n\nTest file asserts:\n- env doctrine lines\n- `ip -br addr` in fn-env.sh (computed), no hardcoded `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1`\n- no F.9 probe-default exports\n- unit: ExecStopPost present, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes, no WantedBy, no [Install]\n- bash -n on host/*.sh and scripts/run-tp2.sh\n- fn-cluster-up.sh contains reap-before-gate: assert reap function defined before ray start? Textual order check: file.index(\"reap\") < file.index(\"ray start\"). Nice determinism.\n- scripts/run-tp2.sh writes 4 receipts (grep step names tp2/residency/fidelity/context).\n\nNow, also re-verify acceptance command: `for f in host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh; do bash -n \"$f\"; done` — all four new .sh in host/ + run-tp2.sh must parse. And greps. Then `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v` from repo root.\n\nAlso make sure repo-invariants still passes: my scripts must not trigger secret scan (no hf_ tokens etc.), GPL scan is fine.\n\nOne more point: run-smoke.sh has IMAGE default \"flashnext:dev\"; my scripts use FN_IMAGE with same default — consistent.\n\nFN_PORT default: donor used 1234; llama-swap runs on its own port on this fleet; pick 1234 for consistency with donor doctrine? Spec/worklist doesn't pin. FN_PORT default 1234? Hmm, risk of clashing with leftover ds4 stack? ds4 not on this fleet (this is coordinator/worker of the same fleet where ds4-vllm ran? Donor env from ds4 campaign on same hardware? ds4-vllm-manifest is donor material from another estate (hy3?). Actually evidence came from \"AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm\" upstream — different rig. Our fleet: coordinator/worker Strix Halo pair. Port 1234 probably free. Pick FN_PORT=${FN_PORT:-1234}. OK.\n\nAlso let me verify the ssh nicknames: §2.3 dotfiles has ssh nicknames — \"worker\" probably aliased. But steering says worker-side ops go over wire 10.99.9.2. Default FN_WORKER_HOST=10.99.9.2. Good, explicit and correct.\n\nEnv-file filtering at container start: include LD_LIBRARY_PATH? No surgery needed here (no patched ROCr preload — that's donor's custom build). Skip.\n\nAlso `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, `VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=1`, `VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=1`, `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1`? Tokenizer loading from local dir anyway. Add HF_HUB_OFFLINE + VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS + VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK (F.6 doctrine).\n\nNote grep acceptance: `grep -q 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' host/fn-env.sh` ✓; `grep -q 'thunderbolt0' host/fn-env.sh` ✓ (in candidates + log).\n\nOne subtlety: fn-env.sh computes rails and logs them on source. In env-file generation (`env` dumps), we must ensure logging goes to stderr only — yes.\n\nIn fn-cluster-up, env file written to $FN_STATE_DIR/ (on disk). Worker: create via ssh heredoc. Worker-side container start also needs model volume + state dir on worker: mkdir -p $FN_STATE_DIR on worker via ssh.\n\npodman rootless + /dev/kfd: needs --group-add keep-groups (rootless podman standard for kfd access). run-smoke.sh used plain podman run with --device; follow existing repo style (run-smoke.sh does not add --group-add). Add `--group-add keep-groups` anyway? deviates from smoke script... smoke runs `podman run --rm --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined --ipc=host`. Mirror that exactly, plus --network host, -d, volumes, env-file, ulimit memlock=-1 (RCCL/RDMA-ish; harmless), sleep infinity.\n\nOK, one more thing: 2-GPU gate counts GPU resources ray sees. Container with /dev/kfd → ray sees 1 GPU each → 2.0 total.\n\nEdge: `ray start --head --port`: also need --num-gpus? Ray autodetects via /dev/kfd? Ray's GPU detection uses nvidia-smi / rocm-smi. Donor got \"2.0 GPU\" on a rig where ray autodetects. Container has rocm stack (vendor wheels) → rocm-smi present → detects. Trust it; gate loudly enforces.\n\nServe host binding: --host 0.0.0.0 to be reachable from... Only coordinator-local client (run-tp2 runs on coordinator). 127.0.0.1 suffices; but worker rank connects to head API? No—only ray. Use 0.0.0.0 as donor does (they had external checks). Keep 0.0.0.0.\n\nLet me also design run-tp2.sh receipts writing via python heredocs. Structure:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# First-light runner: preflight -> pair up -> greedy byte-compare -> residency\n# -> fidelity -> 262144-context probe. Writes tp2.json, residency.json,\n# fidelity.json, context.json per the receipts-verify.py schema.\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts\"\nmkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$REPO_ROOT/results/fidelity\"\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nAPI=\"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT\"\n```\n\nSteps with fail-fast: preflight; cluster-up.\n\nPython helper for completions: `fn_api()` heredoc python with urllib, JSON arg via argv, prints response JSON. Write one python block that does everything? Cleaner: multiple smaller heredocs. Long but explicit. Or embed one python program that handles steps 3–7 with subprocess calls to host measurements? The residency host measurement needs ssh + sysfs — do that in a separate heredoc python (host side, python 3 stdlib: subprocess for ssh).\n\nPlan run-tp2.sh sections:\n\nA. preflight + up (bash).\nB. greedy byte-compare → write tp2.json via python heredoc (also embeds preflight's rail info from preflight.json).\n   Actually simpler: tp2.json written from bash: byte_identical is python compare result; rail speed and rtt pulled from preflight.json via python. Make it one python heredoc \"step_tp2\" that reads preflight.json, makes two completions, compares, writes tp2.json.\nC. Warmup + residency: python heredoc \"step_residency\": warmup req (300 tokens once), then 64-token decode; gather per-node metrics (local + ssh); grep serve.log for ple_mmap; write residency.json.\nD. Fidelity python heredoc.\nE. Context probe python heredoc.\n\nFinal summary line. Any step exits non-zero → script fails (ladder sees it). Receipts written with status fail where meaningful (fidelity/context write receipts with status fail internally on error; tp2/residency are fatal exits after receipt writing).\n\nStreaming timing for context probe: urllib streaming SSE parse — read lines, mark t_first at first data chunk with content, t_last at end. Implement carefully.\n\nFixed prompts (no family name):\nP1 \"The pair boots before the operator arrives, and the ledger records what the night proved.\"\n2–3 variants.\n\n300-token greedy completion max_tokens=300.\n\nToken estimates for context probe: build prompt by repeating a paragraph; request with max_tokens=128; read usage.prompt_tokens from response; require prompt_tokens >= 0.95*target, else fail. Target 262144. To avoid over-shooting max-model-len: aim prompt tokens ≈ target - 160. Estimate chars/token ratio 3.6; iterate: build, then just accept actual prompt_tokens (engine counts). If over → server errors out → fail honestly. Slight margin: target_chars = (262144-192)*4 → ~1,047,808 chars ~1MB. OK.\n\nResidency GTT sysfs: card paths: /sys/class/drm/card*/device/mem_info_gtt_used — iterate cards, prefer card0; also try /sys/class/drm/card0/device/mem_info_vram_used. Convert bytes→GiB.\n\nRSS: sum via os.scandir of /proc/*/cmdline matching 'vllm serve' or 'ray::'.\n\nfincore: `fincore --json`? plain: `fincore <file>` outputs \"RES PAGES ...\" lines. Parse sum of pages? fincore output per file: `RES    PAGES  SIZE SIZE% FILE`. Parse PAGES column (2nd). Use subprocess; skip if fincore not present (shutil.which).\n\nTable glob default: \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*,*table*\" split on comma; fnmatch case-insensitive. Files under FN_MODEL_DIR (one level + nested walk). Cap: only consider files ≥ 100 MiB for page-cache? No — count whatever matched.\n\nNote: fincore on 185GB files — fine, reads statx-like info.\n\nssh remote execution of this python: `ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" python3 - <<'PY' ... PY`. Heredoc over ssh works (stdin).\n\nServe.log location: $FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log on coordinator (inside mounted dir = host path same). ple grep there.\n\nAlright, also \"worker python pool kept small\": RAY_NUM_CPUS=4 → ray start --num-cpus on both nodes. Done.\n\nAlso re-verify the acceptance grep `grep -q 'VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_' host/fn-env.sh` — my line: `export VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_` ✓.\n\n`grep -q 'PYTHONHASHSEED=0'` → `export PYTHONHASHSEED=0` ✓.\n`grep -q 'NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1'` ✓.\n\nTest unittest import: tests/test_host_tooling.py with ROOT relative pathlib — same as siblings.\n\nAlso: don't create results/receipts/preflight.json during test runs — tests don't run preflight.\n\nAlright — also verify `bash -n` for each script after writing.\n\nAnother consideration: conflictDomains requires all paths touched to be inside host/, scripts/run-tp2.sh, tests/. results/fidelity/*.json is created by run-tp2.sh at runtime, not by my commit — fine (git commit touches only my files). Not committing receipts.\n\nAlso — does repo-invariants' test_receipts_verify_runs_clean pass with existing receipts? Existing results/receipts has build.json, weights-*.json — they were already passing. I add no receipts. Good.\n\nLet me also read the tail of the CONTINGENCY doc for anything? Not needed. ROUTING.md — the steering says read ROUTING for compute routing doctrine; the brief says read cited spec sections and style references — done. ROUTING might contain host-tooling-specific guidance; let me skim quickly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me quickly skim `handoff/ROUTING.md` for any host-tooling-specific doctrine, and check dotfiles §2 addressing details:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c5dade61147342ddbd2589f0","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && grep -n -A6 \"host-tooling\" handoff/ROUTING.md | head -40"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f72a694692df4f989c3256f8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && sed -n '175,260p' specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5350,"output":8335,"cacheRead":50688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8135,"totalTokens":64373,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988999036,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ded87e86-00eb-4035-ba3e-7d5cfa19d73f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_c5dade61147342ddbd2589f0","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"36:| **host-tooling** | **qwen, watched** | **single-retry fuse** — see below |\n37-| bench-harness | qwen | default ladder; behavioral test gates it |\n38-| proxy-tooling | qwen | weak grep gate; overseer reads both scripts before `cp-proxy` |\n39-| morning-ledger | qwen | default ladder; content reviewed at close |\n40-| catalog-handoff | qwen | verify patch against precedent row before morning |\n41-| rdma-package | qwen | verify-and-fix (v2 exists under `host/rdma/`); safety greps are real |\n42-| cp-weights … cp-close (7) | no model | direct argv; a failure is a *product* defect, not a lane defect. `cp-weights` fast-paths: pre-staging launched pre-arm, idempotent rsync |\n--\n44:**host-tooling** is the judged case: five scripts + a unit + a test across two\n45-hosts, and its grep gate is satisfiable by wrong code. A pre-dispatch\n46-task-scoped steer (posted at arm time; the pre-dispatch re-read folds it into\n47-the first brief) pins the semantic traps: NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME COMPUTED from\n48-`ip -br addr` (rail 1 has no peer IP — hardcoding both rails passes the grep\n49-and hangs RCCL bootstrap); **NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 unconditionally** (ibverbs\n50-devices now exist on both nodes by design); never export a probe-read engine\n--\n66:2. Retry fails (or a host-tooling fuse trigger) → TAKEOVER: Opus workers\n67-   (ultracode) author the fix in a scratch checkout OUTSIDE the lane\n68-   worktree; Fable reviews and squashes to ONE exact unified diff; the diff\n69-   is delivered TO THE LANE as a steer (\"apply exactly this patch,\n70-   byte-for-byte, with `git apply`; then run the acceptance argv and\n71-   commit\"). If the task is already escalated/blocked,\n72-   `tally campaign resume <master-issue-url> --reason … --wait` pardons the\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989190369},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f72a694692df4f989c3256f8","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### 2.1 The three-layer addressing doctrine **[S]** — `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:12-21`\n\nVerbatim from committed bytes:\n```\n# Addressing doctrine, three layers:\n#   - 10.99.0.0/30  tb-fleet   (thunderbolt0) — the tensor rail, unchanged.\n#   - 10.99.1.0/30  eth-fleet  (enp191s0)     — this file, always-on.\n#   - 10.99.9.x/32  fleet IPs  (lo)           — the STABLE identity.\n```\nSo the third layer is `10.99.9.x/32` on **loopback**, and the ethernet rail is `10.99.1.x/30` — the campaign's guessed shape is correct.\n\nAssignments **[S]**:\n- coordinator: `10.99.0.1/30` tb (`modules/lowlat-cluster.nix:40`), `10.99.1.1/30` eth (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:67`), `10.99.9.1/32` lo (`hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:51`)\n- worker: `10.99.0.2/30` tb, `10.99.1.2/30` eth (`hosts/worker/default.nix:312`), `10.99.9.2/32` lo (`hosts/worker/default.nix:299`)\n\nFleet identity is a dedicated oneshot, not `networking.localCommands` — `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:42-53` and `hosts/worker/default.nix:292-301`, both `Type=oneshot`, `RemainAfterExit=true`, `ExecStart = ip addr replace 10.99.9.x/32 dev lo`, `after = network-pre.target`. Rationale at `eth-fleet.nix:42-43`: \"networking.localCommands is masked under NetworkManager (found the hard way on deploy night).\"\n\n### 2.2 Route metrics — the #240 flip **[S]**\n\n- **eth-fleet route: metric 20**, declared. `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:75` → `route1 = \"10.99.9.2/32,10.99.1.2,20\";` and `hosts/worker/default.nix:317` → `route1 = \"10.99.9.1/32,10.99.1.1,20\";`\n  Keyfile syntax note at `eth-fleet.nix:72-74`: \"(routeN=dest,next-hop,metric) — a nmcli-style `routes` key is silently ignored by the keyfile parser.\"\n- **Thunderbolt route to the fleet IP: metric 50, IMPERATIVE, not in the tree.** `hosts/coordinator/eth-fleet.nix:17-18`: \"via TB at metric 50 (on the tb-fleet profile, added imperatively like the profile itself)\". `:28-29`: \"The imperative TB route stays at 50, untouched — only this profile's declared metric moved below it.\"\n- The flip is dated in-file: `eth-fleet.nix:23-27` — \"METRICS FLIPPED 2026-08-28 (dotfiles#240 ruling): ethernet used to sit at 200 behind TB's 50\".\n\n**[M] Live routing tables confirm it:**\n```\ncoordinator $ ip route show | grep 10.99\n10.99.0.0/30 dev thunderbolt0 proto kernel scope link src 10.99.0.1 metric 101\n10.99.1.0/30 dev enp191s0     proto kernel scope link src 10.99.1.1 metric 103\n10.99.9.2 via 10.99.1.2 dev enp191s0     proto static metric 20\n10.99.9.2 via 10.99.0.2 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 50\n\nworker $ ip route show | grep 10.99\n10.99.0.0/30 dev thunderbolt0 proto kernel scope link src 10.99.0.2 metric 100\n10.99.1.0/30 dev enp191s0     proto kernel scope link src 10.99.1.2 metric 102\n10.99.9.1 via 10.99.1.1 dev enp191s0     proto static metric 20\n10.99.9.1 via 10.99.0.1 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 50\n```\n`[M]` **Incidental**: the worker also carries a stale imperative `10.77.0.0/30 via 10.99.0.1 dev thunderbolt0 proto static metric 100` — the retired legacy /30 to the NAS. It exists in no committed file (`grep -rn \"10.77\" --include=*.nix .` hits only `modules/mesh-registry.nix:70` as an alias). Purely imperative residue.\n\n### 2.3 The SSH nickname — `home/ssh.nix` **[S]**\n\n`home/ssh.nix` is the \"fourth SSH layer\" — operator nickname → target (`:6-13`). 124 lines.\n\n- `operatorAliases` at `:42-50`: `coordinator→coordinator`, `zenbook→zenbook-duo`, `nas→nas`, `worker→worker`.\n- **`workerRail` at `home/ssh.nix:97`:**\n  ```nix\n  workerRail = if hostName == \"coordinator\" then \"10.99.9.2\" else \"worker\";\n  ```\n- `mkBlock` at `:99-112`: `HostName = if target == \"worker\" then workerRail else target;` plus `User = \"tom\"`, `IdentityFile = \"~/.ssh/id_ed25519\"`, `IdentitiesOnly = true`, `StrictHostKeyChecking = \"yes\"`, and `ProxyJump = \"coordinator\"` only for `nas` from a non-coordinator/non-nas host (`needsJump`, `:60`).\n- Emitted via the new HM API: `programs.ssh.settings = lib.mapAttrs mkBlock operatorAliases;` (`:119-123`), `enableDefaultConfig = false` — API deprecation rationale at `:28-34`.\n- Guard at `:114-117`: asserts every alias target exists in `mesh-registry.nix`.\n\n**The measurement that justified the flip is committed in the comment** (`home/ssh.nix:71-76`), 200 samples each, PM QoS held:\n```\n#   TB   10.99.0.2 : rtt min/avg/max = 33/58/122 us, mdev 18 us\n#   eth  10.99.1.2 : rtt min/avg/max = 58/72/142 us, mdev  9 us\n```\nRuling text at `:62-66`: \"Thunderbolt (10.99.0.x) is reserved for LLM-parallelism / tensor traffic ONLY; admin traffic … prefers the dedicated 5GbE cable (eth-fleet) and rides the stable fleet identity.\"\n\n**Coordinator-only preference** (`:91-96`): from zenbook or NAS the nickname resolves to `worker` (the LAN identity 10.42.0.5).\n\n### 2.4 Mesh/fleet registry **[S]** — `modules/mesh-registry.nix` (84 lines)\n\n`worker` row at `:52-63`:\n```nix\naliases = [ \"worker\" \"10.42.0.5\" \"10.99.0.2\" \"10.99.1.2\" \"10.99.9.2\" ];\nhostKey = \"ssh-ed25519 AAAA…CzwLO root@worker\";\nuserKey = \"ssh-ed25519 AAAA…VX8Z tom@mesh-20260729\";\n```\n`coordinator` at `:18-27`: aliases `[ \"coordinator\" \"10.99.1.1\" \"10.99.9.1\" ]` — **note: the coordinator row does NOT list `10.99.0.1` or `10.42.0.2`.** All five worker addresses carry the same pinned host key, so no TOFU on any rail. `modules/mesh.nix:25-28` turns the registry into `programs.ssh.knownHosts`; `:30-36` authorizes the user keys for `tom` and `root`.\n\n### 2.5 The deploy target — **still Thunderbolt** ⚠\n\n`flake.nix:503-542` is the deploy-rs block. Global opts at `:504-512`: `sshUser/user = \"root\"`, `sshOpts = fleetDeploySshOpts` (defined `:299-334`, includes `-F /dev/null`, `StrictHostKeyChecking=yes`, `UserKnownHostsFile=/etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts`, `AddressFamily=inet`, `-i /run/agenix/ssh-user-key`), `autoRollback`/`magicRollback` true, `remoteBuild = false`, `activationTimeout = 1200`.\n\n**`flake.nix:536`:**\n```nix\nhostname = if host == \"worker\" then \"10.99.0.2\" else host;\n```\nSee §7 — this is the single largest divergence from the campaign record.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Model catalog\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989190369},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Everything confirmed:\n- From the coordinator, ssh to the worker is done via the fleet identity `10.99.9.2` (the wire, eth metric 20).\n- Coordinator 10.99.9.1, worker 10.99.9.2. Rail 0: coordinator 10.99.0.1, worker 10.99.0.2.\n- Latency budget 200 µs; while held, RTT is roughly 58–90 µs.\n\nNow I'll write the files. First, let me write `host/fn-env.sh`.\n\nDetails to be careful about in fn-env.sh:\n- Must be both sourceable and executable (bash -n only checks syntax).\n- Rail calculation: parse `ip -br addr show dev $rail`.\n\nFormat of `ip -br addr show dev thunderbolt0`: `thunderbolt0 UP 10.99.0.1/30` — fields: name, flags, address. Flags may contain multiple words (UP,LOWER_UP). Safer approach: use `ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\"` and grab tokens matching the IPv4 pattern: `grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}/[0-9]+'`. Then strip /prefix. Exclude 169.254.*. Exclude 127.*. Then head -1.\n\nfn-env.sh contents:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: ray head, ray worker, `vllm serve`, and\n# the bring-up/teardown scripts. Both ranks MUST see the same doctrine —\n# run-tp2's preflight byte-diffs the FN_ env across the pair before first\n# light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below is what carries\n# FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default copy prefixes;\n# without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh discipline\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1,2).\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: never export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If you think a\n# knob here is \"just pinning the default\", it is not — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n```\n\nThen the FN_ config block, then determinism/alloc/hsa, caches, NCCL (computed rails, IB disable, timeouts), ray, table path, offline.\n\nNext, the rail function. Also, when the candidate list ends up empty, log loudly and fail. Note: acceptance `grep -q thunderbolt0` — the function body contains the literal thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1. Good.\n\nWriting all files now. Executable bit too: chmod +x.\n\nfn-cluster-up.sh — write it carefully. Full text:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-cluster-up.sh — stand the flashnext pair service with one command.\n#\n# Order is load-bearing (reap-then-gate, ds4-cluster-restart.sh doctrine):\n#   1. REAP stranded serve processes on BOTH nodes first, gate on zero residue\n#   2. containers on both nodes (worker over ssh on the WIRE — the rails\n#      carry tensors only, never control traffic)\n#   3. ray head on the coordinator, worker join (python pool capped small)\n#   4. hard two-GPU gate before serve\n#   5. the TP=2 eager serve, then a wait-for-reality API poll\n#\n# Failure cannot strand ranks: flashnext-pair.service runs this as a oneshot\n# with ExecStopPost=fn-cluster-down.sh, so even a failed bring-up tears down.\nset -euo pipefail\n```\n\nFunctions: log, worker(), ship scripts via ssh stdin, env dump function.\n\nLocal env dump:\n```bash\nenv_dump() {  # $1 = output file\n  ( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n    | grep -E '^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)' \\\n    | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$1\"\n}\n```\nfn-env.sh logs to stderr; >/dev/null only suppresses stdout. Keep stderr visible (journald). No problem: `source ... >/dev/null`.\n\nWait — sourcing fn-env.sh requires `ip` etc. On the coordinator no issue.\n\nRemote env dump + container:\n```bash\n# Ship fn-env.sh to the worker over the wire; it computes ITS OWN rail list there.\nremote_tmp=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\nworker \"cat > '$remote_tmp/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nworker \"( set -a; source '$remote_tmp/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) | grep -E '...' | LC_ALL=C sort > '$remote_tmp/env.list'\"\n```\n\nContainer startup helper (same args for both nodes):\n```\npodman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\npodman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" \\\n  --network host --ipc host \\\n  --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n  --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n  -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n  -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n  --env-file \"$env_file\" \\\n  \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity\n```\n\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR\" on both nodes.\n\nReap:\n```bash\nreap_serve_node() {  # runs reap locally on whichever node; prints residue count\n  local pids\n  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n  if [ -n \"$pids\" ]; then\n    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 3\n    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n    [ -z \"$pids\" ] || kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 1\n  fi\n  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n}\n```\nHmm, pattern: vllm serve's cmdline is \"python3 /usr/local/bin/vllm serve ...\" or \"/opt/venv/bin/vllm serve\". Donor used `bin/[v]llm serve`. Use the same. But bare `vllm serve` via podman exec — cmdline contains the \"bin/vllm\" path. OK.\n\nLocal residue gate:\n```bash\nresidue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\n[ \"$residue\" -eq 0 ] || { log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) ...\"; exit 1; }\nremote: residue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\n```\nPassing the function over ssh: `worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\"` — ssh concatenates args into a single command string; fine.\n\nReaping the ray daemons too? Reap only targets serve, and ray stop comes at down time. For fresh startup: `podman exec ... ray stop --force || true` before ray start on each node? If a previous ray lingers inside the container and the container was rm -f'd, it dies with the container (does podman kill processes on rm -f? rootless podman with cgroups — yes, rm -f kills). So rm -f the containers before starting ray. Order: reap serve (host side, so that serve doesn't survive container removal in a stranded state?) — serve runs inside the container via podman exec -d; when rm -f kills the container, does the serve process die? podman exec -d process: when the container is stopped/killed, exec'd processes are killed via conmon/cgroup. Donor's husk issue was systemd-run unit + SIGKILL'd conmon leaving the wrapper behind. Belt and suspenders: reap host-side first (in line with steering \"reap then gate\"), then rm -f containers, then fresh ones.\n\nRay head start:\n```bash\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head \\\n  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n  --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" \\\n  --include-dashboard=false\n```\nRAY_NUM_CPUS from env (sourced fn-env.sh).\n\nWorker join:\n```bash\nworker \"podman exec $FN_CONTAINER ray start --address=$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT --num-cpus $RAY_NUM_CPUS\"\n```\n\n2-GPU gate:\n```bash\ngpus_total=\"\"\nfor _ in $(seq 1 120); do\n  gpus_total=\"$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null \\\n    | awk '$2 == \"GPU\" { split($1, a, \"/\"); print a[2]; exit }')\" || true\n  case \"$gpus_total\" in 2|2.0) break ;; esac\n  sleep 2\ndone\ncase \"$gpus_total\" in\n  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed\" ;;\n  *) log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (got '$gpus_total')\"; podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status || true; exit 1 ;;\nesac\n```\n\nServe launch + log + readiness:\n```bash\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization ${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83} \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n```\n(No comments inside the continuation — include the donor warning as a comment above.)\n\nReadiness:\n```bash\nattempts=$(( FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT / 20 ))\nfor i in $(seq 1 $attempts); do\n  if curl -fsS \"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$FN_SERVED_NAME\\\"\"; then ready=1; break; fi\n  sleep 20\ndone\n[ \"${ready:-0}\" -eq 1 ] || { log FATAL serve did not answer; tail serve.log; exit 1; }\n```\n\nfn-cluster-down.sh:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Idempotent teardown of the flashnext pair service on BOTH nodes.\n# Tolerates broken config, dead ssh, half-dead containers; always exit 0.\nset -u\nSCRIPT_DIR=...\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n: \"${FN_WORKER_HOST:=10.99.9.2}\" \"${FN_CONTAINER:=flashnext-pair}\" ...\n```\n`set -u` and ${FN_X:=default}: the file may only partially source fn-env.sh. Use explicit defaults for every variable used.\n\nSteps (each line `|| true`):\n1. On both nodes reap serve (TERM→KILL), no gate (best effort, though we do log residue).\n2. On both nodes podman exec ray stop --force || true.\n3. On both nodes podman rm -f container || true.\nexit 0.\n\nfn-preflight.sh — the heavier one. Structure:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-preflight.sh — first-light readiness record for the pair.\n#\n#   1. byte-diff of both ranks' FN_/doctrine env (the two TP ranks must not diverge)\n#   2. latency-hold device read on BOTH ends (/dev/cpu_dma_latency == 0)\n#   3. per-rail link-speed record, both nodes\n#   4. round-trip probe on both rails, both ends, vs the 200us budget\n#\n# Writes results/receipts/preflight.json; exits non-zero on any hard failure.\nset -euo pipefail\nSCRIPT_DIR=...; REPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nRECEIPT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/preflight.json\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$RECEIPT\")\"\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"; trap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\nstatus=pass\nfail() { echo \"fn-preflight: FAIL: $*\" >&2; status=fail; }\n```\n\nEnv dump like cluster-up: local + remote. `cmp -s` and record.\n\nLatency hold:\n```bash\nlatency_hold() {  # print \"<held>|<unit-state>|<device-value>\"\n  local unit val=\"\"\n  unit=\"$(systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n  if [ -r /dev/cpu_dma_latency ]; then val=\"$(od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency | tr -d ' \\n')\"\n  else val=\"$(sudo -n od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \\n' || true)\"; fi\n  echo \"${unit}|${val:-unreadable}\"\n}\n```\nheld = unit active && val == 0. unit active but unreadable → record and warn, don't fail. unit not active → fail.\n\nRemote via declare -f over ssh.\n\nLink speed: for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1: on both nodes cat /sys/class/net/$rail/speed (Mb/s), or \"absent\".\n\nRTT probe: for each rail that has a routable IP on that node: compute peer (/30 flip), ping -c 20 -i 0.05 -W 2, parse: `ping -q`'s last line `rtt min/avg/max/mdev = a/b/c/d ms` → avg ms → µs. Record per rail per end. Only rails that have a routable IP (i.e. NCCL rails) are subject to the budget check; rail 1 has no IP → record \"no-peer-ip\" and don't fail.\n\nThen write the receipt JSON via python heredoc that takes the temp files. Simpler: assemble the data as shell vars and write JSON via python reading env. Write small key=value pairs to $TMP/kv and let python construct nested JSON. Simplest, most robust: emit JSON fragments per probe from python? Keep it pragmatic: collect values into shell vars (env_ok, coord_hold, worker_hold, speeds..., rtts...), write JSON in python - <<PY with env-var interpolation via sys.argv. Pass a compact TSV $TMP/report and let python read it:\n\nreport file lines:\n```\nenv_diff\tpass\nhold_coordinator\tactive|0\nhold_worker\tactive|0\nspeed_coordinator_thunderbolt0\t40000\nspeed_coordinator_thunderbolt1\tabsent\n...\nrtt_coordinator_thunderbolt0_us\t87\nrtt_coordinator_thunderbolt1\tno-peer-ip\nrails_chosen\tthunderbolt0\n```\nThen python builds the receipt. Clean.\n\nPeer IP computation: from own IP on rail: a.b.c.1 → peer a.b.c.2; .2 → .1 (a /30 has usable addresses .1 and .2). Implement with awk.\n\nRTT parse: `ping -q -c 20 -i 0.05 -W 2 \"$peer\" | tail -n1 | awk -F'[/ ]' '{print $8}'` is ms avg (following the dotfiles sensor pattern: awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d\", $8*1000 }'). Use that.\n\nBudget: FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}. RTT (µs) > budget on a routable rail → fail.\n\nrun-tp2.sh — full runner. Let me write it with sections. Python heredocs using urllib.request.\n\nCommon API helper inlined in each heredoc:\n\n```python\nimport json, urllib.request\nAPI = ...\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API+path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n```\n\nstep_tp2 heredoc: load preflight.json, do 2 completions, compare, write tp2.json:\ndata: {byte_identical_repeat, completion_chars, rails (from preflight: speeds+rtts), rails_chosen, latency_hold both ends, rtt_budget_us}. Receipts-verify's _tp2 only checks byte_identical_repeat.\n\nstep_residency heredoc:\n- warmup: completion max_tokens=16 \"compile pass\"\n- warmed decode: completion max_tokens=64 (>50)\n- per-node metrics: coordinator locally; worker via ssh python over stdin (same metrics script). Metrics script:\n```python\nimport glob, json, os, subprocess\ndef gtt():\n    for cand, src in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\",\"gtt\"),(\"mem_info_vram_used\",\"vram\")):\n        for p in sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\"+cand)):\n            try: return int(open(p).read().strip()), src, p\n            except Exception: continue\n    return None, None, None\ndef rss():\n    total=0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit(): continue\n        try:\n            cmd=open(f\"/proc/{pid}/cmdline\",\"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception: continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" in cmd or \"ray::\" in cmd:\n            try:\n                with open(f\"/proc/{pid}/status\") as s:\n                    for line in s:\n                        if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                            total += int(line.split()[1])*1024\n            except Exception: pass\n    return total\ndef table_pages(patterns, root):\n    import fnmatch, shutil\n    if not shutil.which(\"fincore\"): return None, []\n    matched=[]\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), p) for p in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath,f))\n    pages=0\n    for m in matched:\n        out=subprocess.run([\"fincore\", m],capture_output=True,text=True).stdout\n        # fincore: header? lines: RES PAGES SIZE SIZE% FILE\n        for line in out.splitlines():\n            parts=line.split()\n            if len(parts)>=2 and parts[1].isdigit(): pages+=int(parts[1]); break? \n```\nHmm, fincore output format: `fincore file` → one line: `RES  PAGES  SIZE  SIZE%  FILE`, e.g. `10.9G  2863452  10.9G  100  /path`. Actually RES comes first. PAGES is the 2nd token. Take int(parts[1]).\n\nPatterns: os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\",\"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").lower().split(\",\").\n\nEmit JSON.\n\n- coordinator side: run the metrics script locally via python subprocess [sys.executable, \"-c\"?] — easier: write the metrics script once to $TMP/rank_metrics.py and: local `python3 $TMP/rank_metrics.py`; remote: `ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" python3 - < $TMP/rank_metrics.py`. Both emit JSON.\n- ple probe: grep -ci \"ple_mmap\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" > 0 → table_gpu_resident_bytes=0, otherwise None → receipt fails (loud, honest).\n- Write residency.json.\n\nNote: rank → node mapping: rank 0 = coordinator (head), rank 1 = worker. Keys in gtt_gib_per_rank should be \"coordinator\"/\"worker\" (the receipts-verify loops keys generically).\n\nstep_fidelity heredoc:\n- Prompts: 3 fixed. For each: completion request {model, prompt, max_tokens:64, temperature:0, logprobs:5, echo:true?}. echo is allowed in /v1/completions. loss = mean of -logprob over generated tokens (exclude the echo'd prompt tokens: response's choices[0].logprobs.token_logprobs — the first is None if not echo; with echo=true the first element corresponds to the first prompt token... Simpler: don't echo; then token_logprobs corresponds only to the generated tokens. loss = -sum/len. Frontier logits: logprobs of the final generated token {token: logprob, top5}.\n- Save results/fidelity/fidelity-<i>.json {prompt, text, loss_nll, frontier_logits, model}. sha256 each. Receipt fidelity.json {status, files:[names], sha256:{...}, prompts:3}.\n\nstep_context heredoc:\n- Short arm: prompt is a small fixed string (~30 tokens), max_tokens=FN_CTX_DECODE (128), stream=true → decode_tps via SSE timing.\n- Long arm: build long prompt: repeat paragraph until estimated tokens ≈ target-192. target = 262144 (spec says \"given\" so hardcode with comment; override via FN_CONTEXT_TARGET).\n- After response, check usage.prompt_tokens (the last stream chunk includes usage if you request it? Not guaranteed with SSE). Alternative: non-streaming for the long arm, compute elapsed total; approximate subtracting prefill... no.\n\n  Cleaner approach: measure decode-only via streaming on both arms. Streaming chunks in vLLM include usage in the last chunk only with stream_options={\"include_usage\":true}. Include that. decode_tps = (completion_tokens - 1) / (t_last_data - t_first_data). Both arms use the same method → ratio is fair.\n- SSE parse: line starts with \"data: \", json.loads, delta text? /v1/completions streaming returns {\"choices\":[{\"text\": \"...\"}]}. Track first text chunk time and final time.\n- Long prompt size: 262144-192 tokens ≈ chars = tokens * 3.6? For safety, build the text, and after the request check actual usage.prompt_tokens; if < 0.9*target → still record ratio? Receipt needs \"context rose to 262144\": status fail if prompt_tokens < 262144-1024... but token estimate uncertainty: overshoot risks server rejection (max-model-len). Strategy: aim for target * 0.98 with conservative 4.5 chars/token → fewer tokens than the estimate → safe on the length side; then verify actual ≥ 262144-1024. If short, pad and retry once (multiply text length by target/actual ratio). Implement one retry adjustment.\n\n- Write context.json: {target_context, actual_prompt_tokens_short/long, short_decode_tps, long_decode_tps, decode_ratio_vs_short_context}.\n\nIf any step fails → the receipt records fail where meaningful; the script exits non-zero.\n\ntp2.json needs preflight data — step_tp2 heredoc reads results/receipts/preflight.json.\n\nFinal bash: echo a summary of the receipt paths.\n\nNext, the test file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Host-tooling doctrine tests — env file, unit file, script hygiene.\n\nThe host-doctrine grep gate is satisfiable by wrong code (ROUTING.md calls\nthis out); these assertions aim at the semantic traps the gate cannot see:\nthe rail list is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`, NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 is\nunconditional, no probe-read engine default is exported (spec F.9), the unit\ntears down via ExecStopPost and installs at no boot target.\n\"\"\"\nimport pathlib, re, subprocess, unittest\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nHOST = ROOT / \"host\"\n\nclass HostTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp: read texts once (classmethod)\n\n    test_fn_env_doctrine_lines: lines containing PYTHONHASHSEED=0 etc.\n    test_fn_env_rails_computed_not_hardcoded: assertIn(\"ip -br addr\", env); assertNotIn(\"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1\", env); assertIn(\"thunderbolt0\", env)\n    test_fn_env_ib_disable_unconditional: re.search(r\"^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\", env, re.M) — must be a plain export, not inside an `if`: check the line is exactly `export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` (no ${...:-} guard).\n    test_fn_env_no_probe_read_defaults (F.9): for each probe in (VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER): assertNotRegex(env, rf\"^\\s*export {probe}=\")\n    test_unit_file: text = flashnext-pair.service: assertIn ExecStopPost, Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes; assertNotIn WantedBy; assertNotIn(\"[Install]\"); assertIn fn-cluster-down.\n    test_scripts_syntax_clean: for host/*.sh + scripts/run-tp2.sh, bash -n.\n    test_cluster_up_reaps_before_gate: text.index(\"reap\") < text.index(\"ray start\"); reap... and residue gate present.\n    test_run_tp2_writes_four_receipts: for each of tp2/residency/fidelity/context, assertIn step name.\n```\n\nAlso the preflight script exists and is referenced from run-tp2.\n\nKeep it moderate.\n\nNow let's write the files. Executability too: chmod +x.\n\nOne more check — acceptance runs `python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v` from repo root. Fine.\n\nAlso — the repo has a .gitignore; verify results/ isn't ignored... results/receipts already holds committed files, so not ignored. Files in results/fidelity are created at runtime — don't commit them now.\n\nEdge case: the heredoc python in run-tp2.sh must avoid `$` expansion conflicts — use quoted 'PY'.\n\nNow let's write the files. Starting with fn-env.sh.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All research done. Now I'll write the six deliverables. Starting with `host/fn-env.sh` — the env doctrine:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bf32c01f36b7467a98c67335","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-env.sh","content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: the ray head, the ray worker, `vllm\n# serve`, and the bring-up/teardown/preflight scripts. Both TP ranks MUST see\n# the same doctrine — fn-preflight.sh byte-diffs the exported env across the\n# pair before first light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below\n# is what carries FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default\n# copy prefixes; without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Discipline lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1–2).\n# Safe to source repeatedly; every default is override-able via the environment.\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: NEVER export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If a knob here\n# looks like \"just pinning the default\", it is not that — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# --- the FN_ configuration surface (propagated to the worker rank by ray) --\nexport FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"\nexport FN_MODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nexport FN_SERVED_NAME=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\nexport FN_PORT=\"${FN_PORT:-1234}\"\nexport FN_RAY_PORT=\"${FN_RAY_PORT:-6379}\"\nexport FN_MAX_CTX=\"${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}\"\nexport FN_GPU_UTIL=\"${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}\"\nexport FN_IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nexport FN_CONTAINER=\"${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}\"\n# Worker-side actions ride the ethernet wire on the STABLE fleet identity,\n# never a 10.99.0.x Thunderbolt rail — the rails carry tensors only\n# (dotfiles-observed.md §2.3: admin traffic prefers the 5GbE cable).\nexport FN_WORKER_HOST=\"${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}\"\nexport FN_HEAD_IP=\"${FN_HEAD_IP:-10.99.9.1}\"\n# The fleet latency budget from modules/lowlat-cluster.nix: 200 us sits well\n# above the measured held figure and well below the unheld one.\nexport FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=\"${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}\"\n\n# --- determinism -------------------------------------------------------------\n# vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from PYTHONHASHSEED:\n# without a fixed value identical token content produces different block\n# filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart.\n# The greedy byte-compare in run-tp2.sh leans on this too.\nexport PYTHONHASHSEED=0\n\n# --- allocator ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# GC is off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB means reclaim starts at\n# ~105 GiB. expandable_segments is the fragmentation half. Safe with\n# --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs.\nexport PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=\"${PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF:-expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85}\"\n\n# --- HSA ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) otherwise, i.e.\n# ~2-3 cores spinning during inference and CPU thermal throttling on a pair\n# whose PM QoS is held for latency. Set FN_HSA_INTERRUPT=0 to revert to spin.\nexport HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\"${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}\"\n\n# --- compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs ------------------\n# The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bring-up after a boot\n# then recompiles every kernel — ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can\n# answer. The state directory is on-disk on both nodes.\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/triton\" 2>/dev/null || true\nexport TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\"${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor}\"\nexport TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\"${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/triton}\"\n\n# --- NCCL: the transport-of-record block --------------------------------------\n# Socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record (spec\n# ruling P7); the RDMA package under host/rdma is operator-attended only, and\n# only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked.\n#\n# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`: only Thunderbolt rails\n# that actually carry a routable /30 IP qualify. Rail 0 (thunderbolt0,\n# 10.99.0.x) is guaranteed; rail 1 (thunderbolt1) is trained but\n# IP-unconfigured today (dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix parks it) and\n# MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP — a peerless link-local rail in\n# the list hangs RCCL bootstrap. We log which rails we chose rather than\n# hardcoding both.\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail addr chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    if [ ! -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ]; then\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent on this node; not listed\" >&2\n      continue\n    fi\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \\\n      | grep -Ev '^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)' | head -n1 || true)\"\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME ($addr is routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  printf '%s' \"$chosen\"\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"\nif [ -z \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ]; then\n  echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no Thunderbolt rail carries a routable IP; refusing to name a phantom transport\" >&2\n  return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1\nfi\n\n# NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY. Since the pre-arm bake an ibverbs device\n# exists on the rails of BOTH nodes (usb4_rdma0 + usb4_rdma5, by design);\n# without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA.\n# Sockets stay the transport of record until the attended morning A/B lands a\n# counterbalanced verdict (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md). Do NOT conditionalize\n# this on device detection — the devices are present by design.\nexport NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\n\n# Cold-kernel-cache bring-up is ~25 min of LLVM before the first collective;\n# the collective timeouts must survive that without a watchdog kill.\nexport TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=\"${TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-2400}\"\nexport TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=\"${TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING:-0}\"\nexport NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=\"${NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS:-2400000}\"\n\n# --- ray ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Propagate FN_* to the worker rank through ray. ESSENTIAL: without this the\n# two TP ranks diverge on every FN_ knob above.\nexport VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\n# Ray and ROCr visible-device handling fight on multi-rank boxes; opt out.\nexport RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"${RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-1}\"\n# On a 128 GB unified-memory box the serve legitimately claims most memory;\n# ray's OOM monitor must not reap the rank. Refresh 0 disables the monitor.\nexport RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=\"${RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms:-0}\"\n# `ray start` pre-starts one idle python worker per CPU (~40 MB each); on a\n# 32-thread box that is over a gigabyte vLLM never touches. Cap the pool\n# small; fn-cluster-up.sh passes this as --num-cpus on both nodes.\nexport RAY_NUM_CPUS=\"${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}\"\n\n# --- the table path ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Serve the engram table from NVMe via mmap: page-cache faults serve gathers,\n# zero table bytes GPU-resident (spec ruling P11's zero-GPU-residency bound\n# leans on this). VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 is the fork's kill switch.\nexport VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\n\n# --- estate hygiene -------------------------------------------------------------\n# Runtime hub downloads stay forbidden (spec F.6): the checkpoint is staged\n# library-to-node, and the engine must never reach for the hub at runtime.\nexport HF_HUB_OFFLINE=\"${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=\"${VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=\"${VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK:-1}\"\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":11993,"output":8521,"cacheRead":55296,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6145,"totalTokens":75810,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989190374,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f132b478-a97b-457c-8a5e-f9ec274043d7","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_bf32c01f36b7467a98c67335","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 8288 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-env.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989355782},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait a sec — there's one problem: `export VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER` — I didn't export it, good. But the donor's environment had `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=0`! Per F.9, the is-set probe for VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* means we must not export it, even as 0. My prohibition comment covers this. OK — deliberately removed.\n\nNext up is fn-cluster-up.sh.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now `host/fn-cluster-up.sh`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ecf16abab04c4e678f62bdf4","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-cluster-up.sh — stand the flashnext pair service with one command.\n#\n# Order is load-bearing (the reap-then-gate doctrine of the ds4 estate's\n# ds4-cluster-restart.sh, specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5.5):\n#\n#   1. REAP stranded serve processes on BOTH nodes first, then GATE on zero\n#      residue. Stopping a supervising unit does not kill the `vllm serve`\n#      behind it; repeated restarts strand one husk each, holding the port.\n#   2. Containers on both nodes — the worker over ssh on the WIRE\n#      (10.99.9.2); the rails carry tensors only, never control traffic.\n#   3. Ray head on the coordinator, worker join; the worker python pool is\n#      capped small (RAY_NUM_CPUS, host/fn-env.sh).\n#   4. A hard two-GPU gate BEFORE serve — if ray never reports 2.0 GPU, we\n#      abort loud rather than serve at TP=1.\n#   5. The TP=2 eager serve, then a wait-for-reality API poll.\n#\n# Failure cannot strand ranks: flashnext-pair.service runs this as a oneshot\n# with ExecStopPost=fn-cluster-down.sh, so even a failed bring-up tears down.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nlog() { echo \"fn-cluster-up: $*\" >&2; }\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n\n# --- 1. reap stranded serve processes, then gate on zero residue -------------\n# The serve process tree is visible from the host /proc even when it runs\n# inside a podman container, so we reap host-side on both nodes. The bracket\n# in the pattern keeps pgrep from matching itself.\nreap_serve_node() {\n  local pids\n  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n  if [ -n \"$pids\" ]; then\n    echo \"fn-cluster-up: reaping stranded serve pids: $(echo $pids | tr '\\n' ' ')\" >&2\n    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 3\n    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n    [ -z \"$pids\" ] || kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 1\n  fi\n  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n}\n\nlog \"reap: coordinator\"\nresidue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\nif [ \"$residue\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the coordinator after reap\"\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"reap: worker ($FN_WORKER_HOST over the wire)\"\nresidue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\nif [ \"$residue\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the worker after reap\"\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"reap gate passed: zero residue on both nodes\"\n\n# --- 2. containers on both nodes ---------------------------------------------\n# The env file is built PER NODE by sourcing fn-env.sh there: the rail list\n# is computed from each node's own `ip -br addr`, never copied across.\nENV_FILTER='^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP_|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)'\n\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR\"\nLOCAL_ENV_FILE=\"$FN_STATE_DIR/container-env.list\"\n( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n  | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$LOCAL_ENV_FILE\"\n\nREMOTE_TMP=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\nworker \"cat > '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nworker \"mkdir -p '$FN_STATE_DIR' \\\n  && ( set -a; source '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) \\\n     | grep -E '$ENV_FILTER' | LC_ALL=C sort > '$REMOTE_TMP/env.list'\"\n\nrun_container() {  # $1 = env-file path; runs on the local node\n  podman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n  podman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" \\\n    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n    --env-file \"$1\" \\\n    \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity >/dev/null\n}\n\nlog \"container: coordinator\"\nrun_container \"$LOCAL_ENV_FILE\"\nlog \"container: worker\"\nworker \"podman rm -f '$FN_CONTAINER' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true \\\n  && podman run -d --name '$FN_CONTAINER' \\\n    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v '$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR' \\\n    --env-file '$REMOTE_TMP/env.list' \\\n    '$FN_IMAGE' sleep infinity >/dev/null\"\n\n# --- 3. ray: distributed head on the coordinator, worker join -----------------\nlog \"ray head on the coordinator\"\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head \\\n  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n  --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" \\\n  --include-dashboard=false >/dev/null\nlog \"ray join from the worker (dials the fleet identity $FN_HEAD_IP on the wire)\"\n# --include-dashboard is head-only: ray PANICs if it is passed to a worker.\nworker \"podman exec '$FN_CONTAINER' ray start \\\n  --address='$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT' \\\n  --num-cpus '$RAY_NUM_CPUS'\" >/dev/null\n\n# --- 4. hard two-GPU gate before serve ----------------------------------------\ngpus_total=\"\"\nfor _ in $(seq 1 120); do\n  gpus_total=\"$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null \\\n    | awk '$2 == \"GPU\" { split($1, a, \"/\"); print a[2]; exit }')\" || true\n  case \"$gpus_total\" in\n    2|2.0) break ;;\n  esac\n  sleep 2\ndone\ncase \"$gpus_total\" in\n  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed: ray reports $gpus_total GPU\" ;;\n  *)\n    log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (last reading: '$gpus_total'); refusing to serve\"\n    podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status >&2 || true\n    exit 1\n    ;;\nesac\n\n# --- 5. the serve: TP=2, eager -------------------------------------------------\n# Do not add comments inside the backslash-continued `vllm serve` command\n# below: a '#' there silently comments out every remaining argument, and\n# `bash -n` still reports the file as valid.\nlog \"serving $FN_MODEL_DIR as '$FN_SERVED_NAME' at TP=2 (eager)\"\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n\n# Wait for reality, not for the target's word (the fleet's library-reachable\n# pattern): poll the models endpoint until the served name answers.\nattempts=$(( ${FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT:-2700} / 20 ))\nready=0\nfor _ in $(seq 1 \"$attempts\"); do\n  if curl -fsS \"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -q \"\\\"$FN_SERVED_NAME\\\"\"; then\n    ready=1\n    break\n  fi\n  sleep 20\ndone\nif [ \"$ready\" -ne 1 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: serve did not answer on :$FN_PORT inside the timeout\"\n  tail -n 40 \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" >&2 || true\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"pair service is up: $FN_SERVED_NAME answering on :$FN_PORT\"\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-cluster-up.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":12513,"output":2347,"cacheRead":63360,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":99,"totalTokens":78220,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989355787,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9945dfdb-b3f0-4115-8cfa-0b8f02d5bff7","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ecf16abab04c4e678f62bdf4","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 6756 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-cluster-up.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989397163},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Note: In fn-cluster-up.sh, reap_serve_node is referenced via declare -f, and it's defined after the log line but before the call — in reality the definition comes before both calls. No problem.\n\nOne issue: `log \"reap: coordinator\"` appears before reap_serve_node is defined? Let me check the order: reap_serve_node is defined in the section 1 comment block, and after that comes `log \"reap: coordinator\"` and the call. Let's verify the file's order: yes, the function is defined first (after the header of section 1), followed by log + call. No problem.\n\nNext up is fn-cluster-down.sh.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now `host/fn-cluster-down.sh`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b9027cee4fbd4ec788860e17","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-cluster-down.sh — idempotent teardown of the flashnext pair service\n# on BOTH nodes. This is the ExecStop AND the ExecStopPost of\n# flashnext-pair.service: ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails,\n# StopPost does, so a failed bring-up still tears the pair down instead of\n# leaving the serve process and ray daemons running behind a 'failed'\n# wrapper. Tolerates broken config, dead ssh, and half-dead containers.\n# ALWAYS exits 0 — a teardown that fails loud is still a teardown the unit\n# must be able to complete.\nset -u\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n# Tolerate a broken env file; fall back to the doctrine defaults.\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n: \"${FN_WORKER_HOST:=10.99.9.2}\"\n: \"${FN_CONTAINER:=flashnext-pair}\"\n\nlog() { echo \"fn-cluster-down: $*\" >&2; }\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\" 2>/dev/null || true; }\n\n# Reap the serve process tree host-side (visible in /proc even from inside a\n# container): SIGTERM, a beat, SIGKILL. No gate here — teardown is best-effort.\nreap_serve_node() {\n  local pids\n  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n  if [ -n \"$pids\" ]; then\n    echo \"fn-cluster-down: reaping serve pids: $(echo $pids | tr '\\n' ' ')\" >&2\n    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 3\n    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n    [ -z \"$pids\" ] || kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n  fi\n  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n}\n\nlog \"reap serve: coordinator\"\nresidue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\n[ \"$residue\" -eq 0 ] || log \"WARNING: $residue serve process(es) survived on the coordinator\"\nlog \"reap serve: worker\"\nresidue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\n[ \"${residue:-0}\" -eq 0 ] || log \"WARNING: ${residue:-?} serve process(es) survived on the worker\"\n\nlog \"ray stop: coordinator\"\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray stop --force >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\nlog \"ray stop: worker\"\nworker \"podman exec '$FN_CONTAINER' ray stop --force >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\"\n\nlog \"container removal: coordinator\"\npodman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\nlog \"container removal: worker\"\nworker \"podman rm -f '$FN_CONTAINER' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\"\n\nlog \"teardown complete\"\nexit 0\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-cluster-down.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2506,"output":951,"cacheRead":75776,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":143,"totalTokens":79233,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989397169,"responseId":"chatcmpl-d51b88db-2323-4238-a708-eb81981ef842","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b9027cee4fbd4ec788860e17","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 2268 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-cluster-down.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989414971},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next is fn-preflight.sh. This one's the tricky one: byte diff of env vars, latency-hold reads on both ends, per-rail link speeds, RTT probes on both rails on both ends, writing the preflight.json receipt.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now `host/fn-preflight.sh`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f6df05f7a64b4237bd1a00d7","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-preflight.sh — first-light readiness record for the pair.\n#\n#   1. byte-diff of both ranks' FN_/doctrine env — the two TP ranks must not\n#      diverge (VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY leans on the base\n#      doctrine being identical on both nodes);\n#   2. latency-hold device read on BOTH ends — /dev/cpu_dma_latency must read\n#      0 with the lowlat-cluster unit active (dotfiles-observed.md §1.2);\n#   3. per-rail link-speed record on both nodes;\n#   4. round-trip probe on both rails from both ends, against the fleet\n#      latency budget (200 us default).\n#\n# Writes results/receipts/preflight.json and exits non-zero on any hard\n# failure. Rails without a routable peer IP are recorded as such, never\n# failed over — rail 1 is trained-but-IP-unconfigured today.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nRECEIPT=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts/preflight.json\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$RECEIPT\")\"\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\nREPORT=\"$TMP/report\"\n: > \"$REPORT\"\n\nstatus=pass\nnote() { echo \"fn-preflight: $*\" >&2; }\nfail() { note \"FAIL: $*\"; status=fail; }\nrecord() { printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$1\" \"$2\" >> \"$REPORT\"; }\n\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n\n# --- 1. byte-diff of both ranks' exported doctrine env ------------------------\nENV_FILTER='^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP_|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)'\n( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n  | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$TMP/env.coordinator\"\nworker \"cat > '$TMP_REMOTE/fn-env.sh' 2>/dev/null\" < /dev/null || true  # placeholder replaced below\n\nREMOTE_TMP=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\nworker \"cat > '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nworker \"( set -a; source '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) \\\n  | grep -E '$ENV_FILTER' | LC_ALL=C sort\" > \"$TMP/env.worker\"\n\nif cmp -s \"$TMP/env.coordinator\" \"$TMP/env.worker\"; then\n  record \"env_diff\" \"pass\"\n  note \"env byte-diff: both ranks carry identical doctrine env\"\nelse\n  record \"env_diff\" \"fail\"\n  fail \"env byte-diff: the ranks' doctrine env diverges\"\n  diff \"$TMP/env.coordinator\" \"$TMP/env.worker\" >&2 || true\nfi\n\n# --- 2. latency-hold device read on both ends ---------------------------------\nlatency_hold_probe() {\n  local unit val=\"\"\n  unit=\"$(systemctl is-active lowlat-cluster 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n  if [ -r /dev/cpu_dma_latency ]; then\n    val=\"$(od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency | tr -d ' \\n')\"\n  else\n    val=\"$(sudo -n od -An -td4 /dev/cpu_dma_latency 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \\n' || true)\"\n  fi\n  printf '%s|%s' \"$unit\" \"${val:-unreadable}\"\n}\n\ncheck_hold() {  # $1 = node label, $2 = \"<unit>|<device-value>\"\n  local node=\"$1\" probe=\"$2\" unit=\"${2%%|*}\" val=\"${2##*|}\"\n  record \"hold_$node\" \"$probe\"\n  case \"$unit\" in\n    active) ;;\n    *) fail \"latency hold on $node: lowlat-cluster is '$unit' (must be active)\"; return ;;\n  esac\n  case \"$val\" in\n    0) note \"latency hold on $node: held (device reads 0)\" ;;\n    unreadable) note \"latency hold on $node: unit active but /dev/cpu_dma_latency unreadable here\" ;;\n    *) fail \"latency hold on $node: device reads '$val' (held figure is 0)\"; return ;;\n  esac\n}\n\ncheck_hold coordinator \"$(latency_hold_probe)\"\ncheck_hold worker \"$(worker \"$(declare -f latency_hold_probe); latency_hold_probe\")\"\n\n# --- 3. per-rail link-speed record, both nodes ---------------------------------\nrail_speeds() {  # emits \"<rail> <speed-Mb/s>|absent\" lines for the two rails\n  local rail\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    if [ -r \"/sys/class/net/$rail/speed\" ]; then\n      printf '%s %s\\n' \"$rail\" \"$(cat \"/sys/class/net/$rail/speed\" 2>/dev/null || echo down)\"\n    else\n      printf '%s absent\\n' \"$rail\"\n    fi\n  done\n}\nfor node in coordinator worker; do\n  if [ \"$node\" = coordinator ]; then\n    speeds=\"$(rail_speeds)\"\n  else\n    speeds=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f rail_speeds); rail_speeds\")\"\n  fi\n  while read -r rail speed; do\n    record \"speed_${node}_${rail}\" \"$speed\"\n  done <<< \"$speeds\"\ndone\n\n# --- 4. round-trip probe on both rails, from both ends -------------------------\n# Peer of a /30 endpoint flips the last octet (.1 <-> .2). A rail that does\n# not carry a routable IP cannot be probed and is recorded as no-peer-ip.\nrtt_probe_node() {\n  local rail addr peer rtt\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \\\n      | grep -Ev '^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)' | head -n1 || true)\"\n    if [ -z \"$addr\" ]; then\n      printf '%s no-peer-ip\\n' \"$rail\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    peer=\"$(printf '%s' \"$addr\" | awk -F. '{ o=$4; $4=(o==1)?2:1; printf \"%d.%d.%d.%d\", $1,$2,$3,$4 }')\"\n    rtt=\"$(ping -q -c 20 -i 0.05 -W 2 \"$peer\" 2>/dev/null | tail -n1 \\\n      | awk -F'[/ ]' '{ printf \"%d\", $8 * 1000 }')\"\n    printf '%s %s\\n' \"$rail\" \"${rtt:-probe-failed}\"\n  done\n}\nfor node in coordinator worker; do\n  if [ \"$node\" = coordinator ]; then\n    rtts=\"$(rtt_probe_node)\"\n  else\n    rtts=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f rtt_probe_node); rtt_probe_node\")\"\n  fi\n  while read -r rail rtt; do\n    record \"rtt_us_${node}_${rail}\" \"$rtt\"\n    case \"$rtt\" in\n      no-peer-ip) note \"rtt on $node/$rail: no routable peer IP, not probed\" ;;\n      probe-failed|'') fail \"rtt on $node/$rail: probe failed on a routable rail\" ;;\n      *)\n        if [ \"$rtt\" -gt \"$FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US\" ]; then\n          fail \"rtt on $node/$rail: ${rtt} us exceeds the ${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US} us budget\"\n        else\n          note \"rtt on $node/$rail: ${rtt} us inside the ${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US} us budget\"\n        fi\n        ;;\n    esac\n  done <<< \"$rtts\"\ndone\n\nrecord \"rails_chosen\" \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\"\nrecord \"budget_us\" \"$FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US\"\n\n# --- receipt --------------------------------------------------------------------\npython3 - \"$RECEIPT\" \"$status\" \"$REPORT\" <<'PY'\nimport json, sys, time\npath, status, report = sys.argv[1:4]\nkv = {}\nfor line in open(report):\n    if line.strip():\n        k, _, v = line.rstrip(\"\\n\").partition(\"\\t\")\n        kv[k] = v\nhold = {n: kv.get(\"hold_\" + n, \"unknown\").split(\"|\") for n in (\"coordinator\", \"worker\")}\nrails = {}\nfor node in (\"coordinator\", \"worker\"):\n    for rail in (\"thunderbolt0\", \"thunderbolt1\"):\n        rails.setdefault(rail, {})[node] = {\n            \"speed_mbps\": kv.get(f\"speed_{node}_{rail}\"),\n            \"rtt_us\": kv.get(f\"rtt_us_{node}_{rail}\"),\n        }\njson.dump({\"step\": \"preflight\", \"status\": status,\n           \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": {\"env_byte_diff\": kv.get(\"env_diff\"),\n                    \"rails_chosen\": kv.get(\"rails_chosen\", \"\").split(\",\"),\n                    \"latency_budget_us\": int(kv.get(\"budget_us\", \"200\")),\n                    \"latency_hold\": {n: {\"unit\": u, \"cpu_dma_latency\": v}\n                                     for n, (u, v) in hold.items()},\n                    \"rails\": rails}},\n          open(path, \"w\"), indent=1)\nPY\nnote \"receipt: $RECEIPT (status=$status)\"\n[ \"$status\" = \"pass\" ]\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3263,"output":2437,"cacheRead":76032,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":52,"totalTokens":81732,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989414977,"responseId":"chatcmpl-591fb009-ced0-4f3b-b9c1-68fea559fd22","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f6df05f7a64b4237bd1a00d7","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 7110 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989456438},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I left a buggy leftover line in fn-preflight.sh:\n\n```bash\nworker \"cat > '$TMP_REMOTE/fn-env.sh' 2>/dev/null\" < /dev/null || true  # placeholder replaced below\n```\n\nTMP_REMOTE is undefined there (and moreover under set -euo pipefail, unbound variables = death). I'll remove this extraneous line.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Removing a leftover placeholder line I accidentally included:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3db4e020a00c44bdad14c34b","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh","edits":[{"oldText":"  | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$TMP/env.coordinator\"\nworker \"cat > '$TMP_REMOTE/fn-env.sh' 2>/dev/null\" < /dev/null || true  # placeholder replaced below\n\nREMOTE_TMP=","newText":"  | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$TMP/env.coordinator\"\n\nREMOTE_TMP="}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3973,"output":262,"cacheRead":77824,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":79,"totalTokens":82059,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989456444,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b10ce82a-ec27-464d-a481-dcd77896c4bf","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3db4e020a00c44bdad14c34b","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh."}],"details":{"diff":"     ...\n  37 # --- 1. byte-diff of both ranks' exported doctrine env ------------------------\n  38 ENV_FILTER='^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP_|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)'\n  39 ( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n  40   | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$TMP/env.coordinator\"\n- 41 worker \"cat > '$TMP_REMOTE/fn-env.sh' 2>/dev/null\" < /dev/null || true  # placeholder replaced below\n  42 \n  43 REMOTE_TMP=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\n  44 worker \"cat > '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n  45 worker \"( set -a; source '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) \\\n     ...","patch":"--- /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh\n+++ /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/fn-preflight.sh\n@@ -37,9 +37,8 @@\n # --- 1. byte-diff of both ranks' exported doctrine env ------------------------\n ENV_FILTER='^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP_|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)'\n ( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n   | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$TMP/env.coordinator\"\n-worker \"cat > '$TMP_REMOTE/fn-env.sh' 2>/dev/null\" < /dev/null || true  # placeholder replaced below\n \n REMOTE_TMP=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\n worker \"cat > '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n worker \"( set -a; source '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) \\\n","firstChangedLine":41},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989463301},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next is the systemd unit. After that, scripts/run-tp2.sh, and then the tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now the systemd unit `host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_17269b6595974ae4a3f15023","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"# host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service — the flashnext pair service.\n#\n# One command stands the pair (fn-cluster-up.sh), one command tears it down\n# (fn-cluster-down.sh). Oneshot with RemainAfterExit so the unit reflects\n# \"the pair is up\" while the serve process runs detached inside its\n# container; failure cannot strand ranks because StopPost tears down even\n# when ExecStart itself failed.\n#\n# Install with `systemctl --user link host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service`\n# from the repository root, then drive explicitly:\n#   systemctl --user start flashnext-pair    # stand the pair\n#   systemctl --user stop  flashnext-pair    # tear the pair down\n#\n# Type=oneshot + RemainAfterExit: ExecStart completes once the served name\n# answers on the API port; the unit stays active while the pair serves.\n# TimeoutStartSec covers a cold-kernel-cache bring-up (~25 min of LLVM plus\n# the weight load).\n[Unit]\nDescription=flashnext pair service — TP=2 vendor FP8 release on coordinator+worker\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nRemainAfterExit=yes\nTimeoutStartSec=45min\nTimeoutStopSec=5min\nExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh\nExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n# ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails; StopPost does, so a\n# failed bring-up still tears the pair down instead of leaving ray daemons\n# and half-up containers behind a 'failed' wrapper. fn-cluster-down.sh is\n# idempotent, so the Stop+StopPost double call is deliberate and safe.\nExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\n\n# Deliberately NO [Install] section: the model takes tens of minutes to load\n# and claims both GPUs, so the stack comes up only on an explicit start by\n# the operator or the overnight overseer — never at boot, never via WantedBy.\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1866,"output":587,"cacheRead":80256,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":21,"totalTokens":82709,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989463306,"responseId":"chatcmpl-4f753fbe-2702-499d-a343-50ebaef5a064","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_17269b6595974ae4a3f15023","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 1819 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989475274},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now for the big one, scripts/run-tp2.sh. Structure:\n\n1. preflight → abort if it fails\n2. cluster up → abort if it fails\n3. Step tp2: 2 greedy 300-token completions, byte-level comparison, write tp2.json\n4. Step residency: warm-up + decode with ≥50 warm tokens, then per-node metrics, ple probe, residency.json\n5. Step fidelity: fixed prompt loss + frontier logits → results/fidelity/*.json + fidelity.json\n6. Step context: short vs 262144 context decode ratio → context.json\n\nWrite the Python heredoc carefully. Common snippet is defined per heredoc (self-contained).\n\nGreedy completion comparison: /v1/completions, temperature=0, top_p=1? Greedy = temperature 0. vLLM: temperature=0 → greedy. seed also fixed? PYTHONHASHSEED covers the hash. Determinism note: no problem.\n\nPer-node metrics Python — written to $TMP/rank_metrics.py, executed locally, and on the worker via ssh stdin.\n\nImportant point: `worker` ssh runs the command string; passing a heredoc: `ssh host python3 - < file` works (stdin passes through).\n\nResidency heredoc (coordinator driver):\n\n```python\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, time, hashlib\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\n...\ndef post(path, body, timeout): ...\n# warmup\npost(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": M, \"prompt\": \"...\", \"max_tokens\": 16, \"temperature\": 0})\n# warmed decode of >50 tokens\nr = post(..., max_tokens=64)\nwarmed = r[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"] >= 50\n```\n\nPer-node metrics via subprocess: local `subprocess.run([sys.executable, metrics_path])`, remote `subprocess.run([\"ssh\", WORKER, \"python3\", \"-\"], stdin=metrics bytes)`.\n\nple probe: read serve.log, count occurrences of 'ple_mmap'.\n\ntable_gpu_resident_bytes = 0 if ple_engaged and VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1, else None.\n\nResidency receipt data:\n{\n \"read_after_warmed_decode\": warmed(bool),\n \"warmed_decode_tokens\": n,\n \"gtt_gib_per_rank\": {\"coordinator\": g, \"worker\": g},\n \"gtt_source\": {...},\n \"rss_gib_per_rank\": {...},\n \"table_page_cache\": {\"files_matched\": n, \"cached_gib\": g|None, \"tool\": \"fincore\"|None},\n \"table_gpu_resident_bytes\": 0|None,\n \"ple_engagement_log_lines\": count\n}\n\nIf the gtt value is None (sysfs file absent) → omit the rank key from gtt_gib_per_rank? Receipt verification skips missing ones. Include null? In that case does the comparison gib > 80 fail? It would iterate with gib=None → None > 80 gives TypeError. So only include ranks with numeric values. OK.\n\nFidelity heredoc: 3 prompts, each:\nbody = {\"model\": M, \"prompt\": p, \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0, \"logprobs\": 5}\nresp choices[0]: text, logprobs: {\"tokens\":[...], \"token_logprobs\":[...], \"top_logprobs\":[...]}\nloss = -mean of token_logprobs (skip None).\nfrontier = top_logprobs[-1] (top 5 of the last generated token) + final token.\nSave results/fidelity/fidelity-{i}.json; sha256.\nReceipt fidelity.json {\"files\": [...], \"sha256\": {...}, \"prompts\": 3, \"losses\": [...]}.\nstatus pass if all requests succeeded.\n\nContext heredoc:\n- Streaming SSE helper:\n```python\ndef stream(body):\n    body[\"stream\"]=True\n    req=...\n    t0=time.time(); t_first=None; t_last=t0; tokens=0; text=[]; usage=None\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line=raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"): continue\n            payload=line[5:].strip()\n            if payload==\"[DONE]\": break\n            chunk=json.loads(payload)\n            now=time.time()\n            if chunk[\"choices\"] and chunk[\"choices\"][0].get(\"text\"):\n                if t_first is None: t_first=now\n                t_last=now\n                text.append(chunk[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"])\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"): usage=chunk[\"usage\"]\n    return ...\n```\ndecode_tps = (completion_tokens-1)/(t_last-t_first) — completion_tokens from usage (use stream_options {\"include_usage\": true}). Fallback: len of text chunks.\n\n- Short arm: fixed prompt.\n- Long arm: repeat a paragraph to build a long prompt: estimated 4.2 chars/token: chars = (target-192)*4.2; then request; check usage.prompt_tokens; if less than target-1024, scale by (target-192)/actual to pad and retry once.\n- ratio = long_tps/short_tps. Receipt context.json:\n{\"target_context\": 262144, \"short_prompt_tokens\":..., \"long_prompt_tokens\":..., \"short_decode_tps\":..., \"long_decode_tps\":..., \"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\": ratio}\nstatus pass if ratio is computable; the bound 0.9 is judged by receipts-verify.\n\nNote: request timeout for the long prompt: prefill of 262K tokens can take several minutes → timeout 3600.\n\nOverall run-tp2.sh: set -euo pipefail; on failure after receipt is written — the receipt remains. Use a helper run_step that emits logs.\n\nReceipt paths: results/receipts/{tp2,residency,fidelity,context}.json.\n\nWriting it now. Note: heredoc python needs environment variables FN_PORT, FN_WORKER_HOST, FN_MODEL_DIR, FN_STATE_DIR, FN_SERVED_NAME — exported via source of fn-env.sh. TMP directory passed as argv.\n\nOne subtle point: in bash heredoc `<<'PY'` prevents expansion — suitable for python.\n\nFixed prompt: define once in bash, pass to heredoc via env: FN_FIXED_PROMPT. But multi-line env via export is fine too.\n\nDefine prompts inside python as constants — simpler, single source of truth per heredoc; but tp2 + fidelity + context each need their own fixed prompt; make each of them constants within their own heredoc. That's fine.\n\nMetrics file $TMP/rank_metrics.py — contents:\n\n```python\nimport fnmatch, glob, json, os, shutil, subprocess\n\ndef first_int(paths):\n    for p in paths:\n        try:\n            return int(open(p).read().strip())\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n    return None\n\ndef gtt():\n    for name, label in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\", \"gtt\"), (\"mem_info_vram_used\", \"vram\")):\n        v = first_int(sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\" + name)))\n        if v is not None:\n            return v, label\n    return None, None\n\ndef rss_bytes():\n    total = 0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit():\n            continue\n        try:\n            cmd = open(\"/proc/%s/cmdline\" % pid, \"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" not in cmd and \"ray::\" not in cmd:\n            continue\n        try:\n            status = open(\"/proc/%s/status\" % pid).read()\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        for line in status.splitlines():\n            if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                total += int(line.split()[1]) * 1024\n    return total\n\ndef table_cache():\n    patterns = [p.strip().lower() for p in\n                os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\", \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").split(\",\") if p.strip()]\n    root = os.environ.get(\"FN_MODEL_DIR\", \"/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8\")\n    matched = []\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), pat) for pat in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath, f))\n    fincore = shutil.which(\"fincore\")\n    pages = 0\n    ok = False\n    if fincore:\n        ok = True\n        for m in matched:\n            try:\n                out = subprocess.run([fincore, m], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False).stdout\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            for line in out.splitlines():\n                parts = line.split()\n                if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].isdigit():\n                    pages += int(parts[1])\n                    break\n    return {\"files_matched\": len(matched), \"files\": matched[:8],\n            \"cached_pages\": pages if ok else None,\n            \"tool\": \"fincore\" if ok else None}\n\ngb, src = gtt()\nprint(json.dumps({\"gtt_bytes\": gb, \"gtt_source\": src,\n                  \"rss_bytes\": rss_bytes(), \"table\": table_cache()}))\n```\n\nNote: parsing fincore output: `fincore` prints a header line \"RES PAGES SIZE SIZE% FILE\", then one line of data per file. Header parts[1] is \"PAGES\", not a digit → gets skipped. Good.\n\nThe driver python for residency imports this via subprocess and assembles it.\n\nNow writing run-tp2.sh. Note: ssh from python: subprocess.run([\"ssh\", worker, \"python3\", \"-\"], input=metrics_src.encode(), capture_output=True). Good.\n\nReceipt verification's _residency: gtt_gib_per_rank values > 80 → fail; table_gpu_resident_bytes != 0 → fail; read_after_warmed_decode is false → fail.\n\nAlso the receipts for steps \"fidelity\"/\"context\" have no bounds beyond what's enumerated (_context: decode_ratio_vs_short_context < 0.9 → fail) — my key names match.\n\nNow writing the file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now the first-light runner `scripts/run-tp2.sh`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b6166a14aff34528b92dbd27","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/run-tp2.sh — flashnext first-light runner on the pair.\n#\n# Sequence (spec claims 4.2–4.5):\n#   preflight -> pair up -> two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared\n#   (tp2.json) -> residency read after 50 warmed decode tokens (residency.json,\n#   ruling P11) -> fidelity baseline: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits\n#   under results/ (fidelity.json) -> the 262144-context probe (context.json).\n#\n# Every receipt is one JSON object {\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"} exactly as\n# scripts/receipts-verify.py expects. A step that cannot complete writes a\n# fail receipt (graded, never silently skipped — spec F.12) and the runner\n# exits non-zero.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts\"\nFIDELITY_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/fidelity\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\n\nlog() { echo \"run-tp2: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# --- preflight, then stand the pair -------------------------------------------\nlog \"preflight\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-preflight.sh\"\n\nlog \"standing the pair service\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n\nexport FN_API=\"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT\"\n\n# --- step tp2: two greedy 300-token completions, byte-compared -----------------\nlog \"step tp2: greedy byte-compare\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nPROMPT = (\"The pair boots before the operator arrives, and the ledger \"\n          \"records what the night proved: \")\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\ndef greedy_300():\n    return post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": PROMPT,\n                                    \"max_tokens\": 300, \"temperature\": 0})\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    a, b = greedy_300(), greedy_300()\n    ta = a[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    tb = b[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = ta == tb\n    data[\"completion_bytes\"] = len(ta)\n    data[\"completion_tokens\"] = a[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\n    if ta != tb:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = False\n\n# Fold the preflight record into the tp2 receipt (U.2: held sub-budget round\n# trips on both ends ride with the tp2 claim).\ntry:\n    pre = json.load(open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"preflight.json\")))\n    data[\"preflight\"] = pre[\"data\"]\nexcept Exception as e:\n    data[\"preflight\"] = f\"unreadable: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"tp2\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"tp2.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: tp2 receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step residency: read after 50 warmed decode tokens, per ruling P11 --------\nlog \"step residency: warmed decode, then per-rank readings\"\ncat > \"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\" <<'PY'\n\"\"\"Per-rank residency probes: GTT from sysfs, process RSS, table page-cache\nresidency. Stdlib only; runs on either node (local or over ssh stdin).\"\"\"\nimport fnmatch, glob, json, os, shutil, subprocess\n\ndef first_int(paths):\n    for p in paths:\n        try:\n            return int(open(p).read().strip())\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n    return None\n\ndef gtt():\n    # GTT usage first; on the unified-memory APU the VRAM reading is the\n    # fallback (patch 0004 re-points VRAM reporting at unified memory).\n    for name, label in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\", \"gtt\"),\n                        (\"mem_info_vram_used\", \"vram\")):\n        v = first_int(sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\" + name)))\n        if v is not None:\n            return v, label\n    return None, None\n\ndef rss_bytes():\n    total = 0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit():\n            continue\n        try:\n            cmd = open(\"/proc/%s/cmdline\" % pid, \"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" not in cmd and \"ray::\" not in cmd:\n            continue\n        try:\n            status = open(\"/proc/%s/status\" % pid).read()\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        for line in status.splitlines():\n            if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                total += int(line.split()[1]) * 1024\n    return total\n\ndef table_cache():\n    patterns = [p.strip().lower() for p in\n                os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\", \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").split(\",\")\n                if p.strip()]\n    root = os.environ.get(\"FN_MODEL_DIR\", \"/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8\")\n    matched = []\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), pat) for pat in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath, f))\n    fincore = shutil.which(\"fincore\")\n    pages, ok = 0, False\n    if fincore:\n        ok = True\n        for m in matched:\n            out = subprocess.run([fincore, m], capture_output=True, text=True,\n                                 check=False).stdout\n            for line in out.splitlines():\n                parts = line.split()\n                if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].isdigit():\n                    pages += int(parts[1])\n                    break\n    return {\"files_matched\": len(matched), \"files\": matched[:8],\n            \"cached_pages\": pages if ok else None,\n            \"tool\": \"fincore\" if ok else None}\n\ngtt_bytes, gtt_src = gtt()\nprint(json.dumps({\"gtt_bytes\": gtt_bytes, \"gtt_source\": gtt_src,\n                  \"rss_bytes\": rss_bytes(), \"table\": table_cache()}))\nPY\n\nexport FN_METRICS=\"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nWORKER = os.environ[\"FN_WORKER_HOST\"]\nSERVE_LOG = os.path.join(os.environ[\"FN_STATE_DIR\"], \"serve.log\")\nMETRICS = os.environ[\"FN_METRICS\"]\nPAGE = 4096\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    # Compile pass, then a decode long enough to be called warmed (>50 tokens).\n    post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                             \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path: \",\n                             \"max_tokens\": 16, \"temperature\": 0})\n    r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                                 \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path further: \",\n                                 \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0})\n    warmed = int(r[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"])\n    data[\"warmed_decode_tokens\"] = warmed\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = warmed >= 50\n    if warmed < 50:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = False\n    data[\"error_warmup\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\nmetrics_src = open(METRICS).read()\ndef probe(node):\n    if node == \"coordinator\":\n        out = subprocess.run([sys.executable, METRICS], capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    else:\n        out = subprocess.run([\"ssh\", WORKER, \"python3\", \"-\"],\n                             input=metrics_src.encode(), capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    return json.loads(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])\n\ntry:\n    per_rank = {n: probe(n) for n in (\"coordinator\", \"worker\")}\n    data[\"gtt_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"gtt_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2)\n        for n, m in per_rank.items() if m[\"gtt_bytes\"] is not None}\n    data[\"gtt_source_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: m[\"gtt_source\"] for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"rss_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"rss_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2) for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"table_page_cache\"] = {\n        n: {**per_rank[n][\"table\"],\n            \"cached_gib\": (None if per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] is None\n                           else round(per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] * PAGE / 2**30, 2))}\n        for n in per_rank}\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error_metrics\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\n# The mmap engagement probe: zero table bytes GPU-resident only if the table\n# path actually engaged in this run's own serve log under VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1.\ntry:\n    log_text = open(SERVE_LOG, errors=\"ignore\").read().lower()\n    engaged = (\"ple_mmap\" in log_text\n               and os.environ.get(\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP\") == \"1\")\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = 0 if engaged else None\n    data[\"ple_mmap_log_lines\"] = log_text.count(\"ple_mmap\")\n    if not engaged:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = None\n    data[\"error_ple_probe\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"residency\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"residency.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: residency receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step fidelity: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits ---------------------\nlog \"step fidelity: reference losses and frontier logits\"\nexport FIDELITY_DIR\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nOUT_DIR = os.environ[\"FIDELITY_DIR\"]\nPROMPTS = [\n    \"The fleet measures before it claims, because \",\n    \"A receipt is a fact the morning operator can check: \",\n    \"Two ranks agree byte for byte when \",\n]\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\nfiles, hashes, losses = [], {}, []\nfor i, prompt in enumerate(PROMPTS):\n    name = f\"fidelity-{i}.json\"\n    path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, name)\n    try:\n        r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                                     \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0,\n                                     \"logprobs\": 5})\n        choice = r[\"choices\"][0]\n        token_logprobs = [x for x in choice[\"logprobs\"][\"token_logprobs\"]\n                          if x is not None]\n        record = {\n            \"model\": MODEL,\n            \"prompt\": prompt,\n            \"text\": choice[\"text\"],\n            \"tokens\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"],\n            # Reference loss: mean negative log-likelihood of the fixed\n            # continuation under the as-served stack.\n            \"loss_nll\": -sum(token_logprobs) / len(token_logprobs),\n            # Frontier logits: the top-5 at the final generated token — the\n            # yardstick later changes must not move without a reason.\n            \"frontier_logits\": {\n                \"token\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"][-1],\n                \"top5\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"top_logprobs\"][-1],\n            },\n        }\n        losses.append(record[\"loss_nll\"])\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    except Exception as e:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        record = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                  \"error\": f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"}\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    files.append(name)\n    hashes[name] = hashlib.sha256(open(path, \"rb\").read()).hexdigest()\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"fidelity\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": {\"files\": files, \"sha256\": hashes, \"losses_nll\": losses,\n                    \"prompts\": len(PROMPTS)}},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"fidelity.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: fidelity receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step context: the 262144-context probe -------------------------------------\nlog \"step context: decode at full context vs short context\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nTARGET = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CONTEXT_TARGET\", \"262144\"))\nDECODE = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CTX_DECODE\", \"128\"))\n# Claim 4.5's given context depth. The prompt is synthesized from a fixed\n# paragraph: tokenization is engine-side, so we verify the actual prompt\n# tokens the server reports and re-pad once if we undershot.\nPARAGRAPH = (\"The ledger records what the night proved. Every receipt is a \"\n             \"fact the morning operator can check. The pair serves from its \"\n             \"own drives; the rails carry tensors and the wire carries \"\n             \"control. Nothing is narrated that was not measured. \")\n\ndef stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens, timeout=7200):\n    body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt, \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n            \"temperature\": 0, \"stream\": True,\n            \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True}}\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + \"/v1/completions\",\n                                 data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    t_first = t_last = None\n    usage = None\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[5:].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                if choice.get(\"text\"):\n                    now = time.time()\n                    if t_first is None:\n                        t_first = now\n                    t_last = now\n    return usage, t_first, t_last\n\ndef decode_tps(prompt, max_tokens):\n    usage, t_first, t_last = stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens)\n    if not usage or t_first is None or t_last is None or t_last <= t_first:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"no usable decode window in the streamed response\")\n    tokens = usage[\"completion_tokens\"]\n    return (tokens - 1) / (t_last - t_first), usage[\"prompt_tokens\"]\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {\"target_context\": TARGET}\ntry:\n    short_tps, short_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(\n        \"Measure the short-context decode: \" + PARAGRAPH, DECODE)\n    data[\"short_prompt_tokens\"] = short_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"short_decode_tps\"] = round(short_tps, 3)\n\n    est_chars_per_token = 4.2  # conservative: undershoot tokens, never the cap\n    long_prompt = PARAGRAPH * int(((TARGET - 2 * DECODE) * est_chars_per_token)\n                                  / len(PARAGRAPH) + 1)\n    long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:  # undershot: re-pad once, exactly\n        long_prompt = long_prompt * int((TARGET - DECODE) / max(long_prompt_tokens, 1)) + PARAGRAPH\n        long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    data[\"long_prompt_tokens\"] = long_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"long_decode_tps\"] = round(long_tps, 3)\n    data[\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\"] = round(long_tps / short_tps, 4)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        data[\"error\"] = f\"server accepted only {long_prompt_tokens} prompt tokens\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"context\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"context.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: context receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\nlog \"first light complete; receipts: $RECEIPTS/{tp2,residency,fidelity,context}.json\"\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1880,"output":7042,"cacheRead":80896,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2320,"totalTokens":89818,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989475279,"responseId":"chatcmpl-6986bf04-fa80-477e-9f9d-5a47cb7b09e4","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b6166a14aff34528b92dbd27","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 16627 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989597796},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait a sec — in run-tp2.sh, the python heredoc references the environment variable RECEIPTS, but we never once export RECEIPTS inside run-tp2.sh. RECEIPTS is a bash variable, and `export FN_API` is done but `export RECEIPTS` isn't. We need to export RECEIPTS (and FIDELITY_DIR is already exported before the fidelity step — good). Let's add `export RECEIPTS` after the mkdir. Also, the heredoc uses os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"] — needs to be exported. Fix.\n\nFurthermore, in the context step heredoc, FN_CONTEXT_TARGET and FN_CTX_DECODE are referenced via os.environ.get — no problem (defaults exist).\n\nAlso inside the tp2 heredoc: `pre[\"data\"]` — the data in preflight.json is written by preflight. No problem.\n\nFixing the export.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Fixing a missing export — the Python heredocs read `RECEIPTS` from the environment:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9f36e0322e4e4de7895f7cb7","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"mkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\nexport RECEIPTS\n","oldText":"mkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\n"}],"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":7961,"output":342,"cacheRead":81920,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":191,"totalTokens":90223,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989597802,"responseId":"chatcmpl-6032022c-abb4-4014-95bc-1a3565089fcb","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9f36e0322e4e4de7895f7cb7","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh."}],"details":{"diff":"     ...\n  20 # shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\n  21 source \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n  22 \n  23 mkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\n+ 24 export RECEIPTS\n  24 TMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n  25 trap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\n  26 \n  27 log() { echo \"run-tp2: $*\" >&2; }\n     ...","patch":"--- /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh\n+++ /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/scripts/run-tp2.sh\n@@ -20,8 +20,9 @@\n # shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\n source \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n \n mkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\n+export RECEIPTS\n TMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n trap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\n \n log() { echo \"run-tp2: $*\" >&2; }\n","firstChangedLine":24},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989606558},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next up is the test file, tests/test_host_tooling.py.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now `tests/test_host_tooling.py`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_49700bc1ae71437c9154ddcc","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"\"\"\"Host-tooling doctrine tests — the env file, the unit file, script hygiene.\n\nThe host-doctrine grep gate is satisfiable by wrong code (handoff/ROUTING.md\nnames this task the judged case), so these assertions aim at the semantic\ntraps a grep cannot catch: the rail list is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`\nrather than hardcoded (a peerless rail 1 in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL\nbootstrap), NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 is unconditional (the ibverbs devices exist on\nboth nodes by design), no probe-read engine default is exported (spec F.9),\nthe unit tears down via ExecStopPost and installs at no boot target (spec\nclaim 6.1), and the bring-up reaps before it gates.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport pathlib\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nHOST = ROOT / \"host\"\nENV_FILE = HOST / \"fn-env.sh\"\nUNIT_FILE = HOST / \"systemd\" / \"flashnext-pair.service\"\nRUNNER = ROOT / \"scripts\" / \"run-tp2.sh\"\n\n# Engine defaults read through is-set probes (spec F.9): exporting any of\n# these — even \"just\" the default value — diverts the oracle into a hard\n# raise. They must never appear as exports in the doctrine env.\nPROBE_READ_DEFAULTS = (\n    \"VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM\",\n    \"VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM\",\n    \"VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER\",\n)\n\n\nclass HostTooling(unittest.TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.env = ENV_FILE.read_text()\n        cls.unit = UNIT_FILE.read_text()\n        cls.up = (HOST / \"fn-cluster-up.sh\").read_text()\n        cls.runner = RUNNER.read_text()\n\n    def test_fn_env_carries_the_doctrine_lines(self):\n        for needle in (\n            \"export PYTHONHASHSEED=0\",\n            \"expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85\",\n            \"HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\",\n            \"TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\",\n            \"TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\",\n            \"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\",\n            \"export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\",\n            \"export VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\",\n            \"export VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\",\n            \"thunderbolt0\",\n        ):\n            self.assertIn(needle, self.env,\n                          f\"doctrine line missing from fn-env.sh: {needle}\")\n\n    def test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded(self):\n        # The rail list must be computed from `ip -br addr` at env time; the\n        # two-rail hardcode is exactly the wrong code that passes the grep.\n        self.assertIn(\"ip -br\", self.env)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt0,thunderbolt1\",\n                         self.env)\n        self.assertNotRegex(\n            self.env,\n            r\"^\\s*export NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt\",\n            \"NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME must be computed per node, never hardcoded\")\n        # A rail without a routable peer IP must be excluded, and the choice\n        # must be logged.\n        self.assertIn(\"169.254\", self.env)\n\n    def test_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional(self):\n        # Unconditional means a bare export — no device-detection guard, no\n        # ${...:-} fallback that a future edit could point elsewhere.\n        self.assertRegex(self.env, r\"^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\\s*$\",\n                         \"NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 must be an unconditional export\")\n\n    def test_fn_env_never_exports_probe_read_defaults(self):\n        for name in PROBE_READ_DEFAULTS:\n            self.assertNotRegex(\n                self.env, rf\"^\\s*export {name}=\",\n                f\"spec F.9: {name} is read through an is-set probe; \"\n                \"exporting the default diverts the oracle into a hard raise\")\n\n    def test_fn_env_cache_dirs_are_not_tmpfs(self):\n        self.assertNotRegex(self.env, r\"CACHE_DIR=[^ ]*/tmp\\b\",\n                            \"compiler caches under /tmp recompile on reboot\")\n        self.assertIn(\"FN_STATE_DIR\", self.env)\n\n    def test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free(self):\n        self.assertIn(\"Type=oneshot\", self.unit)\n        self.assertIn(\"RemainAfterExit=yes\", self.unit)\n        self.assertIn(\"ExecStopPost=\", self.unit)\n        self.assertIn(\"fn-cluster-down.sh\", self.unit)\n        # No boot-time install: the stack claims both GPUs for tens of\n        # minutes and comes up only on an explicit start (spec claim 6.1).\n        self.assertNotIn(\"WantedBy\", self.unit)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"[Install]\", self.unit)\n\n    def test_all_host_scripts_and_runner_are_syntax_clean(self):\n        for sh in sorted(HOST.glob(\"*.sh\")) + [RUNNER]:\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True,\n                           capture_output=True)\n\n    def test_cluster_up_reaps_before_it_gates(self):\n        # Reap-then-gate order is load-bearing: stale husks hold the port.\n        self.assertLess(self.up.index(\"reap_serve_node\"),\n                        self.up.index(\"ray start\"))\n        self.assertIn(\"zero residue\", self.up)\n        self.assertIn(\"two-GPU gate\", self.up)\n        # Worker-side actions ride the wire fleet identity, never a rail.\n        self.assertIn(\"10.99.9.2\", self.env)\n\n    def test_runner_writes_the_four_graded_receipts(self):\n        for step in (\"tp2\", \"residency\", \"fidelity\", \"context\"):\n            self.assertIn(f'\"step\": \"{step}\"', self.runner.replace(\"'\", '\"'),\n                          f\"run-tp2.sh does not write the {step} receipt\")\n        self.assertIn(\"fn-preflight.sh\", self.runner)\n        self.assertIn(\"fn-cluster-up.sh\", self.runner)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_host_tooling.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1199,"output":1496,"cacheRead":89088,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":14,"totalTokens":91783,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989606563,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8981826e-9f70-4f3f-b1da-b82c3a28f09a","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_49700bc1ae71437c9154ddcc","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 5404 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_host_tooling.py"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989632986},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Make the script executable and run the acceptance criteria:\n\n1. chmod +x host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh\n2. bash -n on everything\n3. The entire acceptance argv\n4. python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v\n5. The full unittest discover (repo-tests gate) to confirm nothing regressed\n6. receipts-verify is still clean\n\nOne concern regarding test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded: `assertNotRegex(self.env, r\"^\\s*export NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=thunderbolt\")` — my line is `export NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"` — starts with `export NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${...` — does not match the `=thunderbolt` pattern. Good.\n\nAlso `assertIn(\"169.254\", self.env)` — it's present. Good.\n\ntest_cluster_up_reaps_before_it_gates: \"reap_serve_node\" appears at definition time — before \"ray start\". Wait, does \"ray start\" appear inside a comment before the reap definition? Header comment: \"3. Ray head on the coordinator, worker join\" — no \"ray start\" literal in the header. First occurrence of \"ray start\" literal: the actual command `podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head`. Occurrences of \"reap_serve_node\": function definition (after the header). Header has \"REAP stranded serve processes\" but no literal \"reap_serve_node\". Order OK: function definition is around line 30, before the ray start line.\n\n\"zero residue\" — appears in `log \"reap gate passed: zero residue on both nodes\"`. Good.\n\"two-GPU gate\" — appears in log + comments. Good.\n\nrunner.replace(\"'\", '\"') — my heredoc uses double quotes `\"step\": \"tp2\"` in python, so the literal `\"step\": \"tp2\"` exists directly in the file. Good.\n\nNow I'll run the checks.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Making scripts executable and running the acceptance checks:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_238bc216af264001b5aae54b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && chmod +x host/fn-env.sh host/fn-cluster-up.sh host/fn-cluster-down.sh host/fn-preflight.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh && for f in host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh; do bash -n \"$f\" && echo \"OK $f\"; done"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3143,"output":573,"cacheRead":88704,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":437,"totalTokens":92420,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989632992,"responseId":"chatcmpl-98d68344-9f0a-439c-80c3-c8acf4592362","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_238bc216af264001b5aae54b","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"OK host/fn-cluster-down.sh\nOK host/fn-cluster-up.sh\nOK host/fn-env.sh\nOK host/fn-preflight.sh\nOK scripts/run-tp2.sh\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989645405},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, let's run the complete acceptance argv and the tests.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b692303ede874f24a9010e77","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && bash -euc \"for f in host/*.sh scripts/run-tp2.sh; do bash -n \\\"\\$f\\\"; done && grep -q 'PYTHONHASHSEED=0' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'thunderbolt0' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_' host/fn-env.sh && grep -q 'ExecStopPost' host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service && ! grep -q 'WantedBy' host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service && python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v\" && echo ACCEPTANCE-PASS"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2361,"output":250,"cacheRead":90112,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":13,"totalTokens":92723,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989645411,"responseId":"chatcmpl-85b931c1-3878-44e8-95ca-7924976a5232","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b692303ede874f24a9010e77","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(no output)\n\nCommand exited with code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1787989651556},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Something failed. Let's run each part individually to find the failure location. Watch out for the possibility that `bash -euc` swallows the output. Let's try debugging.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Acceptance failed — let me isolate which clause:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_5126bf376db6450ba422c2b3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling -v 2>&1 | tail -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":589,"output":131,"cacheRead":92160,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":37,"totalTokens":92880,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989651562,"responseId":"chatcmpl-1ca290cb-98f9-486a-9c72-68673cf6d9b8","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_5126bf376db6450ba422c2b3","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"----------------------------------------------------------------------\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_host_tooling.py\", line 70, in test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded\n    self.assertIn(\"169.254\", self.env)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAssertionError: '169.254' not found in '#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\\n#\\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: the ray head, the ray worker, `vllm\\n# serve`, and the bring-up/teardown/preflight scripts. Both TP ranks MUST see\\n# the same doctrine — fn-preflight.sh byte-diffs the exported env across the\\n# pair before first light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below\\n# is what carries FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray\\'s default\\n# copy prefixes; without it the two TP ranks diverge).\\n#\\n# Discipline lifted from the ds4-vllm estate\\'s host/ds4-cluster-env.sh\\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet\\'s\\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1–2).\\n# Safe to source repeatedly; every default is override-able via the environment.\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: NEVER export an environment default the engine reads\\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If a knob here\\n# looks like \"just pinning the default\", it is not that — leave it unset.\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\n# --- the FN_ configuration surface (propagated to the worker rank by ray) --\\nexport FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"\\nexport FN_MODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\\nexport FN_SERVED_NAME=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\\nexport FN_PORT=\"${FN_PORT:-1234}\"\\nexport FN_RAY_PORT=\"${FN_RAY_PORT:-6379}\"\\nexport FN_MAX_CTX=\"${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}\"\\nexport FN_GPU_UTIL=\"${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}\"\\nexport FN_IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\\nexport FN_CONTAINER=\"${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}\"\\n# Worker-side actions ride the ethernet wire on the STABLE fleet identity,\\n# never a 10.99.0.x Thunderbolt rail — the rails carry tensors only\\n# (dotfiles-observed.md §2.3: admin traffic prefers the 5GbE cable).\\nexport FN_WORKER_HOST=\"${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}\"\\nexport FN_HEAD_IP=\"${FN_HEAD_IP:-10.99.9.1}\"\\n# The fleet latency budget from modules/lowlat-cluster.nix: 200 us sits well\\n# above the measured held figure and well below the unheld one.\\nexport FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=\"${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}\"\\n\\n# --- determinism -------------------------------------------------------------\\n# vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from PYTHONHASHSEED:\\n# without a fixed value identical token content produces different block\\n# filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart.\\n# The greedy byte-compare in run-tp2.sh leans on this too.\\nexport PYTHONHASHSEED=0\\n\\n# --- allocator ---------------------------------------------------------------\\n# GC is off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB means reclaim starts at\\n# ~105 GiB. expandable_segments is the fragmentation half. Safe with\\n# --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs.\\nexport PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=\"${PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF:-expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85}\"\\n\\n# --- HSA ---------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) otherwise, i.e.\\n# ~2-3 cores spinning during inference and CPU thermal throttling on a pair\\n# whose PM QoS is held for latency. Set FN_HSA_INTERRUPT=0 to revert to spin.\\nexport HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\"${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}\"\\n\\n# --- compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs ------------------\\n# The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bring-up after a boot\\n# then recompiles every kernel — ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can\\n# answer. The state directory is on-disk on both nodes.\\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/triton\" 2>/dev/null || true\\nexport TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\"${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor}\"\\nexport TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\"${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/triton}\"\\n\\n# --- NCCL: the transport-of-record block --------------------------------------\\n# Socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record (spec\\n# ruling P7); the RDMA package under host/rdma is operator-attended only, and\\n# only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked.\\n#\\n# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`: only Thunderbolt rails\\n# that actually carry a routable /30 IP qualify. Rail 0 (thunderbolt0,\\n# 10.99.0.x) is guaranteed; rail 1 (thunderbolt1) is trained but\\n# IP-unconfigured today (dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix parks it) and\\n# MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP — a peerless link-local rail in\\n# the list hangs RCCL bootstrap. We log which rails we chose rather than\\n# hardcoding both.\\nfn_choose_rails() {\\n  local rail addr chosen=\"\"\\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\\n    if [ ! -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ]; then\\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent on this node; not listed\" >&2\\n      continue\\n    fi\\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\\\\n      | grep -oE \\'([0-9]{1,3}\\\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}\\' \\\\\\n      | grep -Ev \\'^(169\\\\.254\\\\.|127\\\\.)\\' | head -n1 || true)\"\\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME ($addr is routable)\" >&2\\n    else\\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\\n    fi\\n  done\\n  printf \\'%s\\' \"$chosen\"\\n}\\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"\\nif [ -z \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ]; then\\n  echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no Thunderbolt rail carries a routable IP; refusing to name a phantom transport\" >&2\\n  return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1\\nfi\\n\\n# NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY. Since the pre-arm bake an ibverbs device\\n# exists on the rails of BOTH nodes (usb4_rdma0 + usb4_rdma5, by design);\\n# without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA.\\n# Sockets stay the transport of record until the attended morning A/B lands a\\n# counterbalanced verdict (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md). Do NOT conditionalize\\n# this on device detection — the devices are present by design.\\nexport NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\\n\\n# Cold-kernel-cache bring-up is ~25 min of LLVM before the first collective;\\n# the collective timeouts must survive that without a watchdog kill.\\nexport TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=\"${TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-2400}\"\\nexport TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=\"${TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING:-0}\"\\nexport NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=\"${NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS:-2400000}\"\\n\\n# --- ray ---------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Propagate FN_* to the worker rank through ray. ESSENTIAL: without this the\\n# two TP ranks diverge on every FN_ knob above.\\nexport VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\\n# Ray and ROCr visible-device handling fight on multi-rank boxes; opt out.\\nexport RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"${RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-1}\"\\n# On a 128 GB unified-memory box the serve legitimately claims most memory;\\n# ray\\'s OOM monitor must not reap the rank. Refresh 0 disables the monitor.\\nexport RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=\"${RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms:-0}\"\\n# `ray start` pre-starts one idle python worker per CPU (~40 MB each); on a\\n# 32-thread box that is over a gigabyte vLLM never touches. Cap the pool\\n# small; fn-cluster-up.sh passes this as --num-cpus on both nodes.\\nexport RAY_NUM_CPUS=\"${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}\"\\n\\n# --- the table path ------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Serve the engram table from NVMe via mmap: page-cache faults serve gathers,\\n# zero table bytes GPU-resident (spec ruling P11\\'s zero-GPU-residency bound\\n# leans on this). VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 is the fork\\'s kill switch.\\nexport VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\\n\\n# --- estate hygiene -------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Runtime hub downloads stay forbidden (spec F.6): the checkpoint is staged\\n# library-to-node, and the engine must never reach for the hub at runtime.\\nexport HF_HUB_OFFLINE=\"${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}\"\\nexport VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=\"${VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS:-1}\"\\nexport VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=\"${VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK:-1}\"\\n'\n\n======================================================================\nFAIL: test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free (tests.test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_host_tooling.py\", line 97, in test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free\n    self.assertNotIn(\"WantedBy\", self.unit)\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAssertionError: 'WantedBy' unexpectedly found in '# host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service — the flashnext pair service.\\n#\\n# One command stands the pair (fn-cluster-up.sh), one command tears it down\\n# (fn-cluster-down.sh). Oneshot with RemainAfterExit so the unit reflects\\n# \"the pair is up\" while the serve process runs detached inside its\\n# container; failure cannot strand ranks because StopPost tears down even\\n# when ExecStart itself failed.\\n#\\n# Install with `systemctl --user link host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service`\\n# from the repository root, then drive explicitly:\\n#   systemctl --user start flashnext-pair    # stand the pair\\n#   systemctl --user stop  flashnext-pair    # tear the pair down\\n#\\n# Type=oneshot + RemainAfterExit: ExecStart completes once the served name\\n# answers on the API port; the unit stays active while the pair serves.\\n# TimeoutStartSec covers a cold-kernel-cache bring-up (~25 min of LLVM plus\\n# the weight load).\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=flashnext pair service — TP=2 vendor FP8 release on coordinator+worker\\nAfter=network-online.target\\nWants=network-online.target\\n\\n[Service]\\nType=oneshot\\nRemainAfterExit=yes\\nTimeoutStartSec=45min\\nTimeoutStopSec=5min\\nExecStart=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-up.sh\\nExecStop=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\\n# ExecStop does not run when ExecStart itself fails; StopPost does, so a\\n# failed bring-up still tears the pair down instead of leaving ray daemons\\n# and half-up containers behind a \\'failed\\' wrapper. fn-cluster-down.sh is\\n# idempotent, so the Stop+StopPost double call is deliberate and safe.\\nExecStopPost=%h/mecattaf/flashnext/host/fn-cluster-down.sh\\n\\n# Deliberately NO [Install] section: the model takes tens of minutes to load\\n# and claims both GPUs, so the stack comes up only on an explicit start by\\n# the operator or the overnight overseer — never at boot, never via WantedBy.\\n'\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 9 tests in 0.011s\n\nFAILED (failures=3)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989656387},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There are three problems (three failures — the third one was cut off, let's check everything):\n\n1. `test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded`: \"169.254\" is not found — my fn-env.sh uses the escaped form `169\\.254\\.` inside grep -Ev. That assertion is overly strict on my own test side: the env file contains `169\\.254` (escaped dots). Either fix the test to check for \"169\", or just delete that assertion… Actually the semantic intent is: link-local filtering exists. Check for `169\\\\.254` — the raw text contains `169\\.254`. assertIn(\"169\", ...) passes. Let's check for `grep -Ev '^(169`… More simply: assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\", self.env) — Python string \"169\\\\.254\" = literal `169\\.254`. This will match.\n\n2. `test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free`: \"WantedBy\" is found — it's in my own comment (\"never via WantedBy\")! And the acceptance criteria run `! grep -q 'WantedBy' host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service` — the acceptance criteria also fail! I have to remove the word WantedBy from the unit file's comments. Rewrite the last comment without using that word: \"never at boot, no [Install] section\"... wait, does \"[Install]\" appear in a comment too? Acceptance only greps for WantedBy. But my test also asserts NotIn(\"[Install]\") — my comment says \"Deliberately NO [Install] section\". That's fine for acceptance, but my test fails on it. To keep things clean, remove mentions of both literals from the comments: phrase it as \"Deliberately no install section: ...\".\n\nAlso check the third failure — the output was truncated (\"failures=3\"). Let's re-run after the fix to see the remaining failure. Probably test_cluster_up_reaps_before_it_gates: \"zero residue\"? It's there. Or test_fn_env_cache_dirs_are_not_tmpfs: the regex `CACHE_DIR=[^ ]*/tmp\\b` — my line has `CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor` — no /tmp. Hmm, but the comment says \"The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs)\" — the regex `CACHE_DIR=[^ ]*/tmp\\b` — the comment has \"under /tmp (tmpfs)\", but the line before it has no \"CACHE_DIR=\"… Actually the section header comment \"compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs\" has no \"CACHE_DIR=\". OK.\n\nLet's just re-run after the fix to see all the failures.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Two issues found: my unit-file comment mentions `WantedBy` (which breaks the acceptance `! grep`), and a test assertion mismatch. Let me see the third failure too:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_894493af49a04a5d905df46c","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling && python3 -m unittest tests.test_host_tooling 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(FAIL|ERROR):\" "}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":3831,"output":725,"cacheRead":92160,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":599,"totalTokens":96716,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787989656393,"responseId":"chatcmpl-60634d64-4f56-49fb-bb50-9cdc3f62a2e3","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_894493af49a04a5d905df46c","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAIL: test_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional (tests.test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional)\nFAIL: test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded (tests.test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded)\nFAIL: test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free (tests.test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787989674754},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"three failures:\n\n1. `test_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional`: my regex `^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\\s*$` is using re.search without re.M! assertRegex without flags — `^` and `$` are matching against the entire string. Need the re.M flag. Fix the test to use re.search with re.M.\n\n2. rails test: 169.254 escaping issue — either fix the assertion to `assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\", self.env)` (Python literal \"169\\\\.254\" → text `169\\.254`), or just use assertRegex(r\"169\\\\.254\"). The env file contains `^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)`. assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\\\\.\", ...). Easier way: assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\", self.env).\n\n3. unit file: remove references to the strings \"WantedBy\" and \"[Install]\" from the comments (the acceptance test greps for WantedBy. My own test asserts the absence of both).\n\nFix all three.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Three fixes: unit file comments must not contain the literal `WantedBy`/`[Install]`, and two test assertions need correction (multiline regex, escaped literal):\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ae9738c12bc846d9b3d417e5","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"# Deliberately no install section: the model takes tens of minutes to load\n# and claims both GPUs, so the stack comes up only on an explicit start by\n# the operator or the overnight overseer — never at boot.\n","oldText":"# Deliberately NO [Install] section: the model takes tens of minutes to load\n# and claims both GPUs, so the stack comes up only on an explicit start by\n# the operator or the overnight overseer — never at boot, never via WantedBy.\n"}],"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_5662260740634c3d8c4183bf","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"        # A rail without a routable peer IP must be excluded (link-local\n        # 169.254/16 filtered), and the choice must be logged.\n        self.assertIn(\"169\\\\.254\", self.env)","oldText":"        # A rail without a routable peer IP must be excluded, and the choice\n        # must be logged.\n        self.assertIn(\"169.254\", self.env)"},{"newText":"        self.assertRegex(self.env,\n                         re.compile(r\"^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\\s*$\", re.M),\n                         \"NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 must be an unconditional export\")","oldText":"        self.assertRegex(self.env, r\"^\\s*export NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\\s*$\",\n                         \"NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 must be an unconditional export\")"}],"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/57f87640bc5b/host-tooling/tests/test_host_tooling.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","p